var tafsirData_3 = {
  "1": "'Alif-Lãm-Mĩm.' (Quran 3:1). Surah Ali 'Imran opens with three Arabic letters read out one by one: alif, lam, mim. They are not a word and they do not form a sentence. Twenty-nine surahs of the Quran begin like this, and these detached letters are called 'al-huruf al-muqatta'at'.\n\nAl-Tabari, al-Qurtubi and Ibn Kathir all record several explanations from the early scholars, and none of them claim certainty. The most widely accepted reading is that these letters are a quiet challenge. The Quran is built from the same alphabet the Arabs used every day for poetry, speeches and business letters. Nothing was hidden from them. Yet when Allah invited them to match even a small part of it, they could not: 'And if you are in doubt about what We have revealed to Our servant, then produce a surah like it and call your helpers other than Allah, if what you say is true' (Quran 2:23). The challenge was even lowered to ten chapters, and still no one met it (Quran 11:13).\n\nSo the letters make a point before the argument begins. The raw material is ordinary and available to everyone. The result is not.\n\nNotice what follows immediately: 'Allah! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him' (Quran 3:2). The scattered letters give way at once to the clearest statement in the religion. Confusion is not the aim; awe is.\n\nThere is also an honest lesson in admitting we do not know. Some of the early scholars simply said, 'Allah knows best what He intends by these.' A believer can hold something firmly without being able to explain every part of it. Faith does not require that you master every detail before you accept the whole.",
  "2": "'Allah! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him —the Ever-Living, All-Sustaining.' (Quran 3:2). This is the same wording that opens Ayat al-Kursi (Quran 2:255), the greatest verse in the Quran. Right after the mysterious letters, the surah states the one thing that is never in doubt.\n\n'La ilaha illa Huwa' — there is no god except Him. The sentence has two halves. First it clears the ground: nothing else deserves worship, not idols, not saints, not rulers, not money, not our own desires. Then it builds: except Him. Denial first, then affirmation. Islam removes false attachments before it plants the true one.\n\nTwo of Allah's names are then given, and they were chosen carefully. 'Al-Hayy' means the Ever-Living. His life had no beginning and will have no end, unlike ours, which is borrowed and short. 'Al-Qayyum' means the One who stands by Himself and holds up everything else. He needs nothing; everything needs Him. The same pair appears again when the Day of Judgment is described: 'And all faces will be humbled before the Ever-Living, All-Sustaining' (Quran 20:111).\n\nThese two names matter especially here, because Surah Ali 'Imran was largely revealed in conversation with Christians of Najran who honoured 'Isa (Jesus), peace be upon him, as divine. A being who was born, who ate, who slept and who was helpless as an infant is not al-Hayy al-Qayyum. The verse settles the question before the discussion starts.\n\nPractically, this is where anxiety loses its grip. The One who holds up the universe without effort is holding up your affairs too. You are not the one keeping your life running.",
  "3": "'He has revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺ the Book in truth, confirming what came before it, as He revealed the Torah and the Gospel' (Quran 3:3). This verse states how Muslims are to view earlier scripture, and it is the foundation of everything Surah Ali 'Imran says to the People of the Book.\n\n'In truth' — bi al-haqq — means the Quran came with truth in its content and by a real, verified process. It is not inspiration in the vague sense of a good idea. It was sent down.\n\n'Confirming what came before it' is the key phrase. The Quran does not present itself as a brand-new religion competing with older ones. It presents itself as the latest message from the same Sender. The Torah given to Musa and the Injil (Gospel) given to 'Isa, peace be upon them, came from Allah too. A Muslim who denies that has denied part of his own faith.\n\nBut confirming is not the same as endorsing every text in circulation today. The Quran also describes itself as the judge over earlier scripture: 'We have revealed to you O Prophet this Book with the truth, as a confirmation of previous Scriptures and a supreme authority on them' (Quran 5:48). Where the older books still carry the original message, the Quran agrees. Where human hands changed things, the Quran corrects.\n\nThis gives the believer a settled, generous position. We honour Musa and 'Isa as Allah's messengers. We honour the revelations they brought. We do not treat the followers of those books as strangers to our story. And we take our final standard from the last Book, which alone has been preserved.",
  "4": "'previously, as a guide for people, and ˹also˺ revealed the Standard ˹to distinguish between right and wrong˺. Surely those who reject Allah's revelations will suffer a severe torment. For Allah is Almighty, capable of punishment.' (Quran 3:4). The verse completes the thought begun in 3:3 and then adds a warning.\n\nThe Torah and the Gospel were sent 'as a guide for people' — hudan li al-nas. Note the width of that: for people, not only for one nation. Every genuine revelation was mercy for whoever received it in its time.\n\n'The Standard' translates 'al-Furqan', from a root meaning to separate. It is the criterion that splits truth from falsehood, right from wrong, so that a person is no longer guessing. The Quran carries this title itself: 'Blessed is the One Who sent down the Standard to His servant, so that he may be a warner to the whole world' (Quran 25:1). The same word is used for what Musa was given: 'Indeed, We granted Moses and Aaron the standard ˹to distinguish between right and wrong˺—a light and a reminder for the righteous' (Quran 21:48). Revelation's job is to end confusion.\n\nThen the tone sharpens. Rejecting Allah's signs after they have been made clear is not a small mistake. The person has been handed the criterion and pushed it away.\n\nThe verse ends with two names held together: 'Almighty, capable of punishment' — 'Azizun dhu intiqam. Allah is not a distant judge with rules He cannot enforce. He has the power to act. Mercy is offered freely throughout this surah, but it is offered by One who is fully able to do otherwise, and that is what makes mercy meaningful.",
  "5": "'Surely nothing on earth or in the heavens is hidden from Allah.' (Quran 3:5). One short sentence, and it changes how a person lives.\n\nThe Arabic word for hidden here, 'yakhfa', covers anything concealed, whether it is buried, disguised, or simply too small to notice. Nothing in either realm falls outside Allah's knowledge. The same statement appears in the prayer of Ibrahim: 'Our Lord! You certainly know what we conceal and what we reveal. Nothing on earth or in heaven is hidden from Allah' (Quran 14:38).\n\nWhy is this said here, in a surah about a religious dispute? Because arguments about faith often involve hidden motives. Some people who debated the Prophet, peace be upon him, knew the truth privately and resisted it publicly for the sake of status or income. Others hid doubts they were ashamed of. Allah is telling both groups that the inner layer is fully visible: 'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Whether you conceal what is in your hearts or reveal it, it is known to Allah\"' (Quran 3:29). Even a glance is recorded: 'Allah ˹even˺ knows the sly glances of the eyes and whatever the hearts conceal' (Quran 40:19).\n\nThis cuts two ways, and both are useful. If you are tempted to sin quietly, there is no such thing as quietly. If you are doing good that nobody notices, or carrying a grief you cannot explain to anyone, that is not lost either. It is seen by the only One whose seeing finally matters.\n\nThe practical effect is a single, honest life. A believer who really absorbs this verse stops managing two versions of himself, the public one and the private one, because he knows only one of them was ever real.",
  "6": "'He is the One Who shapes you in the wombs of your mothers as He wills. There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him—the Almighty, All-Wise.' (Quran 3:6). Having said that nothing is hidden from Him (Quran 3:5), the surah gives an example nobody can dispute.\n\nBefore ultrasound, the womb was the clearest picture of something completely hidden from human eyes and completely known to Allah. Inside that darkness He forms every feature: 'He creates you in the wombs of your mothers ˹in stages˺, one development after another, in three layers of darkness' (Quran 39:6). The stages are listed elsewhere: 'then developed the drop into a clinging clot ˹of blood˺, then developed the clot into a lump ˹of flesh˺, then developed the lump into bones, then clothed the bones with flesh, then We brought it into being as a new creation' (Quran 23:14).\n\nThe Prophet, peace be upon him, described the same process in time. Ibn Mas'ud reported that he said, \"(The matter of the Creation of) a human being is put together in the womb of the mother in forty days, and then he becomes a clot of thick blood for a similar period, and then a piece of flesh for a similar period. Then Allah sends an angel who is ordered to write four things. He is ordered to write down his deeds, his livelihood, his (date of) death, and whether he will be blessed or wretched\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 3208).\n\n'As He wills' is important. Nobody chooses their own face, height, family, era or natural gifts. That is a hard truth for pride and a comforting one for envy.\n\nAnd there is a direct answer to the Najran delegation here. 'Isa, peace be upon him, was shaped in a womb like everyone else. Whatever was miraculous about his birth, he was formed by the One who forms all of us.",
  "7": "'He is the One Who has revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺ the Book, of which some verses are precise—they are the foundation of the Book—while others are elusive.' (Quran 3:7). This verse explains how the Quran is built and warns about a specific way of misusing it.\n\n'Muhkamat' are the precise verses: clear in meaning, hard to twist. They carry the core beliefs and the practical commands, and the verse calls them 'the foundation of the Book' — umm al-kitab, literally its mother. 'Mutashabihat' are the elusive verses: those whose full meaning is not settled by the wording alone. Details of the unseen fall here, and so do the disconnected letters like the one that opened this surah.\n\nThe problem is not that such verses exist. It is what some people do with them: 'Those with deviant hearts follow the elusive verses seeking ˹to spread˺ doubt through their ˹false˺ interpretations' (Quran 3:7). Notice the order. The heart deviates first; the misreading follows. A person who wants a conclusion can always find an ambiguous line to hang it on, while ignoring the twenty clear ones that contradict him.\n\n'Aisha reported that after the Prophet, peace be upon him, recited this verse he said, \"If you see those who follow thereof that is not entirely clear, then they are those whom Allah has named as having deviation from the Truth, so beware of them\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 4547).\n\nThe sound approach is the one the verse itself gives: 'As for those well-grounded in knowledge, they say, \"We believe in this ˹Quran˺—it is all from our Lord\"' (Quran 3:7). Read the unclear in the light of the clear, and accept that some things remain with Allah.",
  "8": "'˹They say,˺ \"Our Lord! Do not let our hearts deviate after you have guided us. Grant us Your mercy. You are indeed the Giver ˹of all bounties˺.\"' (Quran 3:8). This is the prayer of the people just described as 'well-grounded in knowledge' (Quran 3:7), and what they ask for is revealing.\n\nThey do not ask for more knowledge. They ask to keep what they already have. The Arabic 'zaygh' means a slight tilt away from straight — not a dramatic collapse of faith but a small drift. That is how guidance is usually lost: gradually, and often by people who once knew better.\n\nAnas reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, would often say, \"Ya Muqallibal-qulub, thabbit qalbi 'ala dinik (O Changer of the Hearts! Strengthen my heart upon Your Religion).\" Anas said, \"O Prophet of Allah! We believe in you and what you have come with, but do you fear for us?\" He said, \"Yes. Indeed the hearts are between two Fingers of Allah's Fingers, He changes them as He wills\" (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 2140, graded hasan).\n\nIf the Prophet himself prayed that way, nobody is beyond needing it.\n\n'After you have guided us' shows they understand where guidance came from in the first place. It was a gift, not an achievement, so it must be asked for again. The Quran warns that persistent turning away can be met with being left to turn away: 'So when they ˹persistently˺ deviated, Allah caused their hearts to deviate' (Quran 61:5).\n\nThen they ask for mercy, and close with 'You are indeed the Giver' — al-Wahhab, the One who gives freely without being owed anything. They are not claiming a right. They are asking a Generous One for a gift.",
  "9": "'Our Lord! You will certainly gather all humanity for the ˹promised˺ Day—about which there is no doubt. Surely Allah does not break His promise.' (Quran 3:9). The prayer that began in 3:8 continues, and its second half moves from this life to the next.\n\nHaving asked Allah to keep their hearts steady, these believers now remind themselves why steadiness matters. There is a gathering coming. Everyone who has ever lived will be there, from every century and every corner of the earth, with nobody excused and nobody overlooked.\n\n'About which there is no doubt' — la rayba fihi — is the same phrase used of the Quran itself at the start of Surah al-Baqarah: 'This is the Book! There is no doubt about it' (Quran 2:2). The Book is certain and so is the Day it describes.\n\n'Surely Allah does not break His promise' is the ground the whole thing rests on. Human promises fail for two reasons: either the person becomes unable to deliver, or he changes his mind. Neither applies to Allah, who is 'the Ever-Living, All-Sustaining' (Quran 3:2). The believers repeat this confidence near the end of the surah: 'Our Lord! Grant us what You have promised us through Your messengers and do not put us to shame on Judgment Day—for certainly You never fail in Your promise' (Quran 3:194).\n\nThere is quiet comfort in this for anyone watching injustice go unanswered. Accounts that never got settled here have not been dropped. They have been scheduled.\n\nNotice too that these thoughtful believers pray about the Hereafter rather than argue about it. Knowledge that stays in the head produces debate; knowledge that reaches the heart produces prayer.",
  "10": "'Indeed, neither the wealth nor children of the disbelievers will be of any benefit to them against Allah—and they will be the fuel for the Fire.' (Quran 3:10). After the mention of the Day of gathering (Quran 3:9), the surah names the two things people most rely on instead.\n\nWealth and children were the two great sources of security in Arabian society, and honestly they still are: money and family, savings and successors. Both are real blessings. The verse does not say they are worthless. It says they will not help 'against Allah' — they cannot buy an exemption or supply a defence when the case is being heard.\n\nThe same warning appears in Ibrahim's account of the Day: 'the Day when neither wealth nor children will be of any benefit. Only those who come before Allah with a pure heart ˹will be saved˺' (Quran 26:88-89). Of Abu Lahab, who had plenty of both, Allah says, 'Neither his wealth nor ˹worldly˺ gains will benefit him' (Quran 111:2).\n\n'Fuel for the Fire' is a heavy image. The very people who thought their resources made them substantial become the material that feeds the punishment.\n\nThere is also a warning against measuring people by their assets. It is easy to look at someone doing well while ignoring Allah and quietly conclude that he must be doing something right. The Quran corrects that reading directly: 'So let neither their wealth nor children impress you ˹O Prophet˺. Allah only intends to torment them through these things in this worldly life' (Quran 9:55).\n\nFor the believer the lesson is not to despise wealth or family but to stop treating them as insurance. Use them, be grateful for them, spend them in good — and know they are tools, not shelter.",
  "11": "'Their fate will be like that of the people of Pharaoh and those before them —they all rejected Our signs, so Allah seized them for their sins. And Allah is severe in punishment.' (Quran 3:11). Having said that wealth and children give no protection (Quran 3:10), the verse offers proof from history.\n\nPharaoh is the obvious example. No one in that world had more: an empire, an army, wealth, monuments and total control. He was so confident he declared himself a lord. None of it delayed the outcome by a single day. The identical sentence appears in Surah al-Anfal: 'Their fate is that of the people of Pharaoh and those before them—they all disbelieved in Allah's signs, so Allah seized them for their sins' (Quran 8:52).\n\n'And those before them' widens the lens. Nuh's people, 'Ad, Thamud — the pattern repeats. This is not one unlucky king; it is how the story goes.\n\nThe word translated 'seized' is 'akhadha', to take hold of firmly and suddenly. It suggests a grip that comes without warning, when the person still assumed he had time.\n\nThe stated cause is precise: 'they rejected Our signs'. Not ignorance — rejection. Pharaoh saw the signs Musa brought and pushed them away because accepting them meant losing his position. That is the sin being punished, and it is available to anyone in any century who recognises the truth and calculates that it costs too much.\n\nThe verse was a message to the Prophet's opponents in Makkah, who had money and standing and thought that settled things. It is also a message to us. Reading these accounts as ancient history misses the point. They are printed as warnings, not as records.",
  "12": "'˹O Prophet!˺ Tell the disbelievers, \"Soon you will be overpowered and driven to Hell—what an evil place to rest!\"' (Quran 3:12). This is a prediction, and the timing of it is remarkable.\n\nMost commentators, including Ibn Kathir commenting on this verse, connect it to the Jewish tribes of Madinah after the Battle of Badr. Badr had just shown that a small, poorly equipped group of believers could defeat a much larger force. The Prophet, peace be upon him, warned those who still opposed him to take the lesson. They dismissed it, saying Badr was a fluke against people who knew nothing of war.\n\nThe verse answers that dismissal with a flat promise: 'Soon you will be overpowered'. The Arabic 'sa-tughlabuna' is passive — you will be defeated. It does not say who will defeat them, because the outcome does not depend on the strength of the other side. It was fulfilled within a few years.\n\nThe same words appear about the Makkan opposition: 'They will continue to spend to the point of regret. Then they will be defeated and the disbelievers will be driven into Hell' (Quran 8:36). And more briefly still: '˹Soon˺ their united front will be defeated and ˹forced to˺ flee' (Quran 54:45).\n\nBut notice the two-stage structure. Defeat in this world, then Hell in the next. Worldly loss is the smaller half, and it is mentioned first as a signal of the larger one.\n\n'What an evil place to rest' uses 'al-mihad', a word for a bed or a place prepared for someone. There is bitter irony in it: they were building a comfortable position for themselves, and this is the bed that was actually being made.",
  "13": "'Indeed, there was a sign for you in the two armies that met in battle— one fighting for the cause of Allah and the other in denial. The believers saw their enemy twice their number. But Allah supports with His victory whoever He wills. Surely in this is a lesson for people of insight.' (Quran 3:13). This is Badr, and the verse calls it a 'sign' — an ayah, the same word used for a verse of the Quran. The battle was meant to be read.\n\nThe numbers were roughly three hundred believers against about a thousand of Quraysh, better armed and better supplied. Allah reminds them of it plainly later in the surah: 'Indeed, Allah made you victorious at Badr when you were ˹vastly˺ outnumbered' (Quran 3:123).\n\n'The believers saw their enemy twice their number' has puzzled readers, since the real ratio was larger. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, explains that Allah made the enemy appear smaller than they were so the believers would not lose heart, and this matches Surah al-Anfal: 'Then when your armies met, Allah made them appear as few in your eyes, and made you appear as few in theirs, so Allah may establish what He had destined' (Quran 8:44). Both sides were given a false reading of the odds, and both readings served the same outcome.\n\nThe believers were also helped in ways they could not see: '˹Remember˺ when you cried out to your Lord for help, He answered, \"I will reinforce you with a thousand angels\"' (Quran 8:9), and 'He caused drowsiness to overcome you, giving you serenity' (Quran 8:11).\n\nThe closing phrase names the audience: 'people of insight' — ulu al-absar, those who look past the surface. Anyone could count the soldiers at Badr. Only some could see who was actually deciding the outcome.",
  "14": "'The enjoyment of ˹worldly˺ desires—women, children, treasures of gold and silver, fine horses, cattle, and fertile land—has been made appealing to people. These are the pleasures of this worldly life, but with Allah is the finest destination.' (Quran 3:14). This is one of the most honest verses in the Quran about human wanting.\n\nNotice first that the attraction is not condemned. 'Has been made appealing' — zuyyina — is passive; the pull toward these things was placed in us. A spouse, children, money, transport, livestock and land are the ordinary shape of a good life, and the Quran does not ask anyone to pretend otherwise. Every item on the list is lawful.\n\nThe warning is about proportion, not about the things themselves. They are named 'the pleasures of this worldly life' — mata' al-hayat al-dunya. 'Mata'' means goods you take for a journey: useful, temporary, not the destination. Elsewhere: 'Wealth and children are the adornment of this worldly life, but the everlasting good deeds are far better with your Lord in reward and in hope' (Quran 18:46), and 'Your wealth and children are only a test' (Quran 64:15).\n\nThe Prophet, peace be upon him, spoke about the same pull. Abu Sa'id al-Khudri reported that he said, \"The world is sweet and green (alluring) and verily Allah is going to install you as vicegerent in it in order to see how you act\" (Sahih Muslim 2742). Usama ibn Zayd reported that he said, \"After me I have not left any trial more severe to men than women\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 5096) — a statement about the strength of desire, not a criticism of women.\n\n'With Allah is the finest destination' ends the verse by comparison rather than prohibition. Nothing here is banned. It is simply outranked.",
  "15": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Shall I inform you of what is better than ˹all of˺ this? Those mindful ˹of Allah˺ will have Gardens with their Lord under which rivers flow, to stay there forever, and pure spouses, along with Allah's pleasure.\" And Allah is All-Seeing of ˹His˺ servants,' (Quran 3:15). The previous verse listed what people love. This one offers a trade.\n\nThe question is asked, not commanded. 'Shall I inform you of what is better?' invites the listener to compare rather than simply obey. Allah appeals to the very desire He placed in us and points it somewhere it can be satisfied permanently.\n\nEach item answers something from 3:14. Gardens with flowing rivers answer the love of fertile land. 'Pure spouses' answer the love of companionship. 'To stay there forever' answers the one thing worldly pleasure can never fix — that it ends. Every good thing here is on a countdown, which is why even the best moments carry a trace of sadness.\n\nThen comes the item with no worldly equivalent: 'Allah's pleasure' — ridwan min Allah. This is placed last and deliberately outranks the rest. The same order appears in Surah al-Tawbah, which lists the gardens and homes and then says, 'and—above all— the pleasure of Allah. That is ˹truly˺ the ultimate triumph' (Quran 9:72). Paradise is not merely a reward collected from a distance; it comes with the approval of the One who gives it.\n\n'And Allah is All-Seeing of His servants' closes the verse and quietly does two things. It reassures those who restrain themselves in private that the restraint is noticed. And it reminds anyone claiming taqwa that the claim is being checked.",
  "16": "'who pray, \"Our Lord! We have believed, so forgive our sins and protect us from the torment of the Fire.\"' (Quran 3:16). This begins a description of the God-conscious people promised Paradise in 3:15, and the first thing mentioned about them is not an achievement but a request.\n\nThey open with 'We have believed' — innana amanna. It is the only thing they put forward. They do not list prayers, charity or years of service. They mention faith and then immediately ask for forgiveness, as if faith itself were the reason to expect mercy rather than a reason to feel entitled.\n\n'Forgive our sins' — ighfir lana dhunubana. The Arabic 'ghafara' means to cover over, the way a helmet covers a head. They admit the sins exist. This is what marks out real taqwa: not people who have stopped sinning, but people who have stopped defending their sins. The surah returns to this later: '˹They are˺ those who, upon committing an evil deed or wronging themselves, remember Allah and seek forgiveness and do not knowingly persist in sin' (Quran 3:135).\n\nThen, 'protect us from the torment of the Fire'. They ask for two distinct things — forgiveness for the past, protection for the future — because being forgiven once does not settle what comes tomorrow.\n\nThere is a striking humility here. These are the people Allah has just promised gardens and His pleasure, and their own prayer is simply not to burn. The better a person knows Allah, the less they assume about themselves. Confidence in Allah's mercy and fear of one's own record are meant to sit together in the same heart.",
  "17": "'˹It is they˺ who are patient, sincere, obedient, and charitable, and who pray for forgiveness before dawn.' (Quran 3:17). Five qualities, given in a deliberate order, describing the people whose prayer was quoted in 3:16.\n\n'Patient' — al-sabirin. Sabr covers three things at once: holding steady under hardship, holding back from what is forbidden, and staying consistent in obedience when it becomes dull. It is listed first because none of the others last without it.\n\n'Sincere' — al-sadiqin, the truthful. This is truthfulness in speech and also in claim: a person whose outside matches his inside, who is not performing religion.\n\n'Obedient' — al-qanitin. Qunut is not grudging compliance but a settled, humble standing before Allah, the attitude of someone who has genuinely accepted the arrangement.\n\n'Charitable' — al-munfiqin, those who spend. Faith that never reaches the wallet has not travelled far.\n\nThen the last one, and it is the most private: 'who pray for forgiveness before dawn' — al-mustaghfirina bi al-ashar. The final stretch of night, when nobody is watching and sleep is at its heaviest. The same detail describes the righteous in Surah al-Dhariyat: 'they used to sleep only little in the night, and pray for forgiveness before dawn' (Quran 51:17-18).\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said, \"Our Lord, the Blessed, the Superior, comes every night down on the nearest Heaven to us when the last third of the night remains, saying: 'Is there anyone to invoke Me, so that I may respond to invocation? Is there anyone to ask Me, so that I may grant him his request? Is there anyone seeking My forgiveness, so that I may forgive him?'\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 1145).\n\nFour public qualities, then one that only Allah sees. That last one is where the others are proved.",
  "18": "'Allah ˹Himself˺ is a Witness that there is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him—and so are the angels and people of knowledge. He is the Maintainer of justice. There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him—the Almighty, All-Wise.' (Quran 3:18). This is the greatest statement of testimony in the Quran, and it is arranged as a formal case.\n\nAllah testifies first, about Himself. Nobody else was present at creation, and nobody else can report on His nature. Then the angels testify — beings who see the reality directly. Then 'people of knowledge' — ulu al-'ilm. Ordinary human beings are placed in the same sentence as the angels, and scholars across the centuries have noted the honour in that. What earns it is not degrees but arriving at this conclusion through evidence and living by it.\n\nThe core statement is repeated twice in one verse, which is unusual. Between the two repetitions sits a description: 'He is the Maintainer of justice' — qa'iman bi al-qist, the One who upholds fairness. Placing justice between two declarations of oneness says something. The God who alone deserves worship is not arbitrary. Authority and fairness belong to the same Being.\n\nThe verse then closes with 'the Almighty, All-Wise' — 'Aziz, Hakim. Power without wisdom is tyranny; wisdom without power is helpless advice. Allah has both, so what He decides can be trusted and can be carried out.\n\nFor a believer, this reframes the shahadah. When you say there is no god but Allah, you are not merely stating an opinion. You are joining a testimony already given by Allah Himself, seconded by the angels, and confirmed by everyone who studied the matter honestly.",
  "19": "'Certainly, Allah's only Way is Islam. Those who were given the Scripture did not dispute ˹among themselves˺ out of mutual envy until knowledge came to them. Whoever denies Allah's signs, then surely Allah is swift in reckoning.' (Quran 3:19). This follows directly from the testimony in 3:18, and it draws the conclusion.\n\n'Al-din 'inda Allah al-Islam' — the religion with Allah is Islam. The word 'islam' means submitting yourself to Allah. Understood that way, it is the religion of every prophet, not a new movement begun in the seventh century. Ibrahim said as much to his sons: 'Indeed, Allah has chosen for you this faith; so do not die except in ˹a state of full˺ submission' (Quran 2:132). Of the Torah Allah says it was used to judge 'by the prophets, who submitted themselves to Allah' (Quran 5:44). And the whole line is named together: 'He has ordained for you ˹believers˺ the Way which He decreed for Noah, and what We have revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺ and what We decreed for Abraham, Moses, and Jesus' (Quran 42:13). The surah restates the point plainly later: 'Whoever seeks a way other than Islam, it will never be accepted from them' (Quran 3:85).\n\nThe second sentence diagnoses religious division, and the diagnosis is uncomfortable. Earlier communities split 'out of mutual envy' — baghyan baynahum — and, crucially, only 'until knowledge came to them'. They did not divide from ignorance. They divided after the truth arrived, because accepting it meant conceding to a rival.\n\nThat is worth sitting with. Most stubborn religious disagreement is not really about evidence. It is about not wanting the other person to be right.",
  "20": "'So if they argue with you ˹O Prophet˺, say, \"I have submitted myself to Allah, and so have my followers.\" And ask those who were given the Scripture and the illiterate ˹people˺, \"Have you submitted yourselves ˹to Allah˺?\" If they submit, they will be ˹rightly˺ guided. But if they turn away, then your duty is only to deliver ˹the message˺. And Allah is All-Seeing of ˹His˺ servants.' (Quran 3:20). This tells the Prophet how to end a debate that has stopped being useful.\n\nThe instruction is not to produce a better argument. It is to state a position: 'I have submitted myself to Allah'. When someone is arguing to win rather than to learn, more evidence changes nothing. Clarity does more than cleverness.\n\nThen a question is put back to them — 'Have you submitted yourselves?' — addressed to two groups: 'those who were given the Scripture', meaning Jews and Christians, and 'the illiterate ones', al-ummiyyin, meaning the Arabs who had received no scripture. Both are asked the same thing, because the demand is identical for everyone regardless of religious background.\n\n'If they submit, they will be rightly guided' — the benefit returns to them, not to the Prophet. 'But if they turn away, then your duty is only to deliver the message.' This is one of the great reliefs of the Quran. The messenger is responsible for delivery, not for results: 'The Messenger's duty is only to deliver ˹the message˺ clearly' (Quran 24:54).\n\nAnyone who has tried to guide a family member knows how heavy the failure feels. This verse lifts that weight. Speak the truth kindly, then let go. Hearts are not in your hands.",
  "21": "'Indeed, those who deny Allah's signs, kill the prophets unjustly, and kill people who stand up for justice—give them good news of a painful punishment.' (Quran 3:21). The verse names three crimes and links them, and the third is the one most often overlooked.\n\nThe progression is logical. First a person rejects Allah's signs. Then he moves against the messengers who bring them. Then he moves against anyone at all who calls for justice, prophet or not. Once truth is inconvenient, the people who speak it become the problem, whoever they are.\n\nKilling prophets is mentioned repeatedly in the Quran as a historical fact: 'they invited the displeasure of Allah for rejecting Allah's signs and unjustly killing the prophets' (Quran 2:61), and 'Whenever a messenger came to them with what they did not desire, they denied some and killed others' (Quran 5:70).\n\nThe word 'unjustly' — bi ghayri haqq — is added even though killing a prophet could never be just. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, notes that it underlines how these killings happened with no shadow of a legitimate reason, often dressed up in religious language.\n\nThe third crime removes any excuse based on prophethood being over. 'People who stand up for justice' are ordinary human beings — the one who reports the fraud, refuses the bribe, or speaks against a powerful wrong. Silencing them is placed in the same sentence as killing prophets.\n\n'Give them good news of a painful punishment' uses the word for glad tidings, bashshir, with bitter irony. They expected to be congratulated for defending the established order. This is the announcement they actually receive.",
  "22": "'They are the ones whose deeds are wasted in this world and the Hereafter. And they will have no helpers.' (Quran 3:22). This is the sentence passed on the people described in 3:21, and it is worse than it first sounds.\n\n'Habitat a'maluhum' — their deeds have come to nothing. The Arabic verb 'habita' was used of an animal that eats a poisonous plant and swells up: it looks fuller and healthier than ever, right before it dies. That image is exact. The deeds are not simply absent. They were done, they looked impressive, and they turn out to be empty.\n\nThese people were not idle. They had charities, alliances, public works and reputations. The problem is what those deeds were built on. A structure with no foundation of faith and justice cannot stand, however tall it is: 'Know that this worldly life is no more than play, amusement, luxury, mutual boasting, and competition in wealth and children... then they are reduced to chaff' (Quran 57:20).\n\n'In this world and the Hereafter' covers both accounts. Even here, what they built does not deliver the security they wanted. There, it counts for nothing at all.\n\n'They will have no helpers' — min nasirin. In their own world, help was the one thing they always had: family, tribe, wealth, position. Every one of those channels is closed at once. The people they protected cannot protect them, and the wealth they gathered cannot be spent.\n\nThe lesson is not that good deeds are pointless. It is that deeds are weighed together with the heart behind them and the truth they were offered and refused. Effort alone is not the currency.",
  "23": "'Have you not seen those who were given a portion of the Scriptures? Yet when they are invited to the Book of Allah to settle their disputes, some of them turn away heedlessly.' (Quran 3:23). The surah shifts from history to a scene the first listeners had watched with their own eyes.\n\n'A portion of the Scriptures' — nasiban min al-kitab — is carefully worded. They had a share of revelation, not all of it, and holding a share is not the same as living by it.\n\nThe situation described is specific. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records that a dispute was brought to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and that when the ruling of the Torah went against what the parties wanted, they refused it. The invitation was to 'the Book of Allah' — their own book, the one they claimed to defend. That is what makes the refusal so telling.\n\n'Turn away heedlessly' — wa hum mu'ridun — describes a deliberate turning of the back, not an oversight.\n\nThis is the difference between owning scripture and submitting to it. It is easy to quote a text while it supports you and quietly look elsewhere when it does not. The test of a person's relationship with revelation is not how often he cites it, but what he does the first time it costs him something.\n\nMuslims have no exemption here. Anyone can consult the Quran and the Sunnah for a ruling and then go looking for another opinion when the answer is unwelcome. The verse is not describing a foreign failing; it is describing a human one, recorded so that we recognise it when it is our turn.",
  "24": "'This is because they say, \"The Fire will not touch us except for a few days.\" They have been deceived in their faith by their wishful lying.' (Quran 3:24). The previous verse described people turning away from Allah's ruling. This one explains why they felt safe enough to do it.\n\nThey held a belief that made consequences manageable: a short stay in the Fire, then out. The same claim is recorded in Surah al-Baqarah, along with Allah's reply: '˹Some of˺ the Jews claim, \"The Fire will not touch us except for a number of days.\" Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Have you taken a pledge from Allah— for Allah never breaks His word—or are you ˹just˺ saying about Allah what you do not know?\"' (Quran 2:80). The answer is a demand for evidence, exactly as in 'Show ˹me˺ your proof if what you say is true' (Quran 2:111).\n\n'They have been deceived in their faith by their wishful lying' — ma kanu yaftarun, what they used to invent. Nobody deceived them. They built the comfortable belief themselves and then trusted it.\n\nThis is the most dangerous kind of religious idea: one with no proof behind it that happens to be exactly what we want to hear. Assuming that belonging to the right group is enough, that a lineage covers for us, that Allah will overlook our particular sin because of who we are — these are the same mechanism in different clothes.\n\nThe cure is the one the Quran offers: ask for the evidence. Any claim about Allah's judgment either comes from revelation or from imagination. Hope built on the first is faith. Hope built on the second is just a wish that we have promoted to a doctrine.",
  "25": "'But how ˹horrible˺ will it be when We gather them together on the Day about which there is no doubt—when every soul will be paid in full for what it has done, and none will be wronged!' (Quran 3:25). The comfortable theory of 3:24 meets reality.\n\nThe sentence begins with 'fa-kayfa' — so how will it be? It is left unfinished, which is more effective than any description. How will they feel? How will the argument sound then? The listener is left to complete it.\n\n'Gather them together' — the word 'jama'na' means to collect from everywhere into one place. There is no possibility of being missed in the crowd, and no crowd to hide in.\n\n'Every soul will be paid in full for what it has done' uses the language of settling a debt: wuffiyat, paid in full, nothing withheld. This cuts both ways. Whatever good was done and never acknowledged is paid. Whatever harm was done and never traced is charged. The same principle is stated elsewhere: 'Today every soul will be rewarded for what it has done. No injustice Today! Surely Allah is swift in reckoning' (Quran 40:17), and 'For Allah created the heavens and the earth for a purpose, so that every soul may be paid back for what it has committed. And none will be wronged' (Quran 45:22).\n\nNotice the last clause: 'none will be wronged'. Not one person will be treated unfairly, and nobody will be punished for what they did not do.\n\nThat is the answer to wishful thinking. The Day is not a negotiation and not a lottery. It is an exact accounting, and exactness is frightening only to those who were relying on it being sloppy.",
  "26": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"O Allah! Lord over all authorities! You give authority to whoever You please and remove it from who You please; You honour whoever You please and disgrace who You please—all good is in Your Hands. Surely You ˹alone˺ are Most Capable of everything.\"' (Quran 3:26). After a warning about the Day of Reckoning, the surah teaches a prayer about power.\n\n'Malik al-mulk' means the Owner of all ownership. Kings own their kingdoms; Allah owns kingship itself. Every authority anyone holds is on loan from Him, and the loan is recalled.\n\nFour actions are listed in two pairs: giving authority and removing it, honouring and disgracing. The pairing matters. The same Hand does both, so a person who rises has no grounds for arrogance and a person who falls has no grounds for despair. Rise and fall come from one source.\n\n'All good is in Your Hands' — biyadika al-khayr. Notice that only good is attributed to His hands in this phrasing, even though the verse has just mentioned removal and disgrace. Classical commentators, including al-Qurtubi on this verse, point out that what looks like loss often carries good inside it, and that Allah's decree is good even when its taste is bitter.\n\nThis has a direct application to how Muslims read their own history. Communities rise and decline, and the question is never simply who has the stronger army. Honour is not a possession to be seized: 'all honour and power belongs to Allah' (Quran 35:10), and 'all honour and power belongs to Allah, His Messenger, and the believers, yet the hypocrites do not know' (Quran 63:8).\n\nFor an individual, this is the prayer to say when you want a position, or when you have just lost one.",
  "27": "'You cause the night to pass into the day and the day into the night. You bring forth the living from the dead and the dead from the living. And You provide for whoever You will without limit.' (Quran 3:27). The prayer begun in 3:26 continues, and now it points to evidence anyone can check.\n\nThe first sign is the changing length of day and night. The Arabic 'tulij' means to insert one thing into another gradually. Nobody sees it happen; each day is a few minutes different from the last, and over months the balance shifts completely. The same wording appears elsewhere: 'He merges the night into the day and the day into the night, and has subjected the sun and the moon, each orbiting for an appointed term' (Quran 35:13).\n\nThe second sign is life from death and death from life. A dry seed produces a green plant; a living body returns to dust. 'Indeed, Allah is the One Who causes seeds and fruit stones to sprout. He brings forth the living from the dead and the dead from the living' (Quran 6:95). Al-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes that the early scholars also read it morally: a believer can come from a disbelieving household and the reverse, so no one's origins decide their end.\n\nWhy put these signs inside a prayer about power? Because both are the same kind of act. The One who shifts the balance of night and day so quietly that nobody notices is the One who shifts authority between people. Change is not chaos; it is being managed.\n\nThe verse closes with provision 'without limit' — bi ghayri hisab, without reckoning. Allah does not portion out sustenance from a fixed supply that might run short. That is worth remembering when someone else's gain feels like your loss.",
  "28": "'Believers should not take disbelievers as guardians instead of the believers—and whoever does so will have nothing to hope for from Allah—unless it is a precaution against their tyranny. And Allah warns you about Himself. And to Allah is the final return.' (Quran 3:28). This verse is often misread, so the key word has to be understood first.\n\n'Awliya'' is the plural of 'wali'. It does not mean friend in the casual sense. A wali is a protector, patron and ally — the one whose side you are on and whose cause you serve. The verse forbids taking those hostile to Islam as your patrons 'instead of the believers', min duni al-mu'minin. The issue is loyalty, not politeness.\n\nThat reading is confirmed by the Quran itself, which explicitly permits kindness and fairness across religious lines: 'Allah does not forbid you from dealing kindly and fairly with those who have neither fought nor driven you out of your homes. Surely Allah loves those who are fair' (Quran 60:8). The prohibition is then defined precisely: 'Allah only forbids you from befriending those who have fought you for your faith, driven you out of your homes, or supported others in doing so' (Quran 60:9). So the ruling concerns alliance with active enemies, not ordinary dealings with neighbours, colleagues and relatives.\n\nThe context in Madinah was concrete. Some Muslims kept alliances with groups actively working against the community, treating them as their real security: 'Do they seek honour and power through that company?' (Quran 4:139).\n\nThe exception 'unless it is a precaution against their tyranny' is called taqiyyah. Someone under genuine threat may outwardly go along to protect his life, as in 'not those who are forced while their hearts are firm in faith' (Quran 16:106). It is a concession for danger, not a licence for convenience.",
  "29": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Whether you conceal what is in your hearts or reveal it, it is known to Allah. For He knows whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. And Allah is Most Capable of everything.\"' (Quran 3:29). Placed right after the warning about loyalty in 3:28, this verse tells us why that warning cannot be dodged.\n\nA person can manage his outward behaviour perfectly. He can attend the prayers, use the right words, and keep his real allegiance quietly to himself. This verse closes that door. Concealing and revealing are named together and given the same status: both are known.\n\nThe reasoning then widens. He knows what is in the hearts because He knows 'whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth'. Your inner state is simply one more item in a creation He has complete knowledge of. There is nothing special about the human chest that would make it a blind spot.\n\nThis theme has already appeared in the surah: 'Surely nothing on earth or in the heavens is hidden from Allah' (Quran 3:5). Even the smallest movements are included: 'Allah ˹even˺ knows the sly glances of the eyes and whatever the hearts conceal' (Quran 40:19).\n\n'And Allah is Most Capable of everything' is the necessary second half. Knowledge alone might be endured; knowledge joined to power cannot be. He knows and He can act.\n\nThe practical result is that religion becomes an inside job. Outward compliance is not worthless, but it is not the thing being measured. A believer who takes this verse seriously spends less energy on appearances and more on what is actually in his chest, because that is the part that is fully visible.",
  "30": "'˹Watch for˺ the Day when every soul will be presented with whatever good it has done. And it will wish that its misdeeds were far off. And Allah warns you about Himself. And Allah is Ever Gracious to ˹His˺ servants.' (Quran 3:30). Everything hidden in 3:29 is now brought out into the open.\n\nThe scene is a presentation. 'Muhdaran' means brought forward and set in front of you. Not a summary, not a report — the deeds themselves, produced. Al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, notes that both good and bad are presented, though the wording draws attention to the good first, as if to say: here is everything you managed, now weigh it against the rest.\n\n'It will wish that its misdeeds were far off' is one of the most human lines in the Quran. Nobody in that moment wishes they had been richer or more admired. The single wish is distance from what they did. It is the feeling of a private message read aloud to a room, multiplied beyond measure.\n\nThen the same phrase from 3:28 returns: 'And Allah warns you about Himself' — yuhadhdhirukum Allahu nafsah. The repetition is deliberate. Being warned by Allah about Allah is a strange mercy: the Judge is telling you in advance exactly what the court will consider.\n\nAnd the verse ends where you might least expect it: 'And Allah is Ever Gracious to His servants' — ra'ufun bi al-'ibad. Ra'fah is tenderness, the gentlest word for mercy in Arabic. The warning itself is the kindness. Nobody who arrives on that Day can say they were not told, and everybody who reads the verse still has time to act on it.",
  "31": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"If you ˹sincerely˺ love Allah, then follow me; Allah will love you and forgive your sins. For Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\"' (Quran 3:31). Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records that al-Hasan al-Basri and others called it the verse of testing, ayat al-imtihan, because it puts a claim to the proof.\n\nEveryone says they love God. The delegations who argued with the Prophet said it; so did the Makkans who worshipped idols while insisting they honoured Allah. This verse does not deny the feeling. It asks what the feeling produces.\n\nThe structure is a condition and a result: if you love Allah, follow the Messenger. Love in the Quran is not measured by emotion but by obedience. You cannot claim to love someone whose instructions you find optional. And notice the specific proof required — following the Prophet, peace be upon him. There is no route to Allah's love that bypasses His messenger: 'Indeed, in the Messenger of Allah you have an excellent example for whoever has hope in Allah and the Last Day' (Quran 33:21), and 'Whoever obeys the Messenger has truly obeyed Allah' (Quran 4:80).\n\nThen the response, and it is more than anyone asked for. You offer love; Allah returns love. 'Allah will love you' — yuhbibkumu Allah. The servant's love is the small thing; the Lord's love is the enormous one.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said that Allah said, \"And My slave keeps on coming closer to Me through performing Nawafil (extra deeds besides what is obligatory) till I love him, so I become his sense of hearing with which he hears, and his sense of sight with which he sees, and his hand with which he grips, and his leg with which he walks; and if he asks Me, I will give him\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 6502).\n\nAnd forgiveness comes attached, because love without pardon would help nobody.",
  "32": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Obey Allah and His Messenger.\" If they still turn away, then truly Allah does not like the disbelievers.' (Quran 3:32). The previous verse spoke of love; this one speaks of obedience, and it puts the two side by side on purpose.\n\n'Obey Allah and His Messenger' joins the two in a single command with one verb. Muslims have understood from this that the Sunnah is not an optional supplement to the Quran but part of how the Quran is obeyed. The Book commands prayer; the Messenger showed what prayer is. Take away the second and the first cannot be carried out.\n\n'If they still turn away' — fa in tawallaw. This comes after the invitation of 3:31, which was as generous as an invitation could be: love answered with love and sins forgiven. Turning away from that is not a failure to understand. It is a decision.\n\nThe ending is stark: 'Allah does not like the disbelievers.' In an age that speaks of divine love as automatic and unconditional, this sentence lands hard. The Quran is consistent about it. Allah's mercy is vast and His love is real, but His love is described as a response, given to the sincere, the just, the patient and those who purify themselves. It is not owed to someone who has been shown the way and refused it.\n\nNotice also what the verse does not say. It does not tell the Prophet to force anyone, argue further, or take action against them. He conveys, and they choose. The relief given in 3:20 applies here too: 'your duty is only to deliver ˹the message˺'.",
  "33": "'Indeed, Allah chose Adam, Noah, the family of Abraham, and the family of 'Imran above all people ˹of their time˺.' (Quran 3:33). Here the surah turns to the story that gives it its name, and it begins by establishing a principle: Allah selects.\n\n'Istafa' means to pick out the best of something, the way pure metal is separated from ore. Four selections are named, and together they trace the line of revelation. Adam, the first human and first prophet. Nuh, through whom humanity continued after the flood. The family of Ibrahim, from whom came Isma'il, Ishaq, Ya'qub, Yusuf, Musa, 'Isa and Muhammad, peace be upon them all. And the family of 'Imran, which brings us to Maryam and her son.\n\nTwo things are worth noticing. First, these are families, not just individuals. Faith is passed down; a household can be shaped so that righteousness becomes its normal inheritance. Second, the choosing is Allah's. Nobody nominated themselves onto this list.\n\nThat second point speaks directly to the surah's audience. Both the Jews and Christians of Madinah and Najran took pride in ancestry, and both found it difficult that prophethood had moved to the Arabs. The verse quietly answers them: selection has always belonged to Allah, and He has never been restricted to one bloodline.\n\nThere is also a warning inside the honour. Being descended from the chosen is not the same as being chosen. The Quran says of Nuh's son that he was not saved, and Ibrahim was told his covenant does not reach wrongdoers among his offspring. Lineage opens a door; it does not carry you through it.",
  "34": "'They are descendants of one another. And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.' (Quran 3:34). A short verse doing two jobs.\n\n'Dhurriyyatan ba'duha min ba'd' — offspring, one from another. The prophets named in 3:33 are not scattered figures from unrelated traditions. They form a single family and a single line, physically and in message. What Adam was taught, Nuh carried; what Ibrahim was given, 'Imran's family inherited. This is why the Quran insists that Islam confirms rather than replaces: 'as He revealed the Torah and the Gospel' (Quran 3:3).\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, notes that the resemblance is in guidance as much as in blood — each generation following the way of the one before.\n\nThe two names at the end are not decoration. 'All-Hearing, All-Knowing' — Sami', 'Alim — prepare the reader for what comes next. The following verses record a woman speaking quietly in prayer, a private vow made about an unborn child (Quran 3:35), and a whispered request for protection (Quran 3:36). Allah names Himself as the One who hears immediately before recording something no one else heard.\n\nRead that way, the verse is a hinge. It closes the roll-call of great prophetic houses and opens the story of how one of those houses was actually built — not through conquest or politics, but through a woman praying over her pregnancy.\n\nThat is the pattern worth taking. The families Allah chose were formed by parents who put their children in His hands before they were born.",
  "35": "'˹Remember˺ when the wife of 'Imran said, \"My Lord! I dedicate what is in my womb entirely to Your service, so accept it from me. You ˹alone˺ are truly the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\"' (Quran 3:35). The story of Maryam begins with her mother.\n\nThe Arabic word translated 'entirely to Your service' is 'muharraran', from a root meaning to free. She is releasing the child from all other claims — not to be a helper in the household, not to be married into a good family, not to serve her own ambitions. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records that among the Children of Israel it was a practice to dedicate a child to serve the house of worship, and that this service was traditionally performed by boys.\n\nNotice she does not say 'my son'. She says 'what is in my womb', because she does not know. She makes the promise before she has any information about what she is promising.\n\n'So accept it from me' — fataqabbal minni. She gives, then asks Allah to receive the gift. That is the posture of a believer who understands that even our best offering needs to be accepted rather than assumed.\n\nShe closes with the two names given in 3:34: the All-Hearing, All-Knowing. She is alone. Nobody else hears this vow. She addresses the One who does.\n\nThe lesson is easy to state and hard to live. This woman planned her child's purpose around Allah before birth. Most parents plan around careers, security and status, and hope faith fits in somewhere. The family Allah described as chosen in 3:33 was built by a mother who reversed that order.",
  "36": "'When she delivered, she said, \"My Lord! I have given birth to a girl,\"—and Allah fully knew what she had delivered—\"and the male is not like the female. I have named her Mary, and I seek Your protection for her and her offspring from Satan, the accursed.\"' (Quran 3:36). The vow of 3:35 meets an unexpected outcome.\n\nHer words carry disappointment, and the Quran does not hide it. She had promised a child to the service of the sanctuary, and that service was performed by males. Her comment 'the male is not like the female' is the honest reaction of someone whose plan appears to have failed.\n\nThen comes the parenthesis, and it is placed by Allah, not by her: 'and Allah fully knew what she had delivered'. She thinks something has gone wrong. Allah is pointing out that nothing has. Al-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, observes that the phrase contains the whole answer — the girl she was disappointed by would be greater than any son she could have imagined, and would carry a miracle no man in history has carried.\n\nShe names the child herself: Maryam. And her final act is to place the girl and her descendants under Allah's protection from Satan.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said, \"When any human being is born, Satan touches him at both sides of the body with his two fingers, except Jesus, the son of Mary, whom Satan tried to touch but failed, for he touched the placenta-cover instead\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 3286). The mother's prayer for Maryam 'and her offspring' was answered exactly.\n\nKeep that in view when a prayer seems to have been answered wrongly. She got a girl instead of a boy, and history got Maryam.",
  "37": "'So her Lord accepted her graciously and blessed her with a pleasant upbringing—entrusting her to the care of Zachariah. Whenever Zachariah visited her in the sanctuary, he found her supplied with provisions. He exclaimed, \"O Mary! Where did this come from?\" She replied, \"It is from Allah. Surely Allah provides for whoever He wills without limit.\"' (Quran 3:37). The mother's offering is accepted, and the answer exceeds the request.\n\n'Accepted her graciously' — bi qabulin hasan, with a beautiful acceptance. The vow made over an unborn child in 3:35 was received in full, despite the mother's own doubt in 3:36.\n\n'A pleasant upbringing' — anbataha nabatan hasanan — uses the language of planting. She was grown the way a healthy plant is grown: steadily, in good soil, with care. Then Allah assigns her a guardian, and it is Zakariyya, a prophet. A child dedicated to Allah is placed in the best hands available.\n\nThe heart of the verse is what Zakariyya kept finding. Provisions appeared in her prayer-place with no visible source. His question is that of a man who has cared for her and knows exactly what she has and has not been given.\n\nHer answer is a complete theology in one line: 'It is from Allah. Surely Allah provides for whoever He wills without limit.' She does not treat the miracle as strange. She simply names the source.\n\nTwo things follow. First, the same phrase 'without limit' — bi ghayri hisab — was used of Allah in the prayer of 3:27, and here a young woman is quoting it back as lived experience. Second, this answer is what puts the idea in Zakariyya's mind. Watching provision arrive out of season made him ask for a child out of season, which is exactly where the next verse goes.",
  "38": "'Then and there Zachariah prayed to his Lord, saying, \"My Lord! Grant me—by your grace—righteous offspring. You are certainly the Hearer of ˹all˺ prayers.\"' (Quran 3:38). The two words 'then and there' — hunalika — are the most important in the verse.\n\nHe did not go home and think about it. Seeing out-of-season fruit in Maryam's chamber, he immediately asked for an out-of-season child. His reasoning was sound: a God who provides food without a source can provide a son without the usual means. Watching Allah's generosity to someone else made him hopeful for himself rather than envious of her.\n\nHe was an old man with a barren wife. He states the situation plainly elsewhere: 'My Lord! Surely my bones have become brittle, and grey hair has spread across my head, but I have never been disappointed in my prayer to You, my Lord!' (Quran 19:4). And his concern was not for company in old age but for the message: 'And I am concerned about ˹the faith of˺ my relatives after me, since my wife is barren. So grant me, by Your grace, an heir, who will inherit ˹prophethood˺ from me and the family of Jacob' (Quran 19:5-6).\n\nNotice the wording of his request: 'righteous offspring' — dhurriyyatan tayyibah. Not simply a child. A good one. Many people ask for children; fewer specify the kind.\n\n'From Your grace' — min ladunka — means from You directly, not through ordinary channels, because the ordinary channels were closed.\n\nHe ends with 'You are certainly the Hearer of prayers'. He is asking for something biologically finished, and he closes by affirming the character of the One he is asking. That is what makes a supplication faith rather than a wish.",
  "39": "'So the angels called out to him while he stood praying in the sanctuary, \"Allah gives you good news of ˹the birth of˺ John who will confirm the Word of Allah and will be a great leader, chaste, and a prophet among the righteous.\"' (Quran 3:39). The answer arrives while he is still standing in prayer.\n\nThat detail is worth pausing on. He prayed in 3:38 and the reply came before he had finished. It is one of the clearest illustrations in the Quran of what 'the Hearer of prayers' actually means.\n\nThe child is named by Allah, and the name Yahya was new: 'whose name will be John—a name We have not given to anyone before' (Quran 19:7).\n\nFour qualities are promised. He will 'confirm the Word of Allah', which the commentators, including al-Tabari on this verse, take to mean he would affirm 'Isa, since 'a Word from Him' is how 'Isa is described a few verses later (Quran 3:45). Yahya's role was to prepare the way and testify.\n\nHe will be 'a great leader' — sayyid, one whom people look up to. 'Chaste' — hasur, self-restrained, controlling his desires rather than being controlled by them. And 'a prophet among the righteous'.\n\nLook at the order. Leadership is listed alongside chastity. In the Quran's picture of greatness, mastery over yourself is part of the qualification for authority over others, not a separate private matter.\n\nFor a father who asked for 'righteous offspring', this is the answer in full detail. He asked for good; he was told exactly how good.",
  "40": "'Zachariah exclaimed, \"My Lord! How can I have a son when I am very old and my wife is barren?\" He replied, \"So will it be. Allah does what He wills.\"' (Quran 3:40). The prophet who just received his answer asks how.\n\nThis is not doubt, and it is important to see why. He had prayed for this very thing in 3:38, so he clearly believed it was possible. The question is about mechanism — by what means, given two closed doors: advanced age and a wife who had never been able to conceive. The same exchange is recorded in Surah Maryam: 'He wondered, \"My Lord! How can I have a son when my wife is barren, and I have become extremely old?\"' (Quran 19:8).\n\nThe reply is short and settles everything: 'kadhalika Allahu yaf'alu ma yasha'' — so will it be; Allah does what He wills. No explanation of the process is given. Allah does not owe the how. In the parallel account a little more is added: 'It is easy for Me, just as I created you before, when you were nothing!' (Quran 19:9). That is the decisive argument. A man asking how he can be given a son was himself brought from nothing.\n\nThere is honesty here that many believers need. Asking 'how' is not a sin. Zakariyya was a prophet, and he asked. Faith does not require pretending that obstacles are invisible; it requires trusting that they are not final.\n\nAnd notice what he does with the reply. He accepts it. He does not press for detail. The believer's position is to ask sincerely, then submit when the answer comes, even when the answer is simply: Allah does what He wills.",
  "41": "'Zachariah said, \"My Lord! Grant me a sign.\" He said, \"Your sign is that you will not ˹be able to˺ speak to people for three days except through gestures. Remember your Lord often and glorify ˹Him˺ morning and evening.\"' (Quran 3:41). He asked for a marker, and the marker he received was unusual.\n\nThe request is not doubt. He believed the promise of 3:39; he wanted to know when it had begun, so he could start thanking Allah at the right moment. Al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, explains the sign this way: it would tell him that his wife had conceived.\n\nThe sign itself is striking. He would lose the ability to speak to people for three days — not through illness, since Surah Maryam adds 'despite being healthy' (Quran 19:10). His tongue worked. It simply would not work for conversation.\n\nBut there was an exception, and it is the point of the whole thing. He could still remember Allah. Speech to people was suspended; speech to Allah was not. The command is explicit: 'Remember your Lord often and glorify Him morning and evening.'\n\nSo the man who asked for a sign was given three days of forced silence with everyone except his Lord. Even his instructions to his people had to be given by gesture: 'So he came out to his people from the sanctuary, signalling to them to glorify ˹Allah˺ morning and evening' (Quran 19:11).\n\nThere is a lesson in that design. The gift he had waited a lifetime for arrived with three days of removed distraction. Sometimes the most useful thing that can happen to a person is that the ordinary chatter stops and only dhikr is left.",
  "42": "'And ˹remember˺ when the angels said, \"O Mary! Surely Allah has selected you, purified you, and chosen you over all women of the world.' (Quran 3:42). Maryam is addressed directly by angels — the only woman in the Quran named and honoured this way.\n\nThree verbs are used, and the order matters. 'Istafaki' — He selected you, the same word used of the prophets in 'Allah chose Adam, Noah, the family of Abraham, and the family of 'Imran' (Quran 3:33). 'Tahharaki' — He purified you, cleansing her of sin and of the slander that would later be thrown at her (Quran 19:28). Then selection again, but this time specified: 'chosen you over all women of the world'.\n\nSo there is a general choosing, a purification, and then a choosing for a particular task. Allah does not assign a mission and then leave the person unprepared for it. He purifies first.\n\n'Ali reported that he heard the Prophet, peace be upon him, say, \"Mary, the daughter of 'Imran, was the best among the women (of the world of her time) and Khadija is the best amongst the women (of this nation)\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 3432).\n\nThe Quran praises her repeatedly: 'Mary, the daughter of 'Imran, who guarded her chastity... She testified to the words of her Lord and His Scriptures, and was one of the ˹sincerely˺ devout' (Quran 66:12).\n\nThere is something worth noting about who receives this honour. She held no public office, commanded no army, and led no movement. She worshipped in a small chamber and kept herself pure. Allah's ranking of people does not follow ours.",
  "43": "'O Mary! Be devout to your Lord, prostrate yourself ˹in prayer˺ and bow along with those who bow down.\"' (Quran 3:43). Immediately after being told she is the best of women, Maryam is given instructions — and they are ordinary ones.\n\nThat sequence is the lesson. High rank in the sight of Allah does not lift anyone above worship; it obliges them to more of it. Praise is followed by a command, not by a rest.\n\nThree things are named. 'Uqnuti' — be devout, from qunut, standing before Allah with humility and steady obedience, the same quality praised in 'obedient' (Quran 3:17). 'Wasjudi' — prostrate. 'Warka'i' — bow. The physical acts of prayer are specified, not just the inner attitude, because in Islam the body participates in submission.\n\nThe final phrase adds something the rest does not: 'along with those who bow down' — ma'a al-raki'in. She is told to worship with the congregation. Even a woman living a secluded life of devotion in the sanctuary is directed toward the community of worshippers rather than away from it. Islam has no tradition of holiness achieved by cutting oneself off from other believers.\n\nNotice also the order in the verse: prostration is mentioned before bowing, which is the reverse of how prayer is performed. Al-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes that the Arabic conjunction here does not require sequence, and that prostration may be mentioned first because it is the position of greatest closeness to Allah.\n\nThe takeaway is simple. Whatever Allah has given you — status, knowledge, a good reputation — the response He asks for is more worship, done alongside everyone else.",
  "44": "'This is news of the unseen that We reveal to you ˹O Prophet˺. You were not with them when they cast lots to decide who would be Mary's guardian, nor were you there when they argued ˹about it˺.' (Quran 3:44). The narrative pauses so that Allah can address the Prophet directly.\n\nThe point is evidential. Muhammad, peace be upon him, was an unlettered man in seventh-century Makkah, describing a private dispute among Israelite custodians centuries earlier. He had no access to their records and was not present. So where did the account come from?\n\nThe detail is oddly specific. They 'cast lots' — the Arabic 'yulquna aqlamahum' means they threw their pens or reeds, a known method of drawing lots. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records that they threw them into flowing water to see whose would float or hold against the current, and that Zakariyya's won. The competition over her guardianship was real enough to become an argument, which the verse also mentions.\n\nSmall, unnecessary details like this are the mark of a genuine report rather than an invented one. A forger simplifies; an eyewitness account has odd corners.\n\n'News of the unseen' — anba' al-ghayb — is a category the Quran uses for information no human channel could supply. Here it functions as proof of prophethood, offered in the middle of a story rather than as a separate argument.\n\nFor the reader, there is also something reassuring in it. A quiet contest over who would raise an orphan girl, forgotten by every historian, was recorded by Allah and preserved in a Book recited to this day. Nothing that happens to the servants of Allah is too small to be noticed.",
  "45": "'˹Remember˺ when the angels proclaimed, \"O Mary! Allah gives you good news of a Word from Him, his name will be the Messiah, Jesus, son of Mary; honoured in this world and the Hereafter, and he will be one of those nearest ˹to Allah˺.\"' (Quran 3:45). The announcement of 'Isa's birth, and every phrase in it is deliberate.\n\n'A Word from Him' — kalimatin minhu. This does not mean he is Allah's speech made flesh. The Quran explains itself: 'Isa came into being by the command 'Be', as the next verses state (Quran 3:47). He is the result of a word, not a partner in divinity. Elsewhere he is called 'the fulfilment of His Word through Mary and a spirit ˹created by a command˺ from Him' (Quran 4:171).\n\n'The Messiah' — al-Masih — is a title of honour, and the Quran uses it freely. Islam does not withhold his dignity; it withholds only his divinity.\n\n'Jesus, son of Mary' — 'Isa ibn Maryam. Arabs are named after their fathers. Naming him after his mother, in the very sentence announcing him, states his origin plainly. He had no father, and no father is implied.\n\n'Honoured in this world and the Hereafter' — wajihan, a person of standing. And 'one of those nearest to Allah', al-muqarrabin, a rank of closeness.\n\nSo the verse gives him everything except godhood. That is the Quran's consistent position: 'The Messiah, son of Mary, was no more than a messenger. ˹Many˺ messengers had ˹come and˺ gone before him. His mother was a woman of truth. They both ate food' (Quran 5:75).\n\nNote too what Maryam is told and what she is not told. She is given the honour of her son and none of the difficulty that came with him.",
  "46": "'And he will speak to people in ˹his˺ infancy and adulthood and will be one of the righteous.\"' (Quran 3:46). The announcement continues with a detail that would save his mother's reputation.\n\nSpeaking in infancy is not mentioned as a curiosity. It was the defence. When Maryam returned carrying a child and was accused, she did not argue. She pointed at the baby, and he spoke: 'Jesus declared, \"I am truly a servant of Allah. He has destined me to be given the Scripture and to be a prophet\"' (Quran 19:30).\n\nLook closely at what the infant said first. Not 'I am special'. Not a claim of any kind about himself except servanthood. The very first recorded words of 'Isa in the Quran are 'I am truly a servant of Allah'. Christians and Muslims have debated his nature for centuries, and his own opening statement is on the record.\n\nHe continued: 'He has made me a blessing wherever I go, and bid me to establish prayer and give alms-tax as long as I live, and to be kind to my mother. He has not made me arrogant or defiant' (Quran 19:31-32).\n\n'And in adulthood' — wa kahlan, in mature years. This is a small phrase with a large implication. He would live to full adulthood and preach. Some commentators, including Ibn Kathir on this verse, connect it to his return before the Day of Judgment, when he will speak to people again as a mature man.\n\n'And will be one of the righteous' closes the description. After the miracles and the titles, the final praise given to him is the same praise available to any believer: righteousness.",
  "47": "'Mary wondered, \"My Lord! How can I have a child when no man has ever touched me?\" An angel replied, \"So will it be. Allah creates what He wills. When He decrees a matter, He simply tells it, 'Be!' And it is!' (Quran 3:47). Maryam's question mirrors Zakariyya's in 3:40, and receives a similar answer.\n\nHer question is not refusal. She is a young woman being told she will bear a child, and she states the obvious obstacle. There is no shame in naming the impossible thing; both she and Zakariyya did it, and both were answered rather than rebuked.\n\n'So will it be. Allah creates what He wills' — the same construction used with Zakariyya. Then the verse adds the principle that settles every such question: 'When He decrees a matter, He simply tells it, \"Be!\" And it is.' This phrase recurs across the Quran at exactly these moments (Quran 2:117, Quran 36:82, Quran 19:35).\n\nUnderstand what that means and the debate about 'Isa's nature dissolves. Creation by command is not reproduction. A being brought into existence by 'Be' is a creature, however extraordinary the manner. The Quran makes the comparison explicit: 'Indeed, the example of Jesus in the sight of Allah is like that of Adam. He created him from dust, then said to him, \"Be!\" And he was!' (Quran 3:59). Adam had neither father nor mother, which is the greater anomaly, yet nobody worships Adam.\n\nThere is also comfort here for anyone facing a closed door. The usual means are how Allah normally works, not what He depends on. Maryam had no husband; Zakariyya had age and barrenness against him. In both cases the arrangement was not the limit.",
  "48": "'\"And Allah will teach him writing and wisdom, the Torah and the Gospel,\"' (Quran 3:48). Before any mention of miracles, the promise is about learning.\n\nFour things are named. 'Al-kitab', writing or scripture — the ability to read and record revelation. 'Al-hikmah', wisdom — knowing how to apply knowledge rightly, which is a separate gift from knowledge itself. Then two specific books: the Torah, already in the hands of his people, and the Gospel, the Injil, which he would be given.\n\nThat he is taught the Torah matters. 'Isa did not arrive to abolish what came before. He was trained in it, and he says so himself in the verses that follow: 'And I will confirm the Torah revealed before me' (Quran 3:50). The Quran's picture of revelation is a continuous line, each stage built on the one before, exactly as stated in 'confirming what came before it' (Quran 3:3).\n\nNotice the teacher: 'Allah will teach him'. His knowledge did not come from a school or a rabbi. And notice that this is listed among his honours. In a passage full of extraordinary events — a virgin birth, a speaking infant, the dead raised — Allah mentions the teaching of scripture and wisdom as a favour worth announcing.\n\nThat ranking should reset ours. We are impressed by the spectacular; the Quran gives equal weight to being taught the Book and given the sense to use it. A miracle convinces for a moment. Wisdom guides for a lifetime, and it is the only one of these gifts still available to us.",
  "49": "'and ˹make him˺ a messenger to the Children of Israel ˹to proclaim,˺ \"I have come to you with a sign from your Lord: I will make for you a bird from clay, breathe into it, and it will become a ˹real˺ bird—by Allah's Will. I will heal the blind and the leper and raise the dead to life— by Allah's Will. And I will prophesize what you eat and store in your houses. Surely in this is a sign for you if you ˹truly˺ believe.\"' (Quran 3:49). The miracles are listed, and one phrase is repeated through them like a refrain.\n\n'By Allah's permission' — bi idhni Allah — appears three times in a single verse. Every miracle is stamped with it. 'Isa forms the bird, but it lives by Allah's permission. He touches the blind, but sight returns by Allah's permission. He calls the dead, but they rise by Allah's permission. The Quran does not deny a single one of these events; it simply keeps naming the source. A power that must be permitted is not a divine power.\n\n'A messenger to the Children of Israel' also defines his mission's scope. He was sent to a specific people at a specific time, unlike the final Messenger sent 'to the whole world' (Quran 25:1).\n\nThe last item is the strangest and the most personal: 'I will prophesize what you eat and store in your houses.' Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, notes that he would tell people what they had eaten that morning and what they had hidden away. This is not a grand cosmic sign. It is a private detail, and that is precisely what makes it hard to dismiss.\n\nWhy these particular miracles? Because Allah gives each prophet a sign in the field his people excelled at. Musa's era prized magic; his staff defeated the magicians. 'Isa's era prized medicine; he healed what no physician could. The proof is always pitched at what the audience considers expert.",
  "50": "'\"And I will confirm the Torah revealed before me and legalize some of what had been forbidden to you. I have come to you with a sign from your Lord, so be mindful of Allah and obey me.\"' (Quran 3:50). 'Isa states his mission in his own words, and it contains a correction and a relief.\n\n'Confirming the Torah' places him inside the tradition, not outside it. He did not come to start a rival religion, and Christians who read his mission as the abolition of the Law are reading against his own statement here.\n\n'Legalize some of what had been forbidden to you' is the adjustment. Certain restrictions had been placed on the Children of Israel as a penalty for earlier disobedience, and others had been added by their scholars over time. Part of 'Isa's task was to lift some of that weight. Al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, notes both readings: relief from the penalties Allah had imposed, and correction of what people had made unlawful on their own authority.\n\nThere is a pattern here worth noticing. Religious communities tend to accumulate prohibitions. Each generation adds a caution, and after centuries the faith feels heavier than the revelation ever intended. Prophets repeatedly arrive to remove that accumulated weight rather than add to it.\n\nThe verse ends with two commands: 'be mindful of Allah and obey me'. Taqwa first, then obedience to the messenger — the same pairing given to this ummah in 'Obey Allah and His Messenger' (Quran 3:32). Consciousness of Allah is what makes obedience to a human messenger reasonable rather than servile. You follow him because of who sent him.",
  "51": "'\"Surely Allah is my Lord and your Lord. So worship Him ˹alone˺. This is the Straight Path.'\"' (Quran 3:51). This is 'Isa's own summary of his message, and the surah preserves it word for word.\n\n'My Lord and your Lord' — rabbi wa rabbukum. He puts himself and his audience in the same category. Both are servants of the same Lord. If he were divine, this sentence could not have been said. He does not claim to be the object of worship; he directs worship past himself.\n\nHe said the same thing in other settings: 'O Children of Israel! Worship Allah—my Lord and your Lord' (Quran 5:72). And on the Day of Judgment, when questioned, he will disown the claim entirely: 'Glory be to You! How could I ever say what I had no right to say?' (Quran 5:116).\n\n'So worship Him alone' — fa'buduh. One command, no conditions. This is the message every prophet brought, from Nuh to Muhammad, peace be upon them all.\n\n'This is the Straight Path' — sirat mustaqim. The same phrase Muslims recite in every unit of prayer when reading al-Fatihah. 'Isa's path and the Muslim's path are named identically because they are the same path.\n\nTwo consequences follow. First, a Muslim cannot speak dismissively of 'Isa; he is a great messenger who called to exactly what we call to. Second, a Muslim cannot accept his deification either, because doing so would contradict the man himself. Honouring a teacher means taking his words seriously, and these were his words.",
  "52": "'When Jesus sensed disbelief from his people, he asked, \"Who will stand up with me for Allah?\" The disciples replied, \"We will stand up for Allah. We believe in Allah, so bear witness that we have submitted.\"' (Quran 3:52). After the miracles and the clear message, most of his people refused. This is what he did next.\n\nHe did not chase the majority. He looked for a core. The Arabic 'man ansari ila Allah' means who are my helpers toward Allah — who will move in this direction with me. It is a call for committed partners, not for a crowd.\n\nThe word 'ansar' is the same word the Muslims of Madinah would later carry as their title, and the parallel is intentional. Every truthful mission reaches a point where the general public falls away and a small group has to carry it.\n\nThe disciples — al-hawariyyun — answer with three statements. 'We will stand up for Allah' is commitment. 'We believe in Allah' is faith. 'Bear witness that we have submitted' — bi anna muslimun — is the public declaration. They ask 'Isa himself to be their witness, which is what a person does when they intend to be held to something.\n\nAllah later uses this exact scene to instruct Muslims: 'O believers! Stand up for Allah, as Jesus, son of Mary, asked the disciples, \"Who will stand up with me for Allah?\"... We then supported the believers against their enemies, so they prevailed' (Quran 61:14).\n\nThe encouragement in that is real. The number of people who agree with you is not the measure of whether you are right, and a handful of committed helpers has repeatedly been enough.",
  "53": "'˹They prayed to Allah,˺ \"Our Lord! We believe in Your revelations and follow the messenger, so count us among those who bear witness.\"' (Quran 3:53). Having declared their commitment to 'Isa in 3:52, the disciples now turn and address Allah directly.\n\nThat turn is instructive. A pledge made to a human being is confirmed before Allah, because He is the one who can actually keep them faithful to it.\n\nTheir prayer has three parts. 'We believe in Your revelations' — belief in what was sent down. 'We follow the messenger' — obedience to the one who brought it. Belief and following are named separately, as they always are in the Quran, because agreeing with a message is not the same as acting on it.\n\nThen the request: 'count us among those who bear witness' — ma'a al-shahidin. They do not ask for safety, victory or reward. They ask to be recorded on the right side. A shahid here is one who testifies to the truth by affirming it and living it.\n\nThis should be read against the surah's earlier warning about people who received scripture and turned away when it did not suit them (Quran 3:23). The disciples are the opposite case: they receive the message and ask to be enrolled among its witnesses.\n\nThe prayer is worth borrowing. Most of us pray for outcomes — health, provision, ease. Here is a supplication for a category: let me be counted among a certain kind of people. That is a request about who you are rather than what you get, and it is the more valuable of the two.",
  "54": "'And the disbelievers made a plan ˹against Jesus˺, but Allah also planned —and Allah is the best of planners.' (Quran 3:54). A short verse covering an enormous event.\n\nThe plot was to kill him. Those who rejected 'Isa arranged his arrest and execution, and as far as they knew, it worked. The Quran states otherwise: 'But they neither killed nor crucified him—it was only made to appear so... They certainly did not kill him. Rather, Allah raised him up to Himself' (Quran 4:157-158).\n\nThe word used for both sides is 'makr', translated here as plan. It means a hidden scheme. Applied to people it carries the sense of deceit; applied to Allah it means His countering arrangement, undetectable to those it works against. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, explains it as Allah's response to their plotting, turning their scheme back on them.\n\n'Allah is the best of planners' — khayru al-makirin. The same words appear about the plot against the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him: 'when the disbelievers conspired to capture, kill, or exile you. They planned, but Allah also planned. And Allah is the best of planners' (Quran 8:30). On the night they surrounded his house, he walked out through them. The pattern repeats because the One arranging it does not change.\n\nWhat makes Allah's planning better is not merely greater power. It is that He knows their plan completely while they know nothing of His. They are playing a game in which the other side sees every card.\n\nFor anyone who has been schemed against, this verse is the whole answer. You may not see the counter-plan, and you are not required to. It is already running.",
  "55": "'˹Remember˺ when Allah said, \"O Jesus! I will take you and raise you up to Myself. I will deliver you from those who disbelieve, and elevate your followers above the disbelievers until the Day of Judgment. Then to Me you will ˹all˺ return, and I will settle all your disputes.\"' (Quran 3:55). This is the answer to the plot mentioned in 3:54.\n\n'I will take you' — inni mutawaffika. The Arabic root wafa means to take something in full. It is used for death, but not only for death; the Quran uses the same word for sleep: 'Allah ˹Who˺ calls back the souls ˹of people˺ upon their death as well as ˹the souls˺ of the living during their sleep' (Quran 39:42). Commentators differ on its precise sense here. What the verse states without ambiguity is 'and raise you up to Myself' — rafi'uka ilayya — and that 'they neither killed nor crucified him' (Quran 4:157).\n\n'I will deliver you from those who disbelieve' — the rescue is stated plainly. Their plan failed.\n\n'Elevate your followers above the disbelievers until the Day of Judgment' has been read by commentators, including Ibn Kathir on this verse, as referring to those who genuinely followed 'Isa's message — a promise fulfilled in history and completed by the Muslims who affirm him as Allah's servant and messenger.\n\nThe last line is where all the arguments end: 'Then to Me you will all return, and I will settle all your disputes.' Centuries of dispute about 'Isa — his nature, his death, his return — are not going to be resolved by debate. Allah says He will settle it Himself.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said, \"By Him in Whose Hands my soul is, surely (Jesus,) the son of Mary will soon descend amongst you and will judge mankind justly\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 3448).",
  "56": "'\"As for those who disbelieve, I will subject them to a severe punishment in this life and the Hereafter, and they will have no helpers.\"' (Quran 3:56). Allah's speech to 'Isa continues, now turning to those who rejected him.\n\nTwo timings are given, and both matter. 'In this life' — punishment that arrives before death, in the form of humiliation, loss and the collapse of what they built. 'And the Hereafter' — the lasting one. The Quran repeatedly pairs the two so that nobody assumes the account is settled by getting away with something now.\n\n'They will have no helpers' — min nasirin. This exact phrase appeared earlier in the surah about those who killed the prophets (Quran 3:22). The repetition draws the line: rejecting a messenger puts a person in the same category regardless of which messenger it was.\n\nThe word 'helpers' is pointed, because the disciples in 3:52 were called by the same root — ansar, helpers of Allah. Those who chose to help Allah's cause are helped; those who opposed it find no one.\n\nIt is worth noticing who this warning is addressed to and who it is not. It is not addressed to Christians as such. It is addressed to those who disbelieved in 'Isa when he came to them with clear proof. The Quran's argument with later Christians is about a specific claim regarding his nature, and it makes that argument with evidence and invitation, as the next verses will show.\n\nThe general principle stands for everyone, including Muslims: when the truth is made clear and a person refuses it, no relationship, group membership or ancestry will supply a defence later.",
  "57": "'\"And as for those who believe and do good, they will be rewarded in full. And Allah does not like the wrongdoers.\"' (Quran 3:57). The balancing half of 3:56, and the two conditions named are the ones the Quran always pairs.\n\n'Those who believe and do good' — amanu wa 'amilu al-salihat. Belief and action, never one alone. Faith that produces nothing is not treated as faith in the Quran's language, and good works without faith have already been described as 'wasted in this world and the Hereafter' (Quran 3:22).\n\n'Rewarded in full' — yuwaffihim ujurahum. The same root as in 'every soul will be paid in full for what it has done' (Quran 3:25). It is the language of wages paid completely, with nothing deducted. Whatever was done for Allah is accounted for, including the parts nobody saw.\n\nThen the closing phrase, which does not seem to follow: 'And Allah does not like the wrongdoers.' Why mention wrongdoers in a verse about the reward of the righteous?\n\nAl-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes that the pairing warns believers not to assume that their category protects them. Faith does not license injustice. A person can believe correctly and still wrong others, and Allah's dislike of wrongdoing is stated without exception for anyone.\n\nThere is also a subtler point. The reward is described as being paid in full, and then injustice is condemned — as if to say that Allah pays exactly what is owed because He does not do what wrongdoers do. His fairness to His servants is a reflection of His own nature, not a favour He might withhold.",
  "58": "'We recite ˹all˺ this to you ˹O Prophet˺ as one of the signs and ˹as˺ a wise reminder.' (Quran 3:58). The long account of 'Imran's family, Maryam and 'Isa closes with a comment on why it was told.\n\n'This' refers to everything from 3:33 onward. And it is described as two things.\n\nFirst, 'one of the signs' — min al-ayat. The knowledge itself is evidence. An unlettered man in Makkah accurately relating events among the Israelites centuries earlier is a proof of revelation, exactly as stated in 'This is news of the unseen that We reveal to you' (Quran 3:44). The delegation from Najran could check the account against what they knew, and the correspondence was itself an argument.\n\nSecond, 'a wise reminder' — al-dhikr al-hakim. Not merely information. A reminder is meant to change the listener, and 'wise' means it is precisely fitted to its purpose. Every element in the story does work: a mother's vow, a guardian's question, an old man's prayer, a young woman's trial, a prophet's message and a failed plot.\n\n'We recite to you' — natluhu 'alayk. The Prophet is the recipient, not the author. The verb keeps the direction clear.\n\nThere is a lesson in how the Quran tells history. It never narrates for the sake of the record. Dates are absent, place names are rare, and details that would satisfy a historian are left out, while the details that shape a heart are kept. If you finish a Quranic story knowing more facts but living no differently, you have collected the packaging and left the contents.",
  "59": "'Indeed, the example of Jesus in the sight of Allah is like that of Adam. He created him from dust, then said to him, \"Be!\" And he was!' (Quran 3:59). This is the decisive argument of the whole passage, and it is disarmingly simple.\n\nThe claim being answered is that 'Isa's birth without a father proves he is more than human. The reply: consider Adam. He had no father and no mother. He was made from dust. If an unusual origin makes someone divine, Adam has the stronger case, and nobody has ever worshipped Adam.\n\nThis is reasoning the listener can complete himself. The Quran does not simply assert; it puts two facts side by side and lets the comparison do the work.\n\n'Then said to him, \"Be!\" And he was' — the same creative command that answered Maryam in 3:47. Creation by command is the mark of a creature. What is created by a word cannot be the one speaking it.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records that this passage was revealed regarding the Christian delegation from Najran who came to debate the Prophet, peace be upon him, about 'Isa. This verse was the core of the answer given to them.\n\nNotice what the argument does not do. It does not belittle 'Isa. Comparing him to Adam is comparing him to a prophet whom the angels were commanded to honour. The point is not that he is small. It is that he is created — extraordinary, honoured, chosen, and still a servant, exactly as he said of himself: 'I am truly a servant of Allah' (Quran 19:30).",
  "60": "'This is the truth from your Lord, so do not be one of those who doubt.' (Quran 3:60). After the argument comes the settlement.\n\n'Al-haqq min rabbik' — the truth from your Lord. Not an opinion to be weighed against other opinions, and not the Prophet's own conclusion. The source is named, which is what gives it its authority.\n\nThe address is to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and yet he was the least likely of all people to doubt. Commentators, including al-Tabari on this verse, explain that such addresses are directed at him in form but aimed at everyone who hears the Quran. The same construction appears elsewhere: 'Those who were given the Scripture know that it has been revealed ˹to you˺ from your Lord in truth. So do not be one of those who doubt' (Quran 6:114).\n\nWhy guard against doubt here specifically? Because this is a question people find genuinely difficult. The status of 'Isa has divided nations for two thousand years, and the pressure to soften the position — to blur it into something everyone can accept — is constant. The verse closes that exit.\n\nNote the precise wording: do not be 'one of those who doubt' — mina al-mumtarin. It speaks of belonging to a group rather than having a passing thought. A doubt that crosses the mind is not the sin; taking up residence with the doubters is.\n\nThe practical guidance is this. When Allah has spoken clearly on something, further debate is not humility, and hesitation is not open-mindedness. Certainty about what has been made certain is part of faith, and it makes the rest of life much simpler.",
  "61": "'Now, whoever disputes with you ˹O Prophet˺ concerning Jesus after full knowledge has come to you, say, \"Come! Let us gather our children and your children, our women and your women, ourselves and yourselves—then let us sincerely invoke Allah's curse upon the liars.\"' (Quran 3:61). This is the mubahalah — the mutual invocation — and it was a real event.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records the occasion: the Christian delegation of Najran had debated the Prophet, peace be upon him, at length, and when the arguments were exhausted and they still refused, this verse was revealed. He invited them to a solemn joint prayer, each side asking Allah to curse whichever party was lying.\n\nNotice the terms. Not a debate, not a battle, and not a vote. Both sides bring their families — children, women, and themselves — and ask Allah to decide. The inclusion of children and wives is the point: a man might gamble with his own fate, but nobody brings his family to a curse unless he is certain.\n\nThe delegation withdrew. They consulted among themselves, concluded that he was a prophet, and declined the challenge, choosing instead to make a treaty. Their refusal was itself a verdict.\n\nThe condition attached is important: 'after full knowledge has come to you'. This procedure is not for someone still learning or genuinely searching. It is for a person who has seen the evidence and continues to argue anyway.\n\nThere is a broader lesson about certainty. The Prophet was willing to stake his family on this. That is not the behaviour of a man advancing a theory. It is the behaviour of a man reporting a fact.",
  "62": "'Certainly, this is the true narrative, and there is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Allah. And indeed, Allah ˹alone˺ is the Almighty, All-Wise.' (Quran 3:62). The account of 'Isa is sealed with a verdict.\n\n'The true narrative' — la huwa al-qasas al-haqq. The Arabic 'qasas' means a story told by following its traces, the way a tracker follows footprints. What the Quran has given is the account that actually follows the trail, as distinct from the versions circulating among the communities of the time.\n\nThat was a bold claim to make in seventh-century Arabia, addressed to people who possessed their own scriptures and traditions about 'Isa. The Quran does not soften it. Having given the account and the argument, it states the conclusion.\n\nThen, as consistently happens in this surah, the discussion returns to the one point that everything rests on: 'there is no god worthy of worship except Allah.' This is what the whole debate about 'Isa was ever about. Not history, not miracles, not titles — whether anything besides Allah is worshipped. The same declaration was made at the surah's opening (Quran 3:2) and in the great testimony of 3:18.\n\n'The Almighty, All-Wise' — 'Aziz, Hakim — closes the verse. Al-'Aziz has the power to enforce, which answers those who thought they could kill a prophet and end the matter. Al-Hakim places everything, including the virgin birth and the rescue from the cross, inside a wisdom that had a purpose.\n\nThe takeaway is that every religious question eventually reduces to this one. Who is being worshipped? Get that right and the rest falls into place.",
  "63": "'If they turn away, then surely Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of the corruptors.' (Quran 3:63). The passage ends the way the earlier one did, by handing the outcome over.\n\n'If they turn away' — fa in tawallaw. The same phrase used in 3:20 and 3:32. It appears each time a full case has been presented and refused. The Quran does not follow refusal with pressure. It follows it with a statement about Allah's knowledge.\n\nThe label used is worth pausing on: 'the corruptors' — al-mufsidin, those who spread corruption. Not simply the mistaken, and not the ignorant. Why such a strong word for people who declined a religious argument?\n\nBecause of what precedes it. These are people who had knowledge (3:61), saw the evidence, refused a solemn invocation they could have accepted if they were confident, and then continued teaching others what they had privately conceded was weak. Corruption here is misleading people about Allah while knowing better. Al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, connects the term to those who conceal the truth and lead others away from it.\n\n'Allah has perfect knowledge' — 'alimun bi al-mufsidin. The Prophet is not asked to judge them, name them or act against them. The knowledge sits with Allah and so, by implication, does the response.\n\nThis is a discipline worth learning. After you have said your piece honestly, the temptation is to keep pushing, to win, to expose. The Quran's instruction to its own Prophet was to state the truth, then stop, and to leave the assessment of hearts to the One who can see them.",
  "64": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"O People of the Book! Let us come to common terms: that we will worship none but Allah, associate none with Him, nor take one another as lords instead of Allah.\" But if they turn away, then say, \"Bear witness that we have submitted ˹to Allah alone˺.\"' (Quran 3:64). After the argument closes, the door opens again — and this is one of the most quoted verses in the Quran on interfaith relations.\n\n'Let us come to common terms' — kalimatin sawa'in baynana wa baynakum, a word that is equal between us and you. Not a compromise in which each side gives up part of its faith. A shared foundation that both sides already claim to hold.\n\nThree terms are named. Worship none but Allah. Associate nothing with Him. And do not take one another as lords instead of Allah. The third is aimed at religious authority — the habit of letting scholars and clergy make things lawful and unlawful on their own say-so, which the Quran describes as taking them as lords: 'They have taken their rabbis and monks as well as the Messiah, son of Mary, as lords besides Allah' (Quran 9:31). Muslims are not exempt from that warning.\n\nThis verse was not left on the page. Abu Sufyan described the Prophet's letter to Heraclius, the Byzantine emperor, and reported that it contained the words: \"O people of the scripture! Come to a word common to you and us that we worship none but Allah and that we associate nothing in worship with Him, and that none of us shall take others as Lords beside Allah. Then, if they turn away, say: Bear witness that we are Muslims\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 7).\n\nIf they refuse, the response is neither hostility nor concealment: state your own position clearly and let it stand.",
  "65": "'O People of the Book! Why do you argue about Abraham, while the Torah and the Gospel were not revealed until long after him? Do you not understand?' (Quran 3:65). A new argument begins, and it is settled by a simple matter of dates.\n\nBoth Jews and Christians claimed Ibrahim as their own. Each wanted the prestige of the great patriarch on their side of the dispute with the Prophet, peace be upon him.\n\nThe Quran's reply is chronological. Ibrahim lived long before Musa, and Musa received the Torah. He lived far longer before 'Isa, who was given the Gospel. So Ibrahim could not have been a Jew or a Christian, because neither the Torah nor the Gospel existed in his lifetime. The categories being fought over were created centuries after the man died.\n\n'Do you not understand?' — afala ta'qilun. The Quran appeals to reason, not authority, to close the point. Anyone with a rough sense of the sequence can work it out.\n\nThe same challenge appears in Surah al-Baqarah: 'Or do you claim that Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and his descendants were all Jews or Christians?\" Say, \"Who is more knowledgeable: you or Allah?\"' (Quran 2:140).\n\nThere is a habit being exposed here that outlives its original setting. People routinely claim great figures of the past for their own group, projecting later labels backwards. It happens with scholars, movements and nations. The corrective is the one the verse uses: check the timeline, and ask whether the category you are claiming even existed.",
  "66": "'Here you are! You disputed about what you have ˹little˺ knowledge of, but why do you now argue about what you have no knowledge of? Allah knows and you do not know.' (Quran 3:66). The rebuke sharpens, and it distinguishes two kinds of arguing.\n\nThe first: 'you disputed about what you have some knowledge of'. On matters within their own scriptures — the law of Musa, the message of 'Isa — they had genuine information, even if they used it selectively. Arguing there was at least about something they possessed.\n\nThe second: 'why do you now argue about what you have no knowledge of?' The religion of Ibrahim, who lived before either book was revealed, is outside anything they could claim to know from their own sources. They were not defending evidence. They were defending a preference.\n\n'Allah knows and you do not know' — wallahu ya'lamu wa antum la ta'lamun. The sentence is short and closes the subject. There is a Source with the information, and it is not either of the disputing parties.\n\nThe distinction matters far beyond this occasion. Speaking beyond one's knowledge is treated in the Quran as a serious offence, not a small social fault, especially when the subject is Allah and His religion.\n\nApplied to us: the most confident religious opinions are often expressed by those who read the least. The correct answer, when you do not know, is that you do not know. Saying so costs a little pride and prevents a great deal of harm, and it puts you in the company of the angels, who said as much when they were asked something beyond their knowledge.",
  "67": "'Abraham was neither a Jew nor a Christian; he submitted in all uprightness and was not a polytheist.' (Quran 3:67). The direct answer to the dispute, stated in one line.\n\nTwo negations and two affirmations. He was not a Jew, not a Christian, and not a polytheist. What he was is 'hanifan musliman' — upright and submitted.\n\n'Hanif' means one who turns away from everything false toward what is true, a person who leans away from the crowd. Ibrahim lived among idol-worshippers, including his own household, and turned from all of it.\n\n'Muslim' here carries its root meaning: one who submits himself to Allah. Used this way, it is not the name of a later community but a description of a stance, exactly as in 'Allah's only Way is Islam' (Quran 3:19). Ibrahim was a muslim in the plainest sense — a man who handed himself over to Allah completely, which is what his entire life story demonstrates.\n\nThe Quran repeats this description often: 'Indeed, Abraham was a model of excellence: devoted to Allah, ˹perfectly˺ upright—not a polytheist' (Quran 16:120), and the Prophet was told, 'Follow the faith of Abraham, the upright, who was not one of the polytheists' (Quran 16:123).\n\nThe clause 'and was not a polytheist' is added even though it seems obvious. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, notes that it addresses the Arab idol-worshippers of Makkah too, who also claimed descent from Ibrahim while worshipping stone. Everyone wanted his name. Nobody wanted his practice.\n\nThat gap is the point. Claiming a heritage is easy; carrying it is not.",
  "68": "'Indeed, those who have the best claim to Abraham are his followers, this Prophet, and the believers. And Allah is the Guardian of those who believe.' (Quran 3:68). Having said what Ibrahim was not, the verse now says who actually belongs to him.\n\n'Awla al-nas bi Ibrahim' — the people with the strongest claim to him. And the criterion is not blood. Three groups are named: those who followed him in his own time, 'this Prophet' — Muhammad, peace be upon him — and the believers who follow him.\n\nThat is a redefinition of religious inheritance. Descent from Ibrahim is shared by Jews, Christians and Arabs alike, and the verse quietly sets it aside as the deciding factor. Connection is by conviction and conduct. The one who submits to Allah as Ibrahim submitted is his heir; the one who does not is merely a relative.\n\nThe Prophet is identified as continuing that line. This is the answer to the Makkans who claimed Ibrahim through Isma'il while worshipping idols, and to the People of the Book who claimed him through Ishaq while rejecting the final messenger. The Quran points to Ibrahim's own prayer at the Ka'bah: 'Our Lord! Make us both ˹fully˺ submit to You and from our descendants a nation that will submit to you' (Quran 2:128).\n\nThen the closing phrase, which is a promise rather than an argument: 'Allah is the Guardian of those who believe' — wali al-mu'minin. The word wali appeared earlier in the surah as the alliance believers must not give away (Quran 3:28). Here it returns as the alliance they receive. Give your loyalty to Allah, and He becomes your protector. That is the trade the whole passage has been building toward.",
  "69": "'Some of the People of the Book wish to mislead you ˹believers˺. They mislead none but themselves, yet they fail to perceive it.' (Quran 3:69). A warning, and notice how carefully it is worded.\n\n'Some of' — ta'ifatun min, a group among them. The Quran does not accuse a whole community. Throughout this surah it distinguishes between groups, and it will shortly praise those among them who are honest and upright (Quran 3:75, Quran 3:113).\n\nTheir aim is 'to mislead you' — yudillunakum. Not to defeat the Muslims militarily but to unsettle their faith, which is the harder loss to recover from. The same motive appears in Surah al-Baqarah: 'Many among the People of the Book wish they could turn you ˹believers˺ back to disbelief because of their envy, after the truth has been made clear to them' (Quran 2:109).\n\nThen the twist: 'They mislead none but themselves.' The plan rebounds. Someone who spends his energy trying to shake another person's certainty must keep handling arguments he knows are weak, and he is the one exposed to them longest. Al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, explains that the sin and its consequence fall back on the plotters, since Allah protects the believers.\n\n'Yet they fail to perceive it' — wa ma yash'urun. They cannot see the damage as it happens. That is the nature of this kind of harm; it is measured in a hardening of the heart, which nobody notices in themselves day to day.\n\nThe believer's response is not paranoia but grounding. Know why you believe what you believe. A person who has actually examined his faith is not easily unsettled by someone else's confidence.",
  "70": "'O People of the Book! Why do you reject the signs of Allah while you bear witness ˹to their truth˺?' (Quran 3:70). The address is direct and the question is uncomfortable, because it names what they already know.\n\n'While you bear witness' — wa antum tashhadun. This is the heart of it. The Quran's charge is not that they failed to recognise the truth but that they recognised it and rejected it anyway. The description of that recognition elsewhere is striking: 'Those We have given the Scripture recognize this ˹Prophet˺ as they recognize their own children' (Quran 2:146), repeated in 'Those to whom We gave the Scripture recognize him ˹to be a true prophet˺ as they recognize their own children' (Quran 6:20).\n\nTheir own books described the coming prophet: 'the unlettered Prophet, whose description they find in their Torah and the Gospel' (Quran 7:157). And 'Isa himself had announced him: 'giving good news of a messenger after me whose name will be Ahmad' (Quran 61:6).\n\nSo the question 'why?' is genuine and has no good answer. Rejecting something you have not seen is ignorance. Rejecting what you have identified is something else.\n\nThe Quran uses a question rather than an accusation, and that is a method worth learning. A direct charge invites defence; a question forces the person to supply his own answer, and he already knows there isn't one.\n\nEvery reader has a version of this. There are things we know are true and act against anyway — a duty postponed, a habit we can defend to others but not to ourselves. The verse asks the same question of us: why, when you already know?",
  "71": "'O People of the Book! Why do you mix the truth with falsehood and hide the truth knowingly?' (Quran 3:71). Two techniques are named, and both are more sophisticated than simple lying.\n\nThe first is mixing — talbisun al-haqqa bi al-batil. Truth blended with falsehood is far more effective than falsehood alone, because the true parts carry the false ones. Nobody can be misled by a claim they can immediately check; they are misled when nine accurate statements are used to smuggle in a tenth.\n\nThe second is hiding — taktumuna al-haqq. Not saying anything untrue at all, simply leaving out the part that would change the conclusion. Selective silence is a form of deception that never requires a false word.\n\nBoth were forbidden in Surah al-Baqarah in almost identical language: 'Do not mix truth with falsehood or hide the truth knowingly' (Quran 2:42). And the warning for concealment is severe: 'Those who hide the clear proofs and guidance that We have revealed— after We made it clear for humanity in the Book—will be condemned by Allah' (Quran 2:159).\n\n'Knowingly' — wa antum ta'lamun — appears again, as in 3:70. The offence is deliberate.\n\nThis is not a foreign problem. Anyone who quotes a verse without its context, cites a scholar's opinion while omitting his conditions, or presents one side of a case as the whole of it, is doing exactly what is described here. The standard the verse sets is high: not merely that we say nothing false, but that we do not use true things to produce a false impression.",
  "72": "'A group among the People of the Book said ˹to one another˺, \"Believe in what has been revealed to the believers in the morning and reject it in the evening, so they may abandon their faith.\"' (Quran 3:72). A tactic is exposed, and it is a clever one.\n\nThe plan was psychological rather than argumentative. Publicly embrace Islam in the morning; publicly abandon it by nightfall. Say nothing against it directly. The intended effect on the Muslims, especially new or uncertain ones, was the thought: these are people who know scripture, who studied the earlier books, and they examined this religion and walked away. There must be something wrong with it.\n\nIt works because it borrows authority instead of making a claim. No argument is offered, so no argument can be answered.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records this as an actual scheme discussed among a group in Madinah in the early period, aimed at unsettling the community from within.\n\nThe verse's answer is simply to publish the plot. Allah quotes their private conversation back to them, which both warns the believers and reminds the plotters that nothing said in a closed room is closed: 'Whether you conceal what is in your hearts or reveal it, it is known to Allah' (Quran 3:29).\n\nThe modern version needs no updating. Public, well-publicised departures from the faith, staged for their effect on the watching audience, are the same instrument. The defence is also the same: judge a religion by its evidence, not by the traffic in and out of it. People leave the truth for many reasons, and most of them have nothing to do with whether it is true.",
  "73": "'And only believe those who follow your religion.\" Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Surely, ˹the only˺ true guidance is Allah's guidance.\" ˹They also said,˺ \"Do not believe that someone will receive ˹revealed˺ knowledge similar to yours or argue against you before your Lord.\" Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Indeed, all bounty is in the Hands of Allah—He grants it to whoever He wills. And Allah is All-Bountiful, All-Knowing.\"' (Quran 3:73). The plot of 3:72 continues, and the motive underneath it surfaces.\n\n'Only believe those who follow your religion' is instruction to a closed circle: trust nothing from outside the group. It is the reflex of a community that has stopped testing claims and started sorting by membership.\n\nThe reply cuts through it in one line: 'the only true guidance is Allah's guidance'. Guidance is not tribal property. It belongs to Allah, and He directs it where He wills.\n\nThen their real fear is quoted: do not accept 'that someone will receive revealed knowledge similar to yours'. This was never about evidence. It was about exclusivity. They could not accept that revelation had gone to another community, which is precisely the envy the Quran names elsewhere: 'Or do they envy the people for Allah's bounties?' (Quran 4:54), and 'resenting Allah for granting His grace to whoever He wills of His servants' (Quran 2:90).\n\nThe answer is the correction they needed: 'all bounty is in the Hands of Allah—He grants it to whoever He wills.' The same statement appears in 'they do not have any control over Allah's grace, and that all grace is in Allah's Hands' (Quran 57:29).\n\nMuslims should read this carefully rather than comfortably. Any community can start behaving as though Allah's favour is its possession rather than His gift.",
  "74": "'He chooses whoever He wills to receive His mercy. And Allah is the Lord of infinite bounty.' (Quran 3:74). A short verse that closes the argument about who deserves Allah's favour.\n\n'Yakhtassu bi rahmatihi man yasha'' — He singles out for His mercy whoever He wills. The verb means to designate something for a particular recipient. Prophethood, guidance, and every spiritual gift are assigned by Allah, not claimed by the applicant. The same wording appears in Surah al-Baqarah, in the same context of resentment over the Prophet's appointment: 'Allah selects whoever He wills for His mercy. And Allah is the Lord of infinite bounty' (Quran 2:105).\n\nSome readers hear this as arbitrary. It is not. Elsewhere the Quran describes Allah as knowing exactly where to place His message, and His choices in this surah followed clear qualities: Maryam was purified before she was chosen (Quran 3:42), and the family of Ibrahim was selected after Ibrahim's lifetime of submission. Allah's will is informed by His knowledge, which is why the verse ends with a name of vastness rather than caprice.\n\n'Dhu al-fadl al-'azim' — the Lord of tremendous bounty. Fadl means what is given beyond what is owed. Nothing described in this surah was earned: not the child given to Zakariyya, not the provision found with Maryam, not prophethood.\n\nThe practical benefit is the end of envy. Envy assumes a fixed supply and a misallocation. This verse denies both. Allah's bounty is not limited, and what He gave to someone else was never coming to you by right in the first place. What He might give you is still open, and asking Him is a far better use of energy than resenting a decision.",
  "75": "'There are some among the People of the Book who, if entrusted with a stack of gold, will readily return it. Yet there are others who, if entrusted with a single coin, will not repay it unless you constantly demand it. This is because they say, \"We are not accountable for ˹exploiting˺ the Gentiles.\" And ˹so˺ they attribute lies to Allah knowingly.' (Quran 3:75). The verse is scrupulously fair, and that is the first thing to notice.\n\nTwo types are described. Some are so trustworthy that a fortune left with them comes back intact — 'a stack of gold', qintar, a very large sum. Others will not return a single dinar without being chased. The Quran refuses to flatten a community into one description, and this even-handedness is a standard for how Muslims should speak about others.\n\nThen the reasoning behind the dishonesty is quoted: 'We are not accountable for exploiting the Gentiles' — laysa 'alayna fi al-ummiyyina sabil. The claim is that cheating outsiders carries no blame. Ethics apply inside the group and stop at its border.\n\nThe Quran's verdict is severe: 'they attribute lies to Allah knowingly.' Making a rule like that is not merely dishonest; it puts words in Allah's mouth, because it presents selective ethics as divine permission.\n\nThis is a live temptation in every religious community, including ours. Honesty with fellow believers and sharp practice with everyone else is exactly the attitude condemned here. The Prophet, peace be upon him, described the character it produces: \"The signs of a hypocrite are three: whenever he speaks, he tells a lie; whenever he promises, he always breaks it; if you trust him, he proves to be dishonest\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 33). No exemption for who the other party is.",
  "76": "'Absolutely! Those who honour their trusts and shun evil—surely Allah loves those who are mindful ˹of Him˺.' (Quran 3:76). The reply to the excuse quoted in 3:75, and it begins with a word of emphatic contradiction.\n\n'Bala' — yes indeed, on the contrary. It is used in Arabic to overturn a negative statement. They said there is no blame on us regarding outsiders; this word says: there certainly is.\n\nTwo conditions are named. 'Honour their trusts' — awfa bi 'ahdih, fulfils his covenant. This covers promises to Allah and promises to people, contracts, debts and anything placed in your care. Returning trusts is a repeated command: 'Indeed, Allah commands you to return trusts to their rightful owners' (Quran 4:58), and it is a mark of the believers: '˹the believers are also˺ those who are true to their trusts and covenants' (Quran 23:8).\n\n'And shun evil' — wattaqa, guards himself. Keeping agreements is not enough on its own if the rest of the conduct is careless.\n\nThen the reward, and it is the highest one the Quran offers: 'surely Allah loves those who are mindful of Him.' Not merely rewards them — loves them. This is the second time in the surah that Allah's love is attached to a condition, after 'If you ˹sincerely˺ love Allah, then follow me; Allah will love you' (Quran 3:31).\n\nThe lesson is plain. Trustworthiness is not a social nicety that sits below worship in importance. It is placed here as a qualification for Allah's love, and it is measured precisely where it is least convenient: in how you deal with people who are not from your group.",
  "77": "'Indeed, those who trade Allah's covenant and their oaths for a fleeting gain will have no share in the Hereafter. Allah will neither speak to them, nor look at them, nor purify them on the Day of Judgment. And they will suffer a painful punishment.' (Quran 3:77). Perhaps the heaviest warning in the surah, and it is about small profits.\n\n'A fleeting gain' — thamanan qalilan, a little price. The Quran does not say they sold their covenant for a fortune. The point is that any worldly price is small compared to what is given up.\n\nThree deprivations follow, and they are worse than the punishment that comes after them. Allah will not speak to them — no address at all. He will not look at them — no attention, not even the attention of anger. He will not purify them — no cleansing, so they carry their record as it stands.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said, \"There are three persons whom Allah will not look at on the Day of Resurrection, nor will he purify them and theirs shall be a severe punishment,\" and he named a man who withheld surplus water from travellers, a man who pledged allegiance to a ruler only for worldly benefit, and a man who swore falsely by Allah to sell his goods. Then the Prophet recited this very verse (Sahih al-Bukhari 2358).\n\nLook at that third case. A false oath to close a sale. The transaction might be worth very little, and yet using Allah's name as a tool to gain it lands a person in this verse.\n\nThe same fate is described for those who conceal revelation for gain: 'Allah will neither speak to them on the Day of Judgment, nor will He purify them' (Quran 2:174).",
  "78": "'There are some among them who distort the Book with their tongues to make you think this ˹distortion˺ is from the Book—but it is not what the Book says. They say, \"It is from Allah\"—but it is not from Allah. And ˹so˺ they attribute lies to Allah knowingly.' (Quran 3:78). A specific technique of religious deception, described exactly.\n\n'Distort the Book with their tongues' — yalwuna alsinatahum bi al-kitab. The verb 'lawa' means to twist or bend. It describes reciting in a way that changes the sense: altering a vowel, shifting an emphasis, running one phrase into another, so that what the audience hears is not what the text says. Nothing is written down and nothing can easily be checked, because the manipulation happens in the delivery.\n\nThe verse then breaks the deception into three lies stated in sequence: they make you think it is from the Book, and it is not; they say it is from Allah, and it is not; and they know it. The repetition is deliberate, cornering the act from three directions.\n\nThe Quran describes the same behaviour elsewhere: 'a group of them would hear the word of Allah then knowingly corrupt it after understanding it' (Quran 2:75), and 'They distorted the words of the Scripture and neglected a portion of what they had been commanded to uphold' (Quran 5:13).\n\nThis is why Muslims have been so exacting about chains of transmission, exact wording, and preserving the Quran by memorisation as well as in writing. A text that lives in thousands of memories cannot be bent in the reciting without someone noticing.\n\nFor a reader, the safeguard is simple. When someone tells you what Allah said, ask where. A claim about revelation should always come with an address.",
  "79": "'It is not appropriate for someone who Allah has blessed with the Scripture, wisdom, and prophethood to say to people, \"Worship me instead of Allah.\" Rather, he would say, \"Be devoted to the worship of your Lord ˹alone˺\"—in accordance with what these prophets read in the Scripture and what they taught.' (Quran 3:79). The verse defends the prophets against their own followers.\n\nThe claim being answered is that 'Isa asked to be worshipped. The Quran's response is that this is not something a prophet does. The gifts he was given — scripture, wisdom, prophethood — are precisely what would make such a demand impossible. The more a man knows Allah, the less he could imagine standing in His place.\n\n'Isa will say as much on the Day of Judgment: 'Did you ever ask the people to worship you and your mother as gods besides Allah?\" He will answer, \"Glory be to You! How could I ever say what I had no right to say?\"' (Quran 5:116).\n\nWhat a prophet actually says is then quoted: 'Be devoted to the worship of your Lord alone' — kunu rabbaniyyin. A rabbani is someone shaped by his Lord, whose life is organised around Him. And the verse gives the means: 'in accordance with what these prophets read in the Scripture and what they taught' — through teaching the Book and studying it. That is the prophetic method. Not personality, not spectacle. Text, taught and learned.\n\nThere is a warning for religious teachers in every generation. The line between guiding people to Allah and gathering people around yourself is thinner than it looks, and it is crossed gradually — by accepting devotion that should have been redirected, by allowing your opinion to function as revelation.",
  "80": "'And he would never ask you to take angels and prophets as lords. Would he ask you to disbelieve after you have submitted?' (Quran 3:80). The argument of 3:79 is extended from a prophet's own status to everyone else's.\n\nTwo categories are named: angels and prophets. Both were worshipped by different peoples. Some Arabs called the angels daughters of Allah; some communities had elevated their prophets. The verse rules out both at once. Whatever their rank, they are servants.\n\n'Lords' — arbab, plural of rabb. A rabb is one who owns, sustains and commands. The Quran uses this word carefully, because lordship can be given away without anyone intending worship. It describes those who let their scholars define right and wrong on their own authority as having taken them as lords: 'They have taken their rabbis and monks as well as the Messiah, son of Mary, as lords besides Allah' (Quran 9:31). That was the third clause of the invitation in 'nor take one another as lords instead of Allah' (Quran 3:64).\n\nThen the closing question: 'Would he ask you to disbelieve after you have submitted?' Note the phrasing. Worshipping a prophet is not described as excessive devotion or misplaced enthusiasm. It is called disbelief — and disbelief after submission, which is worse than never having submitted.\n\nThe heart of the matter is this. Loving the Prophet, peace be upon him, is required; praising him is right; following him is the proof of loving Allah (Quran 3:31). But the moment honour turns into worship, the very thing he came to abolish has been rebuilt using his name.",
  "81": "'˹Remember˺ when Allah made a covenant with the prophets, ˹saying,˺ \"Now that I have given you the Book and wisdom, if there comes to you a messenger confirming what you have, you must believe in him and support him.\" He added, \"Do you affirm this covenant and accept this commitment?\" They said, \"Yes, we do.\" Allah said, \"Then bear witness, and I too am a Witness.\"' (Quran 3:81). A covenant taken from the prophets themselves, and it settles the question of who owes allegiance to whom.\n\nEvery prophet was given scripture and wisdom, and every one of them accepted this condition: if a later messenger comes confirming what you have, believe in him and support him. The commitment was taken from the leaders, not the followers.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, quotes 'Ali and Ibn 'Abbas as saying that Allah never sent a prophet without taking this pledge from him, and that if the final Messenger were sent in his lifetime, he would be obliged to believe in him and follow him.\n\nThink about what that means for the followers. If Musa and 'Isa themselves were bound to believe in and support Muhammad, peace be upon them all, then their communities cannot claim loyalty to them as a reason for refusing him. Following a prophet includes following him in this.\n\n'Isa announced it explicitly: 'giving good news of a messenger after me whose name will be Ahmad' (Quran 61:6).\n\nNotice the formality of the exchange. A question is asked — do you affirm and accept? An answer is given — yes. Then witnesses are called, and Allah declares Himself one of them. This is the language of a binding contract, recorded so that nobody can later say the terms were unclear.",
  "82": "'Whoever turns back after this, they will be the rebellious.' (Quran 3:82). One line, and it closes the covenant of 3:81 with its consequence.\n\n'Whoever turns back after this' — fa man tawalla ba'da dhalik. After the covenant was explained, affirmed and witnessed. Turning away now is not a misunderstanding; the terms were confirmed by the parties themselves.\n\n'The rebellious' — al-fasiqun. A fasiq is someone who steps outside the boundary. The Arabs used the word for a date that breaks out of its skin. It describes a person who was inside an arrangement and has broken out of it, which is exactly the situation here: not an outsider who never agreed, but a party to an agreement who walked away.\n\nThis is the third time the surah has used the phrase 'if they turn away' at the end of a completed argument (Quran 3:20, Quran 3:32, Quran 3:63). Each time, the case is put fully and then the outcome is handed to Allah.\n\nThe shortness of the verse is part of its force. After the long, formal ceremony of the previous verse, with its question, its answer and its witnesses, this reads like the final clause of a contract. Nothing more needs to be said.\n\nFor the reader, the principle transfers. Every Muslim has affirmed something similar — in the shahadah, and in the ancient covenant the Quran says was taken from all of Adam's descendants. Faith is not only a belief we hold but a commitment we entered, and this verse is a reminder that agreements have a second half: keeping them.",
  "83": "'Do they desire a way other than Allah's—knowing that all those in the heavens and the earth submit to His Will, willingly or unwillingly, and to Him they will ˹all˺ be returned?' (Quran 3:83). A question that exposes the strangeness of rejecting Islam, once islam is properly understood.\n\nThe whole of creation already submits. Planets keep their orbits, cells divide, the seasons turn, and none of it consults its own preference. The Quran states this repeatedly: 'To Allah ˹alone˺ bow down ˹in submission˺ all those in the heavens and the earth—willingly or unwillingly—as do their shadows, morning and evening' (Quran 13:15). Even the heavens were addressed this way at the beginning: 'Submit, willingly or unwillingly.' They both responded, 'We submit willingly' (Quran 41:11).\n\nSo submission is not a strange demand made of human beings. It is the normal condition of everything that exists. What is unusual about us is only that we were given a choice about it.\n\nThat is what 'willingly or unwillingly' means for us. Your body already submits — you did not choose your heartbeat, your ageing or your death. The question is only whether your will joins what your body is already doing. Al-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes that the unwilling submission includes what happens to a person by force of Allah's decree, whether he likes it or not.\n\n'And to Him they will all be returned' closes the argument. Submission now is voluntary; the return is not optional for anyone.\n\nSeen this way, a Muslim is not someone who has taken on a burden the rest of the world escaped. He is someone who has aligned his choices with what the rest of the universe was already doing.",
  "84": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"We believe in Allah and what has been revealed to us and what was revealed to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and his descendants; and what was given to Moses, Jesus, and other prophets from their Lord—we make no distinction between any of them, and to Him we ˹fully˺ submit.\"' (Quran 3:84). The Muslim creed stated in full, and it is remarkably wide.\n\nThe list moves through the whole prophetic line: Ibrahim, Isma'il, Ishaq, Ya'qub, the tribes, Musa, 'Isa, and the rest. A Muslim's faith includes all of them. This is nearly identical to the declaration in Surah al-Baqarah (Quran 2:136), and to the believers' statement in 'We make no distinction between any of His messengers' (Quran 2:285).\n\n'We make no distinction between any of them' — la nufarriqu bayna ahadin minhum. This does not mean the prophets are equal in rank; the Quran states elsewhere that some were favoured over others. It means we do not accept some and reject others. Faith is not a menu. The Quran is blunt about the alternative: 'those who deny Allah and His messengers and wish to make a distinction between Allah and His messengers, saying, \"We believe in some and disbelieve in others,\" desiring to forge a compromise, they are indeed the true disbelievers' (Quran 4:150-151).\n\nThis is why the Muslim position is at once the most inclusive and the most demanding. We affirm every prophet, honour every revelation, and refuse to shorten the list at either end. That is what makes rejecting the final Messenger inconsistent for anyone who accepts the earlier ones.\n\n'And to Him we fully submit' ends the declaration where it must end: not in a claim about history, but in an act of surrender.",
  "85": "'Whoever seeks a way other than Islam, it will never be accepted from them, and in the Hereafter they will be among the losers.' (Quran 3:85). The conclusion of the argument that began at 3:83, and it must be read with what came before it.\n\nThe word is 'islam', and the previous verses have defined it carefully. It is what the whole universe does (3:83). It is what Ibrahim did before the Torah or Gospel existed (3:67). It is the faith that affirms every prophet and rejects none (3:84). It is 'Allah's only Way' (Quran 3:19). Read in that context, this verse is not a sectarian claim but a statement about the one thing Allah accepts: submission to Him alone, on His terms, through His final messenger.\n\n'It will never be accepted from them' — lan yuqbala minh. This is about acceptance, not effort. A person may be sincere, disciplined and devoted, and still be offering something other than what was asked for. Sincerity determines whether an act is honest; revelation determines whether it is correct.\n\n'Among the losers' — al-khasirin. The word describes someone who has spent capital and got nothing back. It is the same verdict given to those 'whose deeds are wasted in this world and the Hereafter' (Quran 3:22).\n\nTwo things follow for a Muslim. First, take your own submission seriously; membership in the community is not the same as the thing the verse names. Second, this is a matter for Allah's judgment of individuals, not for our verdicts about them. Our task is to convey clearly and kindly, as 3:20 instructed, and to leave the reckoning where it belongs.",
  "86": "'How will Allah guide a people who chose to disbelieve after they had believed, acknowledged the Messenger to be true, and received clear proofs? For Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people.' (Quran 3:86). The question is rhetorical, and the three clauses in it explain why.\n\nLook at what these people had. They believed. They testified that the Messenger was truthful. And they received clear proofs — bayyinat, plain evidence. This is not a case of insufficient information. Every input that produces faith had already been supplied and had already worked.\n\nSo the question 'how will Allah guide them?' is not about Allah's ability. It is about the pointlessness of repeating a process that has already run to completion. Guidance means being shown the way. They were shown, they saw, and they turned around. There is nothing further to show.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, connects it to individuals in Madinah who accepted Islam and then abandoned it, and notes that the verse is followed immediately by the door being reopened in 3:89.\n\n'Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people' names the barrier as wrongdoing, zulm, not ignorance. The problem is in the will, not the mind.\n\nThe Quran gives the mechanism elsewhere: 'Indeed, those who relapse ˹into disbelief˺ after ˹true˺ guidance has become clear to them, ˹it is˺ Satan ˹that˺ has tempted them, luring them with false hopes' (Quran 47:25). False hopes — the promise that leaving will make life easier — are usually what does it, rather than a counter-argument.\n\nThe believer's protection is the prayer already taught in this surah: 'Our Lord! Do not let our hearts deviate after you have guided us' (Quran 3:8).",
  "87": "'Their reward is that they will be condemned by Allah, the angels, and all of humanity.' (Quran 3:87). The consequence for those described in 3:86, and the wording is deliberately ironic.\n\n'Their reward' — jaza'uhum. The word normally means recompense or wages. It is used here for a curse, which is the bitter joke of the verse: this is what they earned.\n\nThree sources of condemnation are named, moving outward. Allah, who is the only one whose verdict finally counts. The angels, who witnessed everything and never wavered. And 'all of humanity' — al-nas ajma'in, meaning that on the Day when the truth is plain, no group will defend them. The people they abandoned the faith to please will not stand with them.\n\nThe same triple formula appears in Surah al-Baqarah: 'Surely those who disbelieve and die as disbelievers are condemned by Allah, the angels, and all of humanity' (Quran 2:161), and the verse after it matches 3:88 almost word for word.\n\nThe Arabic for condemnation is 'la'nah', which means being distanced and shut out from mercy. That is the actual content of the curse: not shouting, but exclusion.\n\nIt is worth noticing what these verses do to our sense of proportion. A person who leaves the faith usually does so for something quite small — a relationship, a job, social ease, relief from pressure. The gain is local and temporary. The verse widens the frame to show what is being traded for it.\n\nAnd still, the very next verses will open a door. Read 3:87 alongside 3:89 and the balance of the Quran becomes clear.",
  "88": "'They will be in Hell forever. Their punishment will not be lightened, nor will they be delayed ˹from it˺.' (Quran 3:88). Three closures, stated one after the other.\n\n'Forever' — khalidina fiha. No end point.\n\n'Their punishment will not be lightened' — la yukhaffafu 'anhum al-'adhab. No reduction over time. Human punishments tend to ease as the offence recedes into the past; this one does not.\n\n'Nor will they be delayed' — wa la hum yunzarun. The Arabic 'nazara' here means to be granted a respite, a postponement. In this world, delay is the great mercy we all rely on. Every sin any of us has repented from was possible only because the consequence was postponed long enough for us to change. This verse describes the state in which that postponement has run out.\n\nThe identical sentence appears in Surah al-Baqarah about the same category of people (Quran 2:162), and the repetition across surahs is a form of emphasis.\n\nIt is important to place this verse in its sequence. It comes directly before the verse of forgiveness, and the two must be read together. The Quran does not present hopelessness. It presents a deadline. Everything described here is what happens after the opportunity closes, and the reader holding the Book still has it open.\n\nThat is the intended effect. Not despair, but urgency. The respite we currently have — the fact that nothing is settled yet — is itself the mercy the verse describes being withdrawn.",
  "89": "'As for those who repent afterwards and mend their ways, then surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 3:89). After three verses of the heaviest warnings in the surah, the door opens.\n\nThe placement is the message. Allah describes eternal punishment, unlightened and undelayed, and then immediately says: unless you turn back. Nothing in the previous verses was a sentence already passed. It was a description of where a road ends if you stay on it.\n\nTwo conditions are named, and both are required. 'Repent' — tabu, turned back, which is inward: regret, and a decision to stop. 'And mend their ways' — wa aslahu, repaired what was damaged, which is outward: changed behaviour, and where possible, wrongs put right. Repentance that never reaches action is a feeling, and reform without regret is a resolution. The Quran asks for both.\n\n'Afterwards' — min ba'di dhalik. After all of it. After believing, disbelieving, testifying and turning away. The offence described in 3:86 was as serious as it gets, and the door is still open.\n\nThere is a limit, and the Quran states it plainly: 'repentance is not accepted from those who knowingly persist in sin until they start dying, and then cry, \"Now I repent!\" nor those who die as disbelievers' (Quran 4:18). The cut-off is death, not the size of the sin.\n\nTwo names close the verse. 'Ghafur' — the One who covers over completely. 'Rahim' — the continually merciful. Not merely a judge willing to acquit, but a Lord inclined to receive.\n\nWhatever a person has done, the calculation is simple: the door closes at death, and it has not closed yet.",
  "90": "'Indeed, those who disbelieve after having believed then increase in disbelief, their repentance will never be accepted. It is they who are astray.' (Quran 3:90). Read straight after 3:89, this verse can be confusing. The key is one phrase.\n\n'Then increase in disbelief' — thumma izdadu kufran. This is not describing someone who left the faith and later wants to return. It describes someone who left and kept going: deeper, harder, adding rejection to rejection with no intention of turning back.\n\nSo how can it say 'their repentance will never be accepted' when the previous verse promised forgiveness to those who repent? Al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, gives the answer accepted by most commentators: this describes those who persist until death, or who offer a repentance that is not real. The word 'repentance' here refers to what such a person produces at the end — the panic of someone who sees the consequence arriving, which the Quran has already ruled out: 'repentance is not accepted from those who knowingly persist in sin until they start dying, and then cry, \"Now I repent!\"' (Quran 4:18).\n\nThe two verses describe two different people. One turns back and reforms while there is still time; the door is open (3:89). The other keeps increasing in rejection until the moment when turning back costs nothing, because the outcome is already visible.\n\n'It is they who are astray' — al-dallun. Not those who lost the road, but those who kept walking after being shown it.\n\nThe practical warning is about delay. Sin has a compounding effect: each refusal makes the next one easier and repentance harder. The time to turn is always now, because the capacity to turn is itself something that wears out.",
  "91": "'Indeed, if each of those who disbelieve then die as disbelievers were to offer a ransom of enough gold to fill the whole world, it would never be accepted from them. It is they who will suffer a painful punishment, and they will have no helpers.' (Quran 3:91). The image is deliberately extreme, and that is what makes the point.\n\n'Enough gold to fill the whole world' — mil'u al-ardi dhahaban. Not a great fortune. The entire earth, converted to gold, offered by one person. It would not be accepted.\n\nThe same picture appears elsewhere: 'even if they were to possess everything in the world twice over ˹and offer it all˺ to ransom themselves from the punishment of the Day of Judgment, it would never be accepted from them' (Quran 5:36), and 'even if they were to possess everything in the world twice over, they would certainly offer it to ransom themselves' (Quran 13:18).\n\nWhy press the image so hard? Because wealth buys almost everything here. It buys lawyers, influence, medical care, second chances, and reputation. A person who has lived inside that system reasonably expects that everything has a price. This verse names the one transaction that cannot be made.\n\n'Then die as disbelievers' — the condition is precise. Not those who disbelieved at some point, but those who died in that state. Everything before death is still negotiable, as 3:89 established.\n\n'And they will have no helpers' repeats the phrase from 3:22 and 3:56. Money is gone, and so is influence.\n\nSet this beside the verse that follows, and the contrast is deliberate: gold offered on that Day is worth nothing, and what you give away today, willingly and for Allah, is worth everything.",
  "92": "'You will never achieve righteousness until you donate some of what you cherish. And whatever you give is certainly well known to Allah.' (Quran 3:92). One of the most demanding verses in the Quran about charity, and one of the most practical.\n\n'Al-birr' — righteousness, goodness in its full sense. The Quran defines it at length in Surah al-Baqarah, and giving is central to that definition: 'who give charity out of their cherished wealth to relatives, orphans, the poor, ˹needy˺ travellers' (Quran 2:177).\n\nThe condition here is specific: 'of what you cherish' — mimma tuhibbun, from what you love. Not from what you have finished with. It is easy to give away the surplus, the outgrown, the slightly broken. That is disposal, not charity. The Quran addresses this directly: 'Do not pick out worthless things for donation, which you yourselves would only accept with closed eyes' (Quran 2:267).\n\nAnas ibn Malik reported that Abu Talhah had more date-palm property than any of the Ansar in Madinah, and his most beloved was Bayruha', facing the Prophet's mosque. When this verse was revealed, Abu Talhah came and said, \"O Allah's Messenger! Allah says: 'By no means shall you attain righteousness, unless you spend of that which you love.' And no doubt, Bayruha' garden is the most beloved of all my property to me. So I want to give it in charity in Allah's Cause.\" The Prophet, peace be upon him, said, \"Bravo! It is useful property,\" and advised him to give it to his relatives, which he did (Sahih al-Bukhari 1461).\n\nNote that the verse says 'some of' — mimma. Not everything you love. Enough that it registers.",
  "93": "'All food was lawful for the children of Israel, except what Israel made unlawful for himself before the Torah was revealed. Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Bring the Torah and read it, if your claims are true.\"' (Quran 3:93). A dispute about dietary law, settled by an invitation to check the text.\n\nThe background, recorded by Ibn Kathir commenting on this verse, is that the Jews of Madinah objected to Muslims eating certain foods, arguing that these had always been forbidden and that Islam was therefore innovating. The Quran's reply has two parts.\n\nFirst, the history. Most restrictions came later. Ya'qub — called Israel here — had personally avoided certain food, before the Torah existed. Some restrictions were then imposed on his descendants as a consequence of their own conduct: 'We forbade the Jews certain foods that had been lawful to them for their wrongdoing' (Quran 4:160), and 'In this way We rewarded them for their violations' (Quran 6:146). So the prohibitions were not part of the original religion of Ibrahim.\n\nSecond, the challenge: 'Bring the Torah and read it.' This is a remarkable thing for a scripture to say. The Quran invites its opponents to open their own book and check. That is the move of a text confident in the facts, and it is a model for how Muslims should argue: point to sources, invite verification, avoid assertion.\n\nThe wider lesson concerns how religious rules accumulate. Restrictions adopted by an individual, or imposed for a specific reason at a specific time, harden over generations into an assumed eternal law. Part of every prophet's work was to separate the two.",
  "94": "'Then whoever still fabricates lies about Allah, they will be the ˹true˺ wrongdoers.' (Quran 3:94). The follow-up to the challenge in 3:93, and it defines when disagreement becomes something worse.\n\n'Then' — min ba'di dhalik, after that. After the invitation to bring the Torah and read it. If someone declines to check the evidence and repeats the claim anyway, the situation has changed. Before the challenge, they might have been mistaken. After it, they are choosing.\n\n'Fabricates lies about Allah' — iftara 'ala Allahi al-kadhib. This is the third time the surah has used this expression (Quran 3:75, Quran 3:78). Making something forbidden that Allah permitted, or permitted that He forbade, and attributing the ruling to Him, is treated as a lie about Allah Himself.\n\n'The true wrongdoers' — al-zalimun. Zulm means putting something where it does not belong. Attributing your own preference to Allah is exactly that: your word placed in His position.\n\nThis should make anyone cautious about how they speak on religious matters. Saying 'this is haram' or 'Allah wants this' is not a casual expression of opinion. It is a report about Allah, and it carries the weight of one.\n\nThe safe practice is the one the Quran itself models here: cite the source. If you know where a ruling comes from, say so. If you do not, say that instead. 'I am not sure' has never harmed anyone's religion, while confident invention is placed in this verse alongside the gravest wrongs in the surah.",
  "95": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Allah has declared the truth. So follow the Way of Abraham, the upright—who was not a polytheist.\"' (Quran 3:95). The dispute over food law ends, and the conversation returns to where the surah has been heading all along.\n\n'Allah has declared the truth' — sadaqa Allah. Allah has spoken truly. It closes the argument by naming the authority behind it.\n\nThen the instruction: 'follow the Way of Abraham' — millata Ibrahima hanifa. Millah means a path or way of life. Not his lineage, which everyone claimed, but his practice.\n\nThis returns to 3:67, where Ibrahim was described in exactly these words — upright and not a polytheist. The surah has now completed a full argument. It began by noting that the disputing parties both claimed Ibrahim (3:65), pointed out that their categories postdated him (3:65), stated what he actually was (3:67), identified his real heirs (3:68), and now issues the command: follow him.\n\nWhy does the Quran keep returning to Ibrahim? Because he is the one figure all three parties in seventh-century Arabia respected — Jews, Christians and the Makkan Arabs alike. Appealing to him is appealing to the common ground the parties themselves acknowledged, exactly as 3:64 did.\n\n'Hanif' carries a quiet challenge, as it did before. It means one who turns away from everything false, and Ibrahim did it while standing alone against his family and his whole society. That is the part of the inheritance nobody was competing for.",
  "96": "'Surely the first House ˹of worship˺ established for humanity is the one at Bakkah—a blessed sanctuary and a guide for ˹all˺ people.' (Quran 3:96). The subject shifts to a physical place, and the claim is about priority.\n\nThe context is a dispute over the direction of prayer. When the qiblah changed from Jerusalem to Makkah, objections followed. The Quran's answer is historical: the Ka'bah is not a later Arab preference but the first house of worship established on earth.\n\nAbu Dharr reported: I said, \"O Allah's Messenger! Which mosque was first built on the surface of the earth?\" He said, \"Al-Masjid-ul-Haram (in Mecca).\" I said, \"Which was built next?\" He replied, \"The mosque of Al-Aqsa (in Jerusalem).\" I said, \"What was the period of construction between the two?\" He said, \"Forty years\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 3366).\n\n'Bakkah' is an old name for Makkah, used only here in the Quran. Al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, records the view of early scholars that Bakkah refers specifically to the area of the House while Makkah names the wider town.\n\nThree descriptions are given. 'Established for humanity' — wudi'a li al-nas — for all people, not one nation. 'Blessed' — mubarakan, a place where good is multiplied. And 'a guide for all people' — hudan li al-'alamin. A building that guides: it fixes the direction of prayer for every Muslim on earth, and it connects them to Ibrahim, who raised its foundations with his son (Quran 2:127).\n\nThe point being made is one of continuity. Muslims did not invent a new centre. They returned to the oldest one.",
  "97": "'In it are clear signs and the standing-place of Abraham. Whoever enters it should be safe. Pilgrimage to this House is an obligation by Allah upon whoever is able among the people. And whoever disbelieves, then surely Allah is not in need of ˹any of His˺ creation.' (Quran 3:97). The description of the House continues, and then a duty is stated.\n\n'Clear signs' — ayatun bayyinat. Among them is the Maqam Ibrahim, the standing-place, where Ibrahim stood while raising the walls, mentioned also in 'take the standing-place of Abraham as a site of prayer' (Quran 2:125).\n\n'Whoever enters it should be safe' describes the sanctuary. Even in pre-Islamic Arabia, when tribal killing was routine, a man could stand in that precinct and not be touched. Allah points to this as a favour that should have produced gratitude: 'Have they not seen how We have made ˹Mecca˺ a safe haven, whereas people ˹all˺ around them are snatched away?' (Quran 29:67).\n\nThen the obligation: 'Pilgrimage to this House is an obligation by Allah upon whoever is able among the people.' Hajj is required once in a lifetime, and the condition is capability — 'man istata'a ilayhi sabila', whoever can find a way to it. Physical health, financial means and a safe route are all part of that, so the duty adjusts to real circumstances.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said, \"Whoever performs Hajj for Allah's pleasure and does not have sexual relations with his wife, and does not do evil or sins then he will return as if he were born anew\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 1521).\n\nThe closing line keeps the direction of benefit clear: 'Allah is not in need of any of His creation.' The pilgrimage is for us.",
  "98": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"O People of the Book! Why do you deny the revelations of Allah, when Allah is a Witness to what you do?\"' (Quran 3:98). The surah returns to the same question it asked in 3:70, and the repetition is the point.\n\nOnce again it is framed as a question rather than an accusation. 'Lima takfuruna' — why do you reject? The Quran keeps asking because there is no answer that survives being spoken aloud. Any honest reply would have to name a motive: status, income, group loyalty, or simple reluctance to admit being wrong.\n\nWhat is added this time is the second clause: 'when Allah is a Witness to what you do'. In 3:70 the emphasis was on their own testimony — they knew and rejected anyway. Here the emphasis shifts to Allah's testimony. Even if they never admit what they know, there is a Witness who does not need their admission.\n\n'Shahidun 'ala ma ta'malun' — a Witness over what you do. Not only what you say, but what you do, which includes the private arrangements that made rejection attractive.\n\nThis is the surah's recurring theme applied once more: 'Whether you conceal what is in your hearts or reveal it, it is known to Allah' (Quran 3:29).\n\nThere is a general principle here worth extracting. When someone resists a truth they clearly understand, arguing harder rarely helps, because the obstacle is not in the argument. The Quran's method is to state the case, ask the question, and then name the Witness — leaving the person alone with what they already know.",
  "99": "'Say, \"O People of the Book! Why do you turn the believers away from the Way of Allah—striving to make it ˹appear˺ crooked, while you are witnesses ˹to its truth˺? And Allah is never unaware of what you do.\"' (Quran 3:99). The charge escalates from rejecting to obstructing.\n\nIn 3:98 the accusation was denial, which harms the one who denies. Here it is 'turning away' — tasudduna, actively blocking others. That is a different order of offence, because the damage lands on people who might otherwise have been guided.\n\n'Striving to make it appear crooked' — tabghunaha 'iwaja. The Arabic word 'iwaj' means a bend or a warp. They could not make the path crooked, so they worked to make it look crooked: raising doubts, highlighting difficulties, presenting the religion in its least attractive form. Nothing they said needed to be an outright lie; the technique of mixing and hiding was already described in 3:71.\n\n'While you are witnesses' repeats the aggravating factor for the third time in this section (3:70, 3:98, 3:99). They were not confused people spreading confusion. They knew.\n\n'And Allah is never unaware of what you do' — bi ghafilin 'amma ta'malun. Ghaflah is inattention, the state in which things slip past unnoticed. It is emphatically denied of Allah.\n\nThe lesson transfers directly. Presenting Islam in a way that repels people — harshly, selectively, with the hardest rulings first and none of the mercy — is a form of making the path look crooked. Anyone who conveys the religion carries responsibility not only for accuracy but for the impression they leave.",
  "100": "'O believers! If you were to yield to a group of those who were given the Scripture, they would turn you back from belief to disbelief.' (Quran 3:100). The address changes. For many verses the Quran has been speaking to the People of the Book; now it turns to the Muslims and warns them.\n\n'If you were to yield' — in tuti'u, if you obey. This is not about conversation, trade or neighbourliness, all of which the Quran permits and regulates. It is about obedience: letting another party's judgment override revelation in your own life.\n\n'A group of' — fariqan — again limits the warning. Not all of them, as the surah will shortly make explicit: 'Yet they are not all alike' (Quran 3:113).\n\nThe mechanism has already been described in this passage. The tactic of public conversion and public abandonment (3:72), the insistence that guidance belongs only to one's own group (3:73), and the effort to make the path look crooked (3:99) were all aimed at exactly this result.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records an incident behind these verses: a man reminded the Aws and Khazraj of their old wars with one another until the two tribes nearly came to blows again, and the Prophet, peace be upon him, intervened. That context explains why the very next verses speak of holding fast and remembering how enmity became brotherhood.\n\nThe warning is not to be suspicious of everyone. It is to notice where your standards are actually coming from. A believer can drift a long way without ever making a decision to leave, simply by adopting, one opinion at a time, a way of judging things that is not his own.",
  "101": "'How can you disbelieve when Allah's revelations are recited to you and His Messenger is in your midst? Whoever holds firmly to Allah is surely guided to the Straight Path.' (Quran 3:101). A question to the first Muslim community, and then a principle for everyone after them.\n\nThe question points to two extraordinary advantages they had. The Quran was being revealed and recited among them, and the Messenger himself was there to be asked. With those two things present, drifting into disbelief would be strange indeed.\n\nBut those advantages were temporary, and the second half of the verse supplies what is permanent: 'Whoever holds firmly to Allah' — man ya'tasim billah. The verb 'i'tasama' means to grip something tightly for protection, the way you hold a rope when the ground gives way. It is the same verb used in the next verse about the rope of Allah.\n\nNotice the object of the holding. Not a place, a generation or a teacher, but Allah. That is available in every century.\n\n'Is surely guided to the Straight Path' — the outcome is stated as a certainty, using a construction of emphasis. Nobody who genuinely holds on is left to find the way alone.\n\nThere is an honest observation buried in the question. Physical proximity to revelation does not guarantee faith; people lived beside the Prophet and still fell away. And its absence does not prevent faith; Muslims who never saw him have held on for fourteen centuries. What decides the matter is not the era you were born into but whether you grip.",
  "102": "'O believers! Be mindful of Allah in the way He deserves, and do not die except in ˹a state of full˺ submission ˹to Him˺.' (Quran 3:102). A short command with a very high standard.\n\n'Be mindful of Allah in the way He deserves' — ittaqu Allaha haqqa tuqatih, the taqwa that is His right. Not the minimum that clears the requirement. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, quotes Ibn Mas'ud's explanation of what it means: that Allah be obeyed and not disobeyed, remembered and not forgotten, thanked and not shown ingratitude.\n\nThat is a ceiling no one reaches, and the Quran acknowledges it elsewhere with a merciful clarification: 'So be mindful of Allah to the best of your ability' (Quran 64:16). The two verses are not in conflict. One sets the direction, the other sets the pace. You aim at what Allah deserves and you are held to what you can manage.\n\n'And do not die except in a state of full submission' — wa la tamutunna illa wa antum muslimun. Read literally it commands something we do not control: the moment of death. So what is being asked?\n\nSince nobody knows when death will come, the only way to obey is to be in that state continuously. The command about dying is really a command about living. It is the same advice Ya'qub gave his sons: 'Indeed, Allah has chosen for you this faith; so do not die except in ˹a state of full˺ submission' (Quran 2:132).\n\nPractically, this means not postponing repentance and not treating faith as something to get serious about later. Later is not a time anyone has been promised.",
  "103": "'And hold firmly to the rope of Allah and do not be divided. Remember Allah's favour upon you when you were enemies, then He united your hearts, so you—by His grace—became brothers. And you were at the brink of a fiery pit and He saved you from it. This is how Allah makes His revelations clear to you, so that you may be ˹rightly˺ guided.' (Quran 3:103). One of the great verses on Muslim unity.\n\n'The rope of Allah' — habl Allah. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records that the early scholars explained it as the Quran, and also as His covenant. A rope is what you hold in common; several people can grip the same rope and be connected by it without holding one another.\n\n'And do not be divided' is part of the same command, not a separate one. Unity comes from a shared grip on revelation, not from agreeing to overlook it.\n\nThen the reminder, and it was vivid for the first audience. The Aws and Khazraj had fought for generations, most recently at Bu'ath shortly before the Hijrah. Within a few years they were praying shoulder to shoulder. 'He united your hearts' — allafa bayna qulubikum — and the verse insists on the cause: 'by His grace'. No treaty achieved it.\n\n'At the brink of a fiery pit' — shafa hufratin mina al-nar. They were standing on the crumbling edge, one step from falling.\n\nNu'man ibn Bashir reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said, \"The similitude of believers in regard to mutual love, affection, fellow-feeling is that of one body; when any limb of it aches, the whole body aches, because of sleeplessness and fever\" (Sahih Muslim 2586a).",
  "104": "'Let there be a group among you who call ˹others˺ to goodness, encourage what is good, and forbid what is evil—it is they who will be successful.' (Quran 3:104). After the command to unite, the community is given a function.\n\nThree tasks are named. 'Call to goodness' — yad'una ila al-khayr, inviting people to Islam and to what benefits them. 'Encourage what is good' — al-ma'ruf, literally the known, meaning what is recognised as right by revelation and sound nature. 'Forbid what is evil' — al-munkar, literally the rejected.\n\nThe verse says 'a group among you' — ummatun minkum. Scholars have read this two ways: that this duty must be carried out by a specialised, qualified section of the community, and that every believer shares in it at his own level. Both are supported, because the very next section addresses the whole community with the same qualities: 'you encourage good, forbid evil, and believe in Allah' (Quran 3:110).\n\nThe method is graded. Abu Sa'id al-Khudri reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said, \"He who amongst you sees something abominable should modify it with the help of his hand; and if he has not strength enough to do it, then he should do it with his tongue, and if he has not strength enough to do it, (even) then he should (abhor it) from his heart, and that is the least of faith\" (Sahih Muslim 49a).\n\nThat grading is a mercy and a discipline. It means nobody is required to act beyond their capacity or authority, and nobody is entirely excused either.\n\n'It is they who will be successful' — al-muflihun, those who attain what they hoped for.",
  "105": "'And do not be like those who split ˹into sects˺ and differed after clear proofs had come to them. It is they who will suffer a tremendous punishment.' (Quran 3:105). The warning attached to the command to unite in 3:103.\n\n'Do not be like those' — the Quran points at earlier communities and tells Muslims plainly that the same thing can happen to them. There is no immunity clause.\n\n'After clear proofs had come to them' — min ba'di ma ja'ahum al-bayyinat. This is the same diagnosis the surah gave at the beginning: 'Those who were given the Scripture did not dispute ˹among themselves˺ out of mutual envy until knowledge came to them' (Quran 3:19), repeated in 'They did not split ˹into sects˺ out of mutual envy until knowledge came to them' (Quran 42:14).\n\nThe pattern is consistent and uncomfortable. Division does not come from a shortage of evidence. It comes after the evidence arrives, because clarity forces people to take positions, and positions become identities, and identities are defended long after the original question has been settled.\n\nNote carefully what is condemned. Not every difference of opinion; the Quran and Sunnah leave real room for scholarly disagreement, and the Companions differed on many rulings without splitting. What is condemned is 'tafarraqu' — breaking apart into factions, where the group becomes the point.\n\n'Each rejoicing in what they have' (Quran 30:32) describes the mood exactly: satisfaction in belonging rather than in truth.\n\nThe practical measure is simple. Ask whether a disagreement is making you study more carefully or simply making you more attached to your side. One of those leads somewhere; the other is what this verse warns about.",
  "106": "'On that Day some faces will be bright while others gloomy. To the gloomy-faced it will be said, \"Did you disbelieve after having believed? So taste the punishment for your disbelief.\"' (Quran 3:106). The consequences of the previous verses appear on faces.\n\nThe Quran often uses faces to show inner states on that Day, because a face is the one thing a person cannot compose when the truth arrives: 'On that Day ˹some˺ faces will be bright, laughing and rejoicing, while ˹other˺ faces will be dusty, cast in gloom' (Quran 80:38-41).\n\n'Bright' — tabyaddu, whitened, meaning radiant. 'Gloomy' — taswaddu, darkened. The contrast is between light and its absence, which is how the Quran consistently pictures guidance and its loss.\n\nThen a question is put to the darkened faces: 'Did you disbelieve after having believed?' This links back to 3:86 and to the warning of 3:100 about being turned back from belief to disbelief. It also connects to the verse just before it about splitting into sects, which is why some commentators, including al-Qurtubi on this verse, read it as including those whose factionalism carried them out of the faith itself.\n\nThe question is not asked for information. It is the confrontation itself — being made to state out loud what you did.\n\n'So taste the punishment' — the word 'taste', dhuqu, appears often in these passages. It suggests direct, personal experience of something that was previously only described. Everything they had been told about was, until that moment, words.\n\nThe verse is placed here as motivation to hold the rope of 3:103 firmly.",
  "107": "'As for the bright-faced, they will be in Allah's mercy, where they will remain forever.' (Quran 3:107). The other half of 3:106, and it is deliberately shorter.\n\nNotice what is not said. There is no question put to them, no interrogation, no listing of what they did. The darkened faces are asked to account for themselves; the bright faces are simply admitted.\n\n'They will be in Allah's mercy' — fi rahmati Allah. The Quran could have said 'in Paradise', and often does. Here it names mercy as the destination itself. Al-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes that this places the emphasis where it belongs: entry is by Allah's mercy, and Paradise is what that mercy provides.\n\nThat matters because nobody's deeds purchase this. The Prophet, peace be upon him, made the point about himself, saying that even he would not enter by his deeds alone but by Allah's mercy. Deeds are the sign of a direction taken, not the price of admission.\n\n'Where they will remain forever' — khalidun. The word answers the deepest problem with every good thing in this life, which is that it ends. Health ends, company ends, comfort ends. The Quran attaches permanence to this one.\n\nReading 3:106 and 3:107 together, the whole surah's argument is visible in miniature. Two groups, two faces, two outcomes, and the dividing line is exactly what the surah has been discussing since its opening: what you did with the truth once it reached you.",
  "108": "'These are Allah's revelations We recite to you ˹O Prophet˺ in truth. And Allah desires no injustice to ˹His˺ creation.' (Quran 3:108). After describing two very different outcomes, the Quran defends the fairness of the arrangement.\n\n'In truth' — bi al-haqq. The verses are recited truly, and they describe things as they really are. This is not motivational language or a useful fiction to keep people in line.\n\n'Allah desires no injustice to His creation' — wa ma Allahu yuridu zulman li al-'alamin. He does not want it, and He does not do it. The Quran states this in the strongest terms elsewhere: 'Indeed, Allah never wrongs ˹anyone˺—even by an atom's weight. And if it is a good deed, He will multiply it many times over' (Quran 4:40), and 'We will set up the scales of justice on the Day of Judgment, so no soul will be wronged in the least' (Quran 21:47).\n\nWhy is this said here? Because the previous verses described eternal outcomes, and the human mind immediately asks whether that is fair. The answer given is not a long argument but a statement about Allah's own will: injustice is not something He wants.\n\nLook at the asymmetry the Quran describes. Bad deeds are counted exactly once; good deeds are multiplied. Repentance erases what came before. Warnings are sent repeatedly, through messengers, scripture and conscience, and the door stays open until death (Quran 3:89).\n\nGiven all that, whoever ends among the darkened faces of 3:106 arrived there through many refusals, each of which was met with another chance. That is the fairness the verse is asserting.",
  "109": "'To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. And to Allah ˹all˺ matters will be returned ˹for judgment˺.' (Quran 3:109). A verse of ownership and return, closing this section of the surah.\n\nTwo statements, and the second follows from the first. If everything belongs to Allah, then everything eventually goes back to Him. Ownership implies return.\n\n'Whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth' — the phrase is comprehensive, and it appeared earlier in the surah in the context of Allah's knowledge (Quran 3:29). Here the emphasis is on possession. Not merely that He knows about it all, but that all of it is His.\n\nThat reframes everything a person calls his own. Property, position, family, health and time are held, not owned. The word 'my' is a convenience of speech.\n\n'And to Allah all matters will be returned' — ilayhi turja'u al-umur. The Arabic 'al-umur' covers affairs, decisions, situations — everything that happens. Every unresolved matter, every case that never came to court, every question about why something was allowed to happen, goes back to Him for settlement.\n\nPlaced after 3:108's insistence that Allah does no injustice, this is the practical guarantee. There is no jurisdiction outside His, so nothing can be filed away unresolved.\n\nFor someone carrying a grievance, this verse is the place to put it down. The matter is not being ignored. It is on its way back to the One who owns the heavens and the earth, and He has already said He does not wrong anyone by an atom's weight.",
  "110": "'You are the best community ever raised for humanity—you encourage good, forbid evil, and believe in Allah. Had the People of the Book believed, it would have been better for them. Some of them are faithful, but most are rebellious.' (Quran 3:110). A famous verse, and the most misused one in the surah.\n\n'The best community' — khayra ummatin. Read the sentence to its end before feeling flattered, because the status comes with its reason attached: 'ukhrijat li al-nas', raised up for humanity, and then three qualities. Encourage good. Forbid evil. Believe in Allah.\n\nThat is a job description, not a trophy. The status is conditional on the function, and 'Umar ibn al-Khattab is reported to have said that whoever wishes to be of this best community should fulfil the condition Allah attached to it.\n\nNotice the order: the two social duties are listed before belief, though belief is logically first. Al-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, explains that they are mentioned first because they are what distinguishes this community and makes its faith visible in the world. Faith is the root; these are the fruit by which the tree is known.\n\n'Raised for humanity' also sets the direction outward. This community was not brought into being for its own comfort.\n\nThen the balance the surah keeps returning to: 'Some of them are faithful, but most are rebellious.' Even while criticising, the Quran refuses to generalise, and the next verses will praise the sincere among the People of the Book in warm terms (Quran 3:113-115).\n\nRead honestly, this verse is a heavy responsibility. A community that stops calling to good and forbidding evil has not lost a title; it has stopped doing the thing the title described.",
  "111": "'They can never inflict harm on you, except a little annoyance. But if they meet you in battle, they will flee and they will have no helpers.' (Quran 3:111). Reassurance for a community that felt surrounded.\n\n'Except a little annoyance' — illa adhan. The Arabic 'adha' means irritation, the kind of harm that stings without doing lasting damage: insults, mockery, rumours, social pressure. The Quran does not deny that this happens. It puts it in its proper category. The believers were told to expect exactly this: 'you will certainly hear many hurtful words from those who were given the Scripture before you and ˹from˺ the polytheists' (Quran 3:186).\n\nNotice what the verse does. It neither promises a life free of hostility nor treats hostility as a crisis. It resizes it. Words hurt, and words are not the thing that destroys a person's faith.\n\n'If they meet you in battle, they will flee' was a prediction, and it was fulfilled within a few years in Madinah, as Ibn Kathir notes commenting on this verse.\n\n'And they will have no helpers' repeats a refrain that has run through the surah (Quran 3:22, Quran 3:56, Quran 3:91). Opposition to Allah's message eventually stands alone.\n\nThe useful takeaway is about proportion. Believers in every era spend a great deal of energy on being mocked and misrepresented, as though it were an emergency. This verse calls it 'a little annoyance' — real, unpleasant, and survivable. The things that actually endanger faith are named elsewhere in this surah: division (3:103), envy (3:19), and obeying the wrong voices (3:100).",
  "112": "'They will be stricken with disgrace wherever they go, unless they are protected by a covenant with Allah or a treaty with the people. They have invited the displeasure of Allah and have been branded with misery for rejecting Allah's revelations and murdering ˹His˺ prophets unjustly. This is ˹a fair reward˺ for their disobedience and violations.' (Quran 3:112). A verse about consequences, and the exception in it is as important as the rule.\n\n'Disgrace wherever they go' — al-dhillah ayna ma thuqifu. Almost the same words appear in Surah al-Baqarah about the same conduct: 'They were stricken with disgrace and misery, and they invited the displeasure of Allah for rejecting Allah's signs and unjustly killing the prophets' (Quran 2:61).\n\nThen the exception: 'unless they are protected by a covenant with Allah or a treaty with the people' — illa bi hablin mina Allahi wa hablin mina al-nas. A rope from Allah, and a rope from people. The word is the same 'habl' used in 3:103 for the rope of Allah that Muslims must hold.\n\nThis exception matters enormously for how Muslims treat others. The verse itself states that a covenant changes the situation. Anyone under a treaty, a covenant of protection, or a guarantee of safety is secured by it, and Islamic law built extensive rules on exactly this principle. Disgrace is described as the outcome of persistent rejection, not as a licence for believers to impose it.\n\nThe causes are then named precisely: rejecting revelation, killing prophets, disobedience and transgression. These are actions, not identities. This surah has already insisted that the community is not uniform (Quran 3:75), and the very next verse says it again in the clearest terms.",
  "113": "'Yet they are not all alike: there are some among the People of the Book who are upright, who recite Allah's revelations throughout the night, prostrating ˹in prayer˺.' (Quran 3:113). After several verses of criticism, the Quran stops and corrects the impression.\n\n'They are not all alike' — laysu sawa'an. This sentence is a standing instruction on how to speak about other communities. However sharp the criticism of a group's conduct, it may not be turned into a blanket judgment on every individual in it.\n\nThe praise given is specific and warm. 'Upright' — ummatun qa'imah, a community that stands firm. 'Who recite Allah's revelations throughout the night' — during the hours when only sincerity keeps a person awake. 'Prostrating' — wa hum yasjudun.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records that it was revealed about those among the People of the Book who accepted Islam, such as 'Abdullah ibn Salam and others, whose faith some had questioned.\n\nThe Quran says similar things elsewhere: 'There are some among the people of Moses who guide with the truth and establish justice accordingly' (Quran 7:159), and of those who recognise the truth: 'you see their eyes overflowing with tears for recognizing the truth. They say, \"Our Lord! We believe, so count us among the witnesses\"' (Quran 5:83).\n\nThis is an important discipline for Muslims to learn from their own Book. It is easy, in the middle of a real conflict, to let a description of some people harden into a description of all of them. The Quran refuses to do that even about its most persistent opponents, and this verse is where it stops mid-argument to say so.",
  "114": "'They believe in Allah and the Last Day, encourage good and forbid evil, and race with one another in doing good. They are ˹truly˺ among the righteous.' (Quran 3:114). The praise of 3:113 continues, and the qualities listed are striking.\n\nCompare this list with the description of the Muslim community four verses earlier: 'you encourage good, forbid evil, and believe in Allah' (Quran 3:110). The same three qualities, in almost the same words. The Quran deliberately applies to these individuals the very description it gave to the best community.\n\nThat tells us what the title in 3:110 actually measures. It is not a label attached to a group name. It is a description of conduct, and whoever does it is described by it.\n\nOne quality is added here: 'race with one another in doing good' — yusari'una fi al-khayrat. The image is of competition, hurrying to get there first. It appears again later in the surah as a command to the believers: 'hasten towards forgiveness from your Lord' (Quran 3:133).\n\n'They are truly among the righteous' — mina al-salihin. The verse ends by placing them in the category, without qualification or reservation.\n\nThere is a lesson here about how to treat converts and newcomers, which was the original context. The community that has been Muslim longer sometimes holds new believers at arm's length. Allah does the opposite: He lists their qualities, ranks them with the righteous, and in the next verse guarantees their reward.",
  "115": "'They will never be denied the reward for any good they have done. And Allah has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of those mindful ˹of Him˺.' (Quran 3:115). The guarantee attached to the praise in 3:113-114.\n\n'Will never be denied' — fa lan yukfaruh. The verb here is from the same root as kufr, and its root meaning is to cover up. So the sentence literally says their good will not be covered over, hidden, or written off. Nothing gets lost in the accounting.\n\n'Any good they have done' — ma yaf'alu min khayr. Not merely the large public acts. Anything.\n\nThis is the principle the Quran states repeatedly: 'So whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it' (Quran 99:7), and 'those who do good—whether male or female—and have faith will enter Paradise and will never be wronged ˹even as much as˺ the speck on a date stone' (Quran 4:124).\n\n'And Allah has perfect knowledge of those mindful of Him' — 'alimun bi al-muttaqin. He knows who they are. That closes off the human habit of deciding who counts. The community may not recognise a person's sincerity; Allah does, and He is the one keeping the record.\n\nNotice how this section ends. It began by warning against being misled (3:100), moved through the disgrace that follows persistent rejection (3:112), and then turned to praise the sincere among the same community and guarantee their reward. That is the Quran's balance: severe about conduct, precise about individuals, and generous to anyone who turns.",
  "116": "'Indeed, neither the wealth nor children of the disbelievers will be of any benefit to them against Allah. It is they who will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever.' (Quran 3:116). This repeats 3:10 almost word for word, and the repetition is doing work.\n\nThe surah made this point at its opening, and now returns to it after a long argument. In between, we have seen exactly why the reminder is needed. The delegations who disputed with the Prophet had wealth, position and standing. The tribes of Madinah had numbers and alliances. All of it looked like security.\n\n'Wealth and children' remain the two great sources of confidence, then and now: assets and family, savings and successors. Both are genuine blessings, and neither is being condemned. What is denied is their usefulness 'against Allah' — mina Allahi shay'a.\n\n'Residents of the Fire' — ashab al-nar. The word 'ashab' means companions or people of a place, those who belong to it.\n\nPlacing this verse right after the praise of the sincere in 3:113-115 sets up a sharp contrast. Those people had nothing that impressed anyone: no wealth mentioned, no lineage, no standing. They had night prayer and honest conduct, and their reward is guaranteed. These people have everything the world respects, and it will not help.\n\nThe practical question the verse asks is simple. What are you actually relying on? Not what you say you rely on — what you feel relieved about when you think of it. That answer tells you where your security is placed.",
  "117": "'The good they do in this worldly life is like the harvest of an evil people struck by a bitter wind, destroying it ˹completely˺. Allah never wronged them, but they wronged themselves.' (Quran 3:117). A parable, and the details of it are exact.\n\nPicture the scene. A crop has been planted, tended and grown. The work is done and the harvest is nearly in. Then a freezing wind — 'rihun fiha sirr', a wind carrying bitter cold — passes over the field and destroys everything overnight.\n\nNotice what the parable does not say. It does not say they were lazy or that they planted nothing. They worked. The loss is not of effort but of return.\n\nThat is the point about good deeds done without faith. Charity, kindness, public service and generosity are real, and the Quran does not pretend otherwise. But the harvest of a deed is collected in the Hereafter, and a deed disconnected from belief in Allah has no root there. Similar images appear elsewhere: 'The parable of the deeds of those who disbelieve in their Lord is that of ashes fiercely blown away by wind on a stormy day' (Quran 14:18), and the thin soil on rock washed away by rain (Quran 2:264).\n\n'Allah never wronged them, but they wronged themselves' — the same insistence as 3:108. The wind did not fall on an innocent field by accident. The choice that left the crop exposed was theirs.\n\nFor a believer there is a warning here too. Deeds can be lost after they are done — by showing off, by reminders of one's generosity, by pride. The harvest is not safe until it is stored.",
  "118": "'O believers! Do not associate closely with others who would not miss a chance to harm you. Their only desire is to see you suffer. Their prejudice has become evident from what they say—and what their hearts hide is far worse. We have made Our revelations clear to you, if only you understood.' (Quran 3:118). A warning about intimacy, and the key word must be understood precisely.\n\n'Bitanah' — translated 'associate closely'. It comes from 'batn', the inside. A person's bitanah is his inner circle: confidants, advisers, the people who know what he is really thinking and whose counsel shapes his decisions. This is not about ordinary friendship, commerce or neighbourly relations, all of which Islam regulates and encourages fairness in (Quran 60:8).\n\nThe verse describes who is excluded, and the description is behavioural, not communal: those who 'would not miss a chance to harm you' — la ya'lunakum khabala, they spare no effort in corrupting your affairs — and whose 'only desire is to see you suffer'.\n\n'Their prejudice has become evident from what they say' — the hostility has already surfaced in their speech. This is not suspicion of hidden motives; it is a response to expressed ones. 'And what their hearts hide is far worse' adds that the visible part is the smaller half.\n\nThe lesson is about who you let inside. Everyone deals with a wide range of people, and that is normal and required. But the small number of people whose opinion actually moves you is a decision, and this verse says to make it deliberately. Advisers who want you to fail will give advice shaped by that wish, however reasonable it sounds.",
  "119": "'Here you are! You love them but they do not love you, and you believe in all Scriptures. When they meet you they say, \"We believe.\" But when alone, they bite their fingertips in rage. Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"˹May you˺ die of your rage!\" Surely Allah knows best what is ˹hidden˺ in the heart.' (Quran 3:119). The situation described is painfully one-sided.\n\n'You love them but they do not love you' — tuhibbunahum wa la yuhibbunakum. The Muslims were the ones extending goodwill. They had every reason to: 'you believe in all Scriptures', meaning Muslims accept the Torah and the Gospel as revelation, as declared in 3:84. From the Muslim side there was genuine common ground and genuine affection.\n\nIt was not returned. 'When they meet you they say, \"We believe\"' — the public face. 'But when alone, they bite their fingertips in rage' — the private one. Biting the fingertips is the gesture of someone consumed by anger he cannot act on.\n\n'May you die of your rage' is not a curse in the sense of asking for their destruction. Al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, explains it as a statement of futility: their anger will neither change Allah's decree nor stop the growth of Islam, so they can carry it to the grave.\n\n'Allah knows best what is hidden in the heart' — dhat al-sudur, what is inside the chests. The verse ends where the surah always ends these passages.\n\nThe emotional lesson matters. Loving people who do not love you back is not naive, and the verse does not tell the believers to stop being decent. It tells them to stop being unaware. Keep your goodwill; adjust your expectations.",
  "120": "'When you ˹believers˺ are touched with good, they grieve; but when you are afflicted with evil, they rejoice. ˹Yet,˺ if you are patient and mindful ˹of Allah˺, their schemes will not harm you in the least. Surely Allah is Fully Aware of what they do.' (Quran 3:120). The diagnosis is completed, and then the remedy is given in five words.\n\nThe first half describes envy in its purest form. Your good news causes them pain; your setback gives them pleasure. Note that this is a description of a heart's condition, not of any action taken. Nothing has been done yet.\n\nThen the protection: 'if you are patient and mindful of Allah, their schemes will not harm you in the least' — in tasbiru wa tattaqu la yadurrukum kayduhum shay'a.\n\nTwo things, and neither is what a person would expect. Not counter-strategy, not exposure, not retaliation. Sabr — patience, holding steady, not being provoked into reacting. And taqwa — staying conscious of Allah, which mainly means not letting their behaviour push you into wrong behaviour.\n\nWhy does this work? Because most schemes need a reaction to succeed. Provocation only functions if the target is provoked. A believer who absorbs the hostility without changing his conduct removes the mechanism.\n\nThe promise is unusually absolute: 'la yadurrukum... shay'a' — will not harm you at all. The same pairing is offered later in the surah: 'if you are patient and mindful ˹of Allah˺—surely this is a resolve to aspire to' (Quran 3:186).\n\nAnd 'Allah is Fully Aware of what they do' means the response is not your job. It is being handled by Someone with better information.",
  "121": "'˹Remember, O Prophet,˺ when you left your home in the early morning to position the believers in the battlefield. And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.' (Quran 3:121). The surah now turns to the Battle of Uhud, and it will spend some sixty verses on it.\n\nThat is remarkable in itself. Uhud was a defeat. The Muslims lost seventy men, including the Prophet's uncle Hamzah; the Prophet, peace be upon him, was wounded, and a rumour spread that he had been killed. The Quran does not pass over it quickly. It examines it in detail, publicly, and names the mistakes.\n\nNo scripture edited by its own community would handle its worst day this way. The honesty is itself an argument.\n\n'When you left your home in the early morning' — ghadawta min ahlik. The detail is domestic and human: a man leaving his house at dawn. 'To position the believers in the battlefield' — tubawwi'u al-mu'minina maqa'ida li al-qital, assigning each group its station. The Prophet planned carefully, placed his archers on the hill, and gave them clear instructions. Preparation and trust in Allah were never alternatives.\n\n'And Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing' closes the verse. He heard the debate in Madinah about whether to fight inside the city or march out. He knew what was in every heart on that road, including the ones who would turn back.\n\nThe Quran begins its account of the defeat by reminding everyone who was watching and listening the whole time.",
  "122": "'˹Remember˺ when two groups among you ˹believers˺ were about to cower, then Allah reassured them. So in Allah let the believers put their trust.' (Quran 3:122). A moment of near-collapse, recorded permanently.\n\nThe two groups were the clans of Banu Salamah from the Khazraj and Banu Harithah from the Aws, as Ibn Kathir records commenting on this verse. On the march to Uhud, 'Abdullah ibn Ubayy turned back with about three hundred men — roughly a third of the army — and these two clans wavered, almost following him.\n\nThey did not. 'Then Allah reassured them' — wallahu waliyyuhuma, Allah was their protector. He steadied them, and they went on to fight.\n\nWhat is striking is that Allah recorded the wavering at all. Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, whose own clan was one of the two, is reported to have said he would not have wished the verse removed, because of the words 'Allah was their protector'. The moment of weakness is preserved, and so is the rescue.\n\nThis is a mercy for every believer who has felt his own resolve slipping. Faith is not the absence of that feeling. These men were named in the Quran as almost failing, and they are still counted among the believers who fought at Uhud.\n\n'So in Allah let the believers put their trust' — the conclusion drawn. Not: be stronger next time. Rely on Him, because what steadied those two clans was not their own resolve. It was His protection, and it is available to anyone who leans on it rather than on his own nerve.",
  "123": "'Indeed, Allah made you victorious at Badr when you were ˹vastly˺ outnumbered. So be mindful of Allah, perhaps you will be grateful.' (Quran 3:123). Before analysing the defeat, the Quran recalls the victory.\n\nBadr had happened a year earlier: about three hundred believers, poorly equipped, against roughly a thousand of Quraysh. The word translated 'vastly outnumbered' is 'adhillah', which means weak and lowly — the Quran does not flatter their condition. They won anyway, and the surah has already called it 'a sign' (Quran 3:13).\n\nWhy raise Badr here? Two reasons. First, because the contrast explains Uhud. At Badr they had nothing and relied entirely on Allah. At Uhud they were better armed, more numerous and confident, and the archers left their posts for the spoils. The difference between the two days was not in equipment.\n\nSecond, because remembering past help is how a person survives present difficulty. The Quran repeatedly directs believers to look back at what Allah has already done. Faith is not sustained by feelings; it is sustained by evidence, and everyone has some.\n\n'So be mindful of Allah, perhaps you will be grateful' — the response asked for is taqwa, and its fruit is gratitude. Note the order. Usually we think gratitude produces good conduct. Here consciousness of Allah produces gratitude, because a person who is paying attention notices what he has been given.\n\nThe whole Uhud passage is framed by this verse. Before a word of criticism, the community is reminded that they have seen what Allah can do.",
  "124": "'˹Remember, O Prophet,˺ when you said to the believers, \"Is it not enough that your Lord will send down a reinforcement of three thousand angels for your aid?\"' (Quran 3:124). The reminder of Badr continues, and now the invisible half of that day is described.\n\nThe question is rhetorical and slightly reproachful in tone: is it not enough? It was addressed to men counting the enemy and finding the arithmetic against them. The Prophet's answer was to point at a resource they could not count.\n\nThree thousand angels for three hundred men. The proportion is not accidental. Where they were outnumbered three to one, the support was ten to one.\n\nSurah al-Anfal describes the same event from Allah's side: '˹Remember˺ when you cried out to your Lord for help, He answered, \"I will reinforce you with a thousand angels—followed by many others\"' (Quran 8:9), and 'when your Lord revealed to the angels, \"I am with you. So make the believers stand firm\"' (Quran 8:12).\n\nThat last line is worth pausing on. One task given to the angels was to steady the hearts of the believers. Some of the help was in the fighting; some of it was in the confidence.\n\nAl-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes the difference of opinion among the early scholars about whether this promise applied to Badr or was offered at Uhud on a condition that was not met. Either way, the lesson the surah is drawing is the same: the count you can see is never the whole count.",
  "125": "'Most certainly, if you ˹believers˺ are firm and mindful ˹of Allah˺ and the enemy launches a sudden attack on you, Allah will reinforce you with five thousand angels designated ˹for battle˺.' (Quran 3:125). The promise of 3:124 is raised, and a condition is attached.\n\nThree thousand becomes five thousand. But notice what has been added: 'if you are firm and mindful of Allah' — in tasbiru wa tattaqu. The same two conditions given in 3:120 as protection against schemes. Patience and God-consciousness are the constant currency of this surah.\n\nThat conditionality explains Uhud. The archers on the hill had explicit orders not to leave their position under any circumstances. When the battle appeared won, most of them came down for the spoils, and the cavalry swept around behind. The condition was 'if you are firm', and firmness is exactly what failed.\n\n'Designated for battle' — musawwimin. Commentators, including Ibn Kathir on this verse, explain that the word means marked or distinguished, describing angels bearing a visible sign.\n\nAl-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, records the difference of opinion about whether this five thousand was fulfilled at Badr or offered at Uhud on a condition not met. Either way the principle stands.\n\nThe lesson is about how divine help works. It is real and it is enormous, and it is tied to conduct. Allah's promises to a community are usually conditional promises, and reading only the first half of them has caused Muslims a great deal of confusion about why help sometimes does not come.",
  "126": "'Allah ordained this ˹reinforcement˺ only as good news for you and reassurance for your hearts. And victory comes only from Allah—the Almighty, All-Wise.' (Quran 3:126). The angels are put in perspective.\n\n'Only as good news for you and reassurance for your hearts' — illa bushra lakum wa li tatma'inna qulubukum bih. The reinforcement was not sent because Allah needed the extra numbers. It was sent for the effect on the believers. Allah could have granted victory with no army at all.\n\nSo the angels were, in part, a psychological mercy. Men facing a superior force needed to know they were not alone, and Allah supplied that knowledge in a form they could hold on to. That is a striking thing for Allah to disclose: the help was calibrated to their hearts.\n\n'And victory comes only from Allah' — wa ma al-nasru illa min 'indi Allah. This closes off every other explanation. Not from numbers, not from angels as independent agents, not from strategy or courage. The identical statement is made about Badr in Surah al-Anfal: 'And Allah made this a sign of victory and reassurance to your hearts. Victory comes only from Allah' (Quran 8:10).\n\n'The Almighty, All-Wise' — 'Aziz, Hakim. Power to give victory, wisdom about when to give it. That second name matters most on a day like Uhud, when victory was withheld.\n\nThe practical benefit is freedom from superstition about causes. Prepare fully, then know that preparation is not what decides the outcome. Both halves of that are necessary; either one alone produces a distorted religion.",
  "127": "'to destroy a group of the disbelievers and humble the rest, causing them to withdraw in disappointment.' (Quran 3:127). The purpose of the help described in the previous verses, stated in terms of the enemy.\n\nTwo outcomes are named. 'Destroy a group' — yaqta'a tarafan, literally to cut off an edge or a section. At Badr, the losses fell precisely on the leadership of Quraysh: Abu Jahl and many of the chiefs who had driven the persecution died there. It was not a random reduction in numbers; it was the removal of a specific layer.\n\n'Humble the rest, causing them to withdraw in disappointment' — yakbitahum fa yanqalibu kha'ibin. The word 'kha'ib' describes someone who came expecting something and went home with nothing. They had marched out confident of wiping out a small band of Muslims, and they returned having lost their leaders to that same small band.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, connects it to Badr, where both halves are visible in the historical record.\n\nThere is a pattern here that recurs throughout the Quran's account of conflict. The stated aim is not extermination. It is breaking the capacity and the confidence of an aggressive force, so that the pressure lifts. Verses in Surah al-Baqarah define the limit in the same spirit: fighting continues while aggression continues, and stops when it stops.\n\nRead in sequence, verses 3:124-127 describe help, the reason for it, and its effect. And then the very next verse pulls the Prophet himself back from any assumption about who deserves which outcome.",
  "128": "'You ˹O Prophet˺ have no say in the matter. It is up to Allah to turn to them in mercy or punish them, for indeed they are wrongdoers.' (Quran 3:128). One of the most remarkable verses in the Quran, because of who it is addressed to.\n\nThe occasion is recorded in the hadith collections and by Ibn Kathir commenting on this verse: at Uhud the Prophet, peace be upon him, was wounded — his tooth broken, his face cut — and he began to invoke against specific leaders of Quraysh by name. This verse came down.\n\n'Laysa laka mina al-amri shay'' — you have no part in the matter at all. Not a rebuke for anger, which was entirely justified, but a correction of jurisdiction. The decision about who is forgiven and who is punished is not his to make or to influence.\n\nConsider what follows from that. If the Messenger of Allah, at the moment of his own injury, has no say in the fate of the men who wounded him, then no one else does either. Every declaration by ordinary Muslims about who is destined for Hell falls under this verse.\n\nAnd the sequel proves the point. Several of the men he invoked against — including Abu Sufyan, Khalid ibn al-Walid and 'Amr ibn al-'As — later accepted Islam and served it. Had the invocation been granted, Islam would have lost some of its greatest figures.\n\n'For indeed they are wrongdoers' confirms that the description was accurate. They were wrongdoers. The verse does not dispute the diagnosis; it removes the sentencing power.",
  "129": "'To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. He forgives whoever He wills, and punishes whoever He wills. And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 3:129). The reasoning behind 3:128.\n\nThe first sentence repeats 3:109 exactly. Ownership is stated first because it settles the question of authority. The one who owns is the one who decides.\n\n'He forgives whoever He wills, and punishes whoever He wills' — the two possibilities are named side by side, in that order. Forgiveness first.\n\nThen look at the ending. Two divine names are given, and both are names of mercy: 'Ghafur' and 'Rahim'. The verse mentions punishment but closes with forgiveness. The Quran does this repeatedly, and it is not accidental. It reflects what the Prophet, peace be upon him, reported that Allah said: that His mercy prevails over His wrath.\n\nThat closing is the answer to the Prophet's invocation in the previous verse. He had asked for punishment on named men; the reply reminds him that the One deciding is All-Forgiving, and He may have other plans for those men — as He did.\n\nFor the reader there are two applications. First, hope: whatever your record, the decision rests with a Lord who names Himself by forgiveness. Second, restraint: stop assigning outcomes to other people. The wrongdoer you have written off is under the authority of the same two names, and you have no more information about His decision than you have about your own.",
  "130": "'O believers! Do not consume interest, multiplying it many times over. And be mindful of Allah, so you may prosper.' (Quran 3:130). In the middle of the account of Uhud, a commandment about money. The placement is not random.\n\nThe connection is this. The surah is examining why a battle was lost, and it has just identified the failure: men left their posts for the spoils. Greed lost Uhud. So the passage turns to the most systematic form of financial greed and forbids it.\n\n'Riba' is usually translated interest or usury. Its root meaning is increase — money growing from money, with no trade, risk or work in between. The Quran forbids it comprehensively in Surah al-Baqarah: 'Allah has permitted trading and forbidden interest' (Quran 2:275), and the warning there is the sternest in the Book on any financial matter: 'If you do not, then beware of a war with Allah and His Messenger!' (Quran 2:279).\n\n'Multiplying it many times over' — ad'afan muda'afah — describes the practice as it existed: a debt that doubled at each missed deadline until the debtor was ruined. Scholars have noted that this phrase describes the worst form rather than setting a threshold below which riba becomes acceptable, since the prohibition elsewhere is stated without qualification.\n\nJabir reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, \"cursed the accepter of interest and its payer, and one who records it, and the two witnesses,\" and he said, \"They are all equal\" (Sahih Muslim 1598).\n\n'So you may prosper' — la'allakum tuflihun. Real prosperity is placed on the other side of the prohibition.",
  "131": "'Guard yourselves against the Fire prepared for the disbelievers.' (Quran 3:131). A single line following the prohibition of interest, and the juxtaposition is deliberate.\n\n'Prepared for the disbelievers' — u'iddat li al-kafirin. The believers are being warned about a Fire described as prepared for those who reject faith. Al-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes the force of the warning: a Muslim who takes what Allah has forbidden while knowing the ruling is walking toward a punishment described as belonging to others.\n\nThe word 'prepared' — u'iddat, in the past tense — is worth noticing. It is not something that will be made later. It exists.\n\n'Guard yourselves' — ittaqu. This is the same root as taqwa, and here its literal meaning is visible. Taqwa is not primarily a feeling of piety; it is protection, placing a barrier between yourself and harm. The command in this verse is the plainest illustration of the word in the whole surah.\n\nThe instruction resembles the one in Surah al-Tahrim: 'Protect yourselves and your families from a Fire whose fuel is people and stones' (Quran 66:6). Note that the family is included there. Guarding yourself against something that is forbidden while your household is immersed in it is only half the work.\n\nPlaced where it is, the verse gives the reason to leave riba even when it is profitable, legal and normal in the surrounding economy. The calculation is not between more money and less money. It is between money and this.",
  "132": "'Obey Allah and the Messenger, so you may be shown mercy.' (Quran 3:132). Short, and precisely placed.\n\nAfter a prohibition that costs money (3:130) and a warning about the Fire (3:131), the surah returns to the foundation: obedience. It is the same command given earlier in 'Obey Allah and His Messenger' (Quran 3:32), and it appears again because the Uhud passage is about a failure of exactly this. The archers had an order from the Messenger and they interpreted around it.\n\nThat is worth stating plainly, because it is the lesson of the whole battle. The archers were not disloyal. They were believers who reasoned that the instruction no longer applied since the situation had changed. Their intention was to help. And the disaster followed anyway.\n\nObedience means following the instruction as given, including when your own judgment suggests a better course.\n\n'So you may be shown mercy' — la'allakum turhamun. The motive offered is not fear of punishment but hope of mercy. The Quran pairs obedience with mercy repeatedly: 'establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and obey the Messenger, so you may be shown mercy' (Quran 24:56).\n\nNotice also the pairing of Allah and the Messenger in one command, as in 3:32. The Sunnah is not an optional appendix to the Quran. The order at Uhud came from the Prophet's mouth, not from a revealed verse, and breaking it had the same consequence as breaking a revealed command.",
  "133": "'And hasten towards forgiveness from your Lord and a Paradise as vast as the heavens and the earth, prepared for those mindful ˹of Allah˺.' (Quran 3:133). After the warnings comes an invitation, and the energy of it is completely different.\n\n'Hasten' — sari'u, run, compete, get there first. The Quran uses this language of urgency about good deeds repeatedly: 'compete with one another in doing good' (Quran 2:148), and 'So compete with one another for forgiveness from your Lord and a Paradise as vast as the heavens and the earth' (Quran 57:21).\n\nLook at the order. Forgiveness first, then Paradise. You cannot enter carrying an unresolved record, so the first thing to run toward is being cleared.\n\n'As vast as the heavens and the earth' — 'arduha al-samawatu wa al-ard. If that is the width, the whole of it is beyond imagining. And note the deliberate echo: the Fire in 3:131 was 'prepared', and Paradise here is 'prepared' with the same verb. Both already exist. The question is only which one a person is walking toward.\n\n'For those mindful of Allah' — al-muttaqin. The same word used in 3:131's command to guard yourself. Guarding yourself from the Fire and running toward the Garden are the same movement described from two ends.\n\nThere is something psychologically important in this placement. The surah has just forbidden a lucrative practice and warned of Hell. Then, immediately, it opens a horizon. Islam does not motivate by fear alone; every warning in this surah has been followed by an opening, and the following verses will describe exactly who these people are.",
  "134": "'˹They are˺ those who donate in prosperity and adversity, control their anger, and pardon others. And Allah loves the good-doers.' (Quran 3:134). Paradise was promised to the God-conscious in 3:133; here is the description, and it is entirely about character.\n\n'Donate in prosperity and adversity' — fi al-sarra'i wa al-darra'. Giving when there is plenty, and giving when there is not. The second is the harder one, and it is what makes generosity a trait rather than a by-product of having spare money.\n\n'Control their anger' — al-kazimina al-ghayz. The verb 'kazama' means to swallow something down and hold it in, used in Arabic for a waterskin tied shut when full. This is not about not feeling anger. It is about containing it when it is present and pressing.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said, \"The strong is not the one who overcomes the people by his strength, but the strong is the one who controls himself while in anger\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 6114).\n\n'And pardon others' — al-'afina 'ani al-nas. This is a further step. Swallowing anger means not acting on it; pardoning means releasing the person from the debt. The Quran presents this as optional and superior: 'The reward of an evil deed is its equivalent. But whoever pardons and seeks reconciliation, then their reward is with Allah' (Quran 42:40).\n\n'Allah loves the good-doers' — al-muhsinin. Consider that these three qualities are what earn that description, in a surah full of theology and battle. Generosity, self-control and forgiveness.",
  "135": "'˹They are˺ those who, upon committing an evil deed or wronging themselves, remember Allah and seek forgiveness and do not knowingly persist in sin—and who forgives sins except Allah?' (Quran 3:135). The description of the people of Paradise continues, and here it takes an unexpected turn.\n\nThey sin. That is stated as part of the description, not as an exception to it. The Quran's portrait of the righteous is not of people who never fall; it is of people who respond correctly when they do.\n\nThe sequence is exact. They commit the wrong. They 'remember Allah' — dhakaru Allah — which is the pivot; sin usually happens in a moment of forgetting. They seek forgiveness. And they 'do not knowingly persist' — wa lam yusirru 'ala ma fa'alu wa hum ya'lamun. Persisting is the danger, not falling.\n\nThen the question, and it is one of the most comforting lines in the Quran: 'who forgives sins except Allah?' It is rhetorical, and it works two ways. Nobody else can forgive, so there is no point looking elsewhere; and forgiving is what He does, so there is every reason to come to Him.\n\nThe Quran states it directly elsewhere: 'O My servants who have exceeded the limits against their souls! Do not lose hope in Allah's mercy, for Allah certainly forgives all sins' (Quran 39:53), and 'Whoever commits evil or wrongs themselves then seeks Allah's forgiveness will certainly find Allah All-Forgiving, Most Merciful' (Quran 4:110).\n\nThe word 'knowingly' is the hinge. There is a great difference between a person who falls and gets up and one who has quietly decided that a particular sin is simply part of his life now.",
  "136": "'Their reward is forgiveness from their Lord and Gardens under which rivers flow, staying there forever. How excellent is the reward for those who work ˹righteousness˺!' (Quran 3:136). The promise for the people described in 3:134-135.\n\n'Forgiveness from their Lord' comes first, before any mention of gardens. The order matches 3:133, where hastening was toward forgiveness and then Paradise. This is consistent throughout the Quran: pardon precedes reward, because nothing can be enjoyed while an account is still open.\n\n'Gardens under which rivers flow' — the standard Quranic image, and it meant a great deal to people living in a desert, where flowing water was the difference between life and death, and shade was the most valuable thing a place could offer.\n\n'Staying there forever' — khalidina fiha. Permanence again, answering the one flaw in every good thing here.\n\n'How excellent is the reward for those who work' — wa ni'ma ajru al-'amilin. Note the last word: al-'amilin, those who work, who act. Not the ones who believed in theory or intended well. The surah has just spent two verses describing what that work looks like — giving in hardship, swallowing anger, pardoning people, and turning back quickly after sin. None of it is spectacular. All of it is difficult.\n\nThat is the encouraging part. The reward described here is not attached to extraordinary deeds available to a few. It is attached to ordinary struggles available to everyone, done consistently and for Allah.",
  "137": "'Similar situations came to pass before you, so travel throughout the land and see the fate of the deniers.' (Quran 3:137). After the interlude on character, the surah returns to Uhud, and it opens with perspective.\n\n'Similar situations came to pass before you' — qad khalat min qablikum sunan. The word 'sunan' is the plural of 'sunnah', meaning an established way or pattern. Allah has patterns in how history runs: how truth is opposed, how communities are tested, how arrogance ends. These are not random events but a system operating consistently.\n\nThat reframes a defeat. If there are patterns, then Uhud was not a sign that Allah had abandoned the believers. It was the system working, and the system includes setbacks.\n\n'Travel throughout the land and see' — fa siru fi al-ardi fanzuru. The Quran gives an instruction that is physical and practical: go and look. Ruins were scattered across Arabia and the surrounding regions, and they were evidence available to anyone who walked. The same command appears often: 'Have they not travelled throughout the land to see what was the end of those ˹destroyed˺ before them? They were far superior in might' (Quran 30:9), and 'Say, \"Travel throughout the land and see the fate of the deniers\"' (Quran 6:11).\n\nBut the seeing has to be more than sightseeing: 'Have they not travelled throughout the land so their hearts may reason, and their ears may listen? Indeed, it is not the eyes that are blind, but it is the hearts in the chests that grow blind' (Quran 22:46).",
  "138": "'This is an insight to humanity—a guide and a lesson to the God-fearing.' (Quran 3:138). A comment on the Quran itself, placed between the instruction to study history and the encouragement that follows.\n\nThree descriptions are given, and they are not synonyms.\n\n'An insight to humanity' — bayanun li al-nas, a clear explanation for all people. This is the widest circle. The Quran's statements are available to anyone; its arguments can be examined by anyone; and the historical evidence it points to in 3:137 can be checked by anyone.\n\n'A guide' — hudan. This is narrower. Explanation is offered to everyone, but guidance is received by those who accept it. The distinction was drawn at the very beginning of Surah al-Baqarah, where the Book is 'a guide for those mindful ˹of Allah˺' (Quran 2:2), even though its clarity is public.\n\n'A lesson to the God-fearing' — maw'izatun li al-muttaqin. Narrower still. A maw'izah is an admonition that actually moves someone — the kind of reminder that changes behaviour rather than merely being noted.\n\nSo the three form a funnel. Everyone can read it. Some are guided by it. Fewer are changed by it. The same words fall on all three groups; what differs is the receiving.\n\nThat is worth applying honestly to our own reading. It is entirely possible to finish a page of tafsir better informed and completely unmoved. The verse quietly asks which of the three circles the reader is standing in.",
  "139": "'Do not falter or grieve, for you will have the upper hand, if you are ˹true˺ believers.' (Quran 3:139). The first words spoken directly to a defeated army, and they address the heart before the strategy.\n\nTwo feelings are forbidden. 'Do not falter' — la tahinu, do not go weak, from a root meaning to become feeble or lose resolve. That is about the future: do not let this stop you. 'Do not grieve' — wa la tahzanu. That is about the past: do not be crushed by what already happened.\n\nBoth are natural after a loss like Uhud, where seventy men died and the Prophet himself was wounded. The Quran does not pretend the feelings are unreasonable. It commands the believers not to settle into them.\n\nThen the promise: 'you will have the upper hand' — wa antumu al-a'lawn. Not you were, and not you might be. You are the higher ones. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, explains that this refers to the ultimate outcome and to the elevation of faith itself, which is not measured by the result of a single day.\n\n'If you are true believers' — in kuntum mu'minin. The condition is attached to faith, not to numbers or equipment. That is both the encouragement and the demand.\n\nThe order of these three elements is worth noticing. Allah forbids despair before He explains what went wrong. The analysis comes later in the passage, some of it severe. But first the wounded are told to stand up. Correction is easier to receive from someone who has already told you that you are not finished.",
  "140": "'If you have suffered injuries ˹at Uhud˺, they suffered similarly ˹at Badr˺. We alternate these days ˹of victory and defeat˺ among people so that Allah may reveal the ˹true˺ believers, choose martyrs from among you—and Allah does not like the wrongdoers—' (Quran 3:140). Perspective, then purpose.\n\nFirst the comparison. The enemy had suffered exactly this a year earlier at Badr. Losses are not a verdict on which side Allah favours; both sides had now had a bad day.\n\nThen the principle: 'We alternate these days among people' — tilka al-ayyamu nudawiluha bayna al-nas. The verb comes from 'dawlah', meaning something that circulates, passing from hand to hand. Fortune rotates. No community holds the advantage permanently, and the surah has already stated why: 'You give authority to whoever You please and remove it from who You please' (Quran 3:26).\n\nTwo purposes are then given for the rotation. 'So that Allah may reveal the true believers' — li ya'lama Allahu alladhina amanu. Allah already knows; the point is that it becomes known, made visible in action. Ease conceals the difference between sincere and nominal faith, and hardship exposes it.\n\n'And choose martyrs from among you' — wa yattakhidha minkum shuhada'. Notice the verb: choose, take. The seventy who died at Uhud were not merely casualties of a mistake. They were selected. That reframing must have meant everything to the families who buried them.\n\nThe closing clause, 'Allah does not like the wrongdoers', keeps the balance. Losses being purposeful does not make wrongdoing acceptable.",
  "141": "'and distinguish the ˹true˺ believers and destroy the disbelievers.' (Quran 3:141). The second half of the purpose begun in 3:140, and the imagery in the Arabic is precise.\n\n'Distinguish' translates 'yumahhisa', a word used for refining metal by fire, burning off what is impure so that only the pure remains. That is the function of a hard day. It does not create the difference between sincere and insincere faith; it removes what was never solid, leaving what is.\n\nEvery believer who was at Uhud came out of it knowing something about himself he could not have known before. That knowledge is worth having, and there is no way to obtain it in comfort.\n\n'And destroy the disbelievers' — wa yamhaqa al-kafirin. The verb 'mahaqa' means to wipe out gradually, to erase by degrees. It is the word used for the waning of the moon. So the two verbs describe two processes running at once: the believers being refined by pressure, and the opposition being worn down over time.\n\nThat is a useful way to read the years after Uhud. Quraysh appeared to have won, and within six years they had lost Makkah. Nothing dramatic reversed the situation; it eroded.\n\nThe pairing of these two verbs is the answer to a question every believer asks during a setback: what is this for? The Quran's answer is that the same event is doing two different jobs, and neither of them is visible on the day.",
  "142": "'Do you think you will enter Paradise without Allah proving which of you ˹truly˺ struggled ˹for His cause˺ and patiently endured?' (Quran 3:142). A question that dismantles a very common assumption.\n\nThe assumption is that faith, once declared, is enough. The Quran asks this question repeatedly, in almost identical words: 'Do people think once they say, \"We believe,\" that they will be left without being put to the test?' (Quran 29:2), and 'Do you think you will be admitted into Paradise without being tested like those before you?' (Quran 2:214).\n\nTwo things are named as what the test reveals: those who 'struggled' — jahadu, who exerted themselves — and those who 'patiently endured' — al-sabirin. Effort and endurance. One is about what you do, the other about what you absorb, and most of the religious life requires both.\n\n'Without Allah proving' — wa lamma ya'lami Allah. As in 3:140, the knowledge in question is not new information for Allah but the actualisation of what was potential. A claim untested is only a claim.\n\nThe believers at Uhud had asked for this battle. Many of them had wanted the chance to fight, and 3:143 will point that out directly. They got what they asked for, and it turned out to be harder than the version they had imagined.\n\nThat is the honest content of this verse. Everyone wants the reward, and everyone assumes they would pass the test. Paradise is described as vast as the heavens and the earth (Quran 3:133), and this verse simply asks what a person expects to be admitted on.",
  "143": "'You certainly wished ˹for the opportunity˺ for martyrdom before encountering it, now you have seen it with your own eyes.' (Quran 3:143). A pointed reminder, delivered without cruelty.\n\nThe background explains it. Many of the younger Muslims had missed Badr and heard about the honour of those who fought there. When Quraysh marched on Madinah, these men pushed hard for the army to go out and meet them, against the Prophet's own initial preference to remain in the city and defend it. He agreed to the majority view.\n\nThen they saw what they had asked for: the noise, the wounds, the bodies of men they knew, the mutilation of Hamzah. 'Now you have seen it with your own eyes' — faqad ra'aytumuhu wa antum tanzurun.\n\nThere is no mockery in this. It is the correction of imagination by reality. It is easy to want a hardship you have only pictured, and the picture is always cleaner than the event.\n\nThe Prophet, peace be upon him, taught the balanced attitude directly. 'Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa reported that he stood among the people and said, \"O people! Do not wish to face the enemy (in a battle) and ask Allah to save you (from calamities) but if you should face the enemy, then be patient and let it be known to you that Paradise is under the shades of swords\" (Sahih al-Bukhari 2965, 2966).\n\nBoth halves matter. Do not ask for the trial. If it arrives, hold firm. That is the sober attitude of a believer, as distinct from the enthusiasm that has not met reality yet.",
  "144": "'Muhammad is no more than a messenger; other messengers have gone before him. If he were to die or to be killed, would you regress into disbelief? Those who do so will not harm Allah whatsoever. And Allah will reward those who are grateful.' (Quran 3:144). Revealed about a rumour, and it later saved the community.\n\nDuring Uhud, the cry went up that the Prophet had been killed. It was false, but it broke the army. Some stopped fighting entirely, and a few began to speak of surrendering. The verse addresses that reaction directly.\n\n'Muhammad is no more than a messenger' — the message is delivered by a man, and men die. The mission was never dependent on his survival, because it was never his mission. He is 'the seal of the prophets' (Quran 33:40), and he is also mortal: 'You ˹O Prophet˺ will certainly die, and they will die too' (Quran 39:30).\n\nThe great proof of this verse came eight years later. Abu Bakr, on the day the Prophet actually died, stood before a community in shock — 'Umar among them insisting he was not dead — and said, \"Whoever worshipped Muhammad, then Muhammad is dead, but whoever worshipped Allah, then Allah is Alive and shall never die.\" Then he recited this very verse (Sahih al-Bukhari 3667, 3668). 'Umar later said his legs would not carry him when he heard it, and he realised the Prophet had truly died.\n\n'Would you regress into disbelief?' — the Arabic is literally 'turn back on your heels', the image of a soldier fleeing.\n\nThe permanent lesson: attachment must be to Allah. Teachers, scholars and leaders die.",
  "145": "'No soul can ever die without Allah's Will at the destined time. Those who desire worldly gain, We will let them have it, and those who desire heavenly reward, We will grant it to them. And We will reward those who are grateful.' (Quran 3:145). Two separate consolations in one verse.\n\nThe first is about death, and it is aimed at men who had just watched friends die and were replaying the day in their heads. 'Kitaban mu'ajjala' — a decree with a fixed term. Nobody at Uhud died because of a tactical error, and nobody survived because of good positioning. The appointed time arrived, or it did not.\n\nThe Quran states this elsewhere with the same intent: 'Wherever you may be, death will overcome you—even if you were in fortified towers' (Quran 4:78), and 'The death you are running away from will inevitably come to you' (Quran 62:8).\n\nThis is not fatalism. It does not remove the duty to be careful; the Prophet planned the battle carefully. It removes the torment of thinking that one's own miscalculation controls the length of a life.\n\nThe second consolation sorts the day's participants by intention. 'Those who desire worldly gain, We will let them have it' — the archers who came down for the spoils got the spoils, and that was their return. 'Those who desire heavenly reward, We will grant it to them.' The same battle, the same actions from the outside, two entirely different outcomes, decided by what a man was reaching for. The principle is stated again: 'Whoever desires the harvest of the Hereafter, We will increase their harvest' (Quran 42:20).",
  "146": "'˹Imagine˺ how many devotees fought along with their prophets and never faltered despite whatever ˹losses˺ they suffered in the cause of Allah, nor did they weaken or give in! Allah loves those who persevere.' (Quran 3:146). Encouragement by example, drawn from history.\n\n'Ribbiyyun' — translated 'devotees'. Commentators, including al-Tabari on this verse, explain it as large numbers of devoted followers, men of learning and worship who stood with their prophets.\n\nThree things are said about them, and the sequence describes exactly what happens to a defeated army. They 'never faltered' — ma wahanu, did not lose heart. They did not 'weaken' — ma da'ufu, did not lose strength. And they did not 'give in' — wa ma istakanu, did not submit to the enemy or accept humiliation.\n\nThose are three distinct stages. First the spirit goes, then the effort goes, then the surrender follows. The verse says these people stopped it at the first stage.\n\n'Despite whatever losses they suffered in the cause of Allah' — the losses were real, including the deaths of prophets among them, according to one reading of the verse. This is the point: earlier communities suffered worse than Uhud and kept going.\n\n'Allah loves those who persevere' — al-sabirin. This is the third time in this surah that Allah's love has been attached to a specific quality: to following the Messenger (3:31), to the good-doers (3:134), and now to the patient.\n\nThe message to a bruised community is that they are not the first, their loss is not the largest, and the response that earns Allah's love is simply to continue.",
  "147": "'And all they said was, \"Our Lord! Forgive our sins and excesses, make our steps firm, and grant us victory over the disbelieving people.\"' (Quran 3:147). The prayer of those earlier believers, and its structure is instructive.\n\n'And all they said was' — wa ma kana qawlahum illa an qalu. Notice what is absent. No complaint about the outcome, no blame directed at anyone, no question about why Allah allowed it. Their entire verbal response was a supplication.\n\nThe prayer has three parts, in a deliberate order.\n\nFirst, forgiveness: 'forgive our sins and excesses' — dhunubana wa israfana fi amrina. Two categories are named, sins and going beyond bounds in our affairs. They begin by assuming the fault is theirs. When something goes wrong, the first instinct of these believers is to examine their own record rather than the enemy's strength.\n\nSecond, steadiness: 'make our steps firm' — thabbit aqdamana. Not remove the difficulty. Hold us in place inside it.\n\nThird, and only third, victory. The request they actually wanted is placed last, after purification and stability.\n\nThat order is the lesson. Most of us pray in the reverse: help me win, and if there is time, forgive me. These believers understood that the obstacle to victory might be in themselves, and that a person who cannot stand firm cannot be helped to advance.\n\nAl-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes that they sought forgiveness first because sins are the cause of defeat.",
  "148": "'So Allah gave them the reward of this world and the excellent reward of the Hereafter. For Allah loves the good-doers.' (Quran 3:148). The answer to the prayer of 3:147, and they received more than they asked for.\n\nThey asked for three things: forgiveness, firmness and victory. They were given 'the reward of this world' — thawab al-dunya, which includes the victory they requested — 'and the excellent reward of the Hereafter'. The second was not on their list.\n\nThe Arabic for the second is 'husna thawab al-akhirah', the beautiful reward of the next life. The adjective is attached only to the second, marking the difference in scale between the two.\n\nThere is a principle here worth naming. Those who pray primarily for the Hereafter often receive both; those who pray only for this world receive only what was asked, as 3:145 stated. The order of priorities does not deprive a person of worldly good; it usually secures it.\n\n'For Allah loves the good-doers' — al-muhsinin. The fourth mention of Allah's love in this surah, and here it is attached to ihsan, doing things beautifully and well. Ihsan is more than correctness. It is the quality of a person who does the required thing with full attention, as though he sees Allah.\n\nRead 3:146-148 as a single unit and the message to the survivors of Uhud is complete: others faced worse, they responded with prayer rather than complaint, they asked for forgiveness before victory, and Allah gave them everything. The path is not hidden. It is written out in three verses.",
  "149": "'O believers! If you yield to the disbelievers, they will drag you back to disbelief—and you will become losers.' (Quran 3:149). A warning about the aftermath of defeat, because that is when this danger appears.\n\n'If you yield' — in tuti'u, if you obey. The same construction used in 3:100 about a group of the People of the Book. Here it is about the disbelievers generally, and the context is a demoralised community that has just lost a battle.\n\nThat timing is the point. After a defeat, voices become persuasive that would have been laughed at a month earlier. Perhaps we were wrong. Perhaps we should compromise. Perhaps the old arrangements were better. Defeat does not usually destroy a community's faith directly; it makes the community willing to listen to people who will.\n\n'They will drag you back' — yaruddukum 'ala a'qabikum, turn you back on your heels. It is the same image used in 3:144 about those who nearly abandoned the faith when they heard the Prophet was dead. The Quran uses it for reversal: walking backwards over ground already covered.\n\n'And you will become losers' — khasirin. The word for someone whose transaction has failed, used earlier in 'in the Hereafter they will be among the losers' (Quran 3:85).\n\nNotice the mechanism being described. Nobody is asked to renounce anything. They are asked to obey — to take direction, to accept another party's judgment about what is realistic. The drift back happens afterwards, and by then it feels like a series of reasonable decisions rather than a change of religion.",
  "150": "'But no! Allah is your Guardian, and He is the best Helper.' (Quran 3:150). The correction to 3:149, and it is short because it needs to be.\n\n'Bal' — but no, on the contrary. It cancels the suggestion of the previous verse. If they are not to obey the disbelievers, then whose protection are they under? This.\n\n'Allah is your Guardian' — Allahu mawlakum. The word 'mawla' means patron, protector, the one whose responsibility you are. It is from the same family as 'wali', which the surah used in 3:28 when it forbade taking the hostile as patrons, and again in 3:68: 'Allah is the Guardian of those who believe.'\n\nSo the argument has been completed. Do not take them as protectors, because you already have one.\n\n'And He is the best Helper' — khayru al-nasirin. This is precisely what a defeated army needs to hear. They had just experienced a day when help seemed absent, and here Allah names Himself the best of helpers. The claim is not that a believer never loses. It is that no better ally exists, on any terms, anywhere.\n\nThe two names together — protector and helper — cover both halves of a person's need: someone responsible for you when you are weak, and someone who acts for you when you are struggling.\n\nFor anyone weighing an unwise alliance out of fear, this verse is the whole answer. The compromise being considered is a trade of the best helper for a lesser one, made at a moment when the loss feels temporary and the gain feels urgent.",
  "151": "'We will cast horror into the hearts of the disbelievers for associating ˹false gods˺ with Allah—a practice He has never authorized. The Fire will be their home—what an evil place for the wrongdoers to stay!' (Quran 3:151). A promise fulfilled almost immediately after Uhud.\n\nQuraysh had won the field and then withdrawn without pressing on to Madinah, which was undefended and only a short march away. It was the strangest decision of the campaign. They got partway home, realised what they had left undone, and turned back — and then, according to the historical accounts, lost their nerve again when they learned the Muslims had come out to meet them. 'Cast horror into their hearts' — sanulqi fi qulubi alladhina kafaru al-ru'b — describes exactly what happened.\n\nAllah uses this weapon repeatedly: 'I will cast horror into the hearts of the disbelievers' (Quran 8:12), and about a later siege, 'the decree of Allah came upon them from where they never expected. And He cast horror into their hearts' (Quran 59:2).\n\nThe stated reason is shirk: 'for associating false gods with Allah—a practice He has never authorized.' Al-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, points out the argument buried in the phrase: they worship what Allah sent down no authority for, so at some level they know their gods are unsupported. A person defending a position he cannot justify is never fully secure in it, and that insecurity is where fear enters.\n\nThe encouragement for believers is real. The opposition may look immovable and still be internally fragile, because confidence built on nothing has nothing holding it up.",
  "152": "'Indeed, Allah fulfilled His promise to you when you ˹initially˺ swept them away by His Will, then your courage weakened and you disputed about the command and disobeyed, after Allah had brought victory within your reach. Some of you were after worldly gain while others desired a heavenly reward. He denied you victory over them as a test, yet He has pardoned you. And Allah is Gracious to the believers.' (Quran 3:152). The full account of what happened at Uhud, and it is unsparing.\n\nThe sequence is laid out step by step. 'Allah fulfilled His promise' — the early part of the battle was a rout in the Muslims' favour. Then three failures, in order. 'Your courage weakened' — fashiltum. 'You disputed about the command' — the archers argued among themselves on the hill about whether the order still applied. 'And disobeyed' — most came down.\n\nAnd the timing: 'after Allah had brought victory within your reach' — min ba'di ma arakum ma tuhibbun, after He showed you what you love. The failure came at the moment of success, not under pressure. That is when discipline usually breaks.\n\n'Some of you were after worldly gain while others desired a heavenly reward' — the same division stated in 3:145. About fifty men were on that hill and they split into two groups defined by what they wanted.\n\nThen the verdict, and it is astonishing: 'yet He has pardoned you' — wa laqad 'afa 'ankum. After naming the failure precisely, Allah forgives it.\n\n'And Allah is Gracious to the believers' — dhu fadlin 'ala al-mu'minin. They are still called believers. The mistake was serious, it is recorded permanently in the Quran, and they remain His people.",
  "153": "'˹Remember˺ when you were running far away ˹in panic˺—not looking at anyone—while the Messenger was calling to you from behind! So Allah rewarded your disobedience with distress upon distress. Now, do not grieve over the victory you were denied or the injury you suffered. And Allah is All-Aware of what you do.' (Quran 3:153). The rout described exactly as it happened.\n\n'Running far away—not looking at anyone' — tus'iduna wa la talwuna 'ala ahad. The Arabic 'la talwun' means not turning to look back at anyone. Panic does that: it removes peripheral vision, and a man running does not see his companion fall.\n\n'While the Messenger was calling to you from behind' — this is the detail that hurts. He was the last one facing the enemy, wounded, calling them back by name, and they were ahead of him running.\n\n'Distress upon distress' — ghamman bi ghamm. Grief on top of grief: the defeat itself, the deaths, and then the false report that the Prophet had been killed. Some commentators, including al-Tabari on this verse, read one of the two griefs as the relief that came when they found him alive, which cancelled the earlier sorrow.\n\nThen, remarkably: 'do not grieve over the victory you were denied or the injury you suffered.' The Quran describes the failure in detail and then tells them not to sink under it. This is the pattern of the whole passage — honesty about the mistake, refusal to let the mistake become an identity.\n\n'Allah is All-Aware of what you do' closes it. He saw the running, and He saw who stayed.",
  "154": "'Then after distress, He sent down serenity in the form of drowsiness overcoming some of you, while others were disturbed by evil thoughts about Allah—the thoughts of ˹pre-Islamic˺ ignorance. They ask, \"Do we have a say in the matter?\" Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"All matters are destined by Allah.\"...' (Quran 3:154). A remarkable scene on the battlefield after the fighting stopped.\n\nAllah sent sleep. Men lying among the dead, expecting a second attack, fell asleep. Abu Talhah, who was there, described his sword falling from his hand as drowsiness overcame him. The same mercy came at Badr: 'when He caused drowsiness to overcome you, giving you serenity' (Quran 8:11). Sleep in that situation is not ordinary; it is a physical sign of a heart at rest.\n\nBut not everyone slept. 'Others were disturbed by evil thoughts about Allah — the thoughts of pre-Islamic ignorance.' The same night, the same field, two entirely different inner states. Their bodies revealed what their words concealed.\n\nTheir thought is quoted: 'If we had any say in the matter, none of us would have come to die here.' It is second-guessing dressed as analysis — if the decision had gone my way, none of this would have happened.\n\nThe answer given is blunt: 'Even if you were to remain in your homes, those among you who were destined to be killed would have met the same fate.' The appointed time is not moved by staying home, as 3:145 already said.\n\nThe verse ends by naming the purpose: 'Allah tests what is within you and purifies what is in your hearts.' The night after the defeat did more sorting than the battle.",
  "155": "'Indeed, those ˹believers˺ who fled on the day when the two armies met were made to slip by Satan because of their misdeeds. But Allah has pardoned them. Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Forbearing.' (Quran 3:155). The verdict on those who ran, and it is gentler than they could have expected.\n\n'Made to slip by Satan' — innama istazallahumu al-shaytan. The verb is from 'zalla', to slip, the way a foot slips on wet ground. It is not the language of a decision to betray. It describes a loss of footing.\n\nBut the cause is named honestly: 'because of their misdeeds' — bi ba'di ma kasabu, because of some of what they had earned. Al-Qurtubi, commenting on this verse, notes what the early scholars drew from this: earlier sins weaken a person at the moment of testing. Satan finds a grip where one has already been made. The steadiness a believer needs on a hard day is built quietly on ordinary days.\n\nThen: 'But Allah has pardoned them' — wa laqad 'afa Allahu 'anhum. This is the second explicit pardon in the passage, after 3:152. Men who abandoned the Prophet on the battlefield are forgiven, and the Quran says so twice.\n\nTwo names close the verse. 'Ghafur' — All-Forgiving. And 'Halim' — Most Forbearing, the One who does not rush to punish, who gives time. That second name is the one that explains why we are all still here.\n\nFor anyone carrying a serious failure, this verse is the measure of Allah's mercy. If fleeing from the Prophet's own side in battle was forgiven, the door is wider than we assume.",
  "156": "'O believers! Do not be like the unfaithful who say about their brothers who travel throughout the land or engage in battle, \"If they had stayed with us, they would not have died or been killed.\" Allah makes such thinking a cause of agony in their hearts. It is Allah who gives life and causes death. And Allah is All-Seeing of what you do.' (Quran 3:156). A warning against a way of thinking, not merely a way of speaking.\n\nThe sentence quoted is the classic regret: if only they had stayed. It sounds like sympathy and functions as an accusation — the dead are blamed for their own deaths, and by extension the decision to go is blamed.\n\nNotice the two situations named: travelling for trade and going out to fight. Ordinary risk and religious risk are treated together, because the logic is the same in both. Nobody dies from travelling. People die when their term arrives.\n\n'Allah makes such thinking a cause of agony in their hearts' — li yaj'ala Allahu dhalika hasratan fi qulubihim. Hasrah is a specific kind of grief: regret over what cannot be changed, the ache of the might-have-been. And the verse says this thinking is the cause of that agony, not a response to it. People who believe outcomes turn on their own decisions never stop torturing themselves with alternate versions.\n\nThe cure is the sentence that follows: 'It is Allah who gives life and causes death.' Not travel, not war, not staying home.\n\nThis is one of the most practical mercies in the Quran. Belief in the decree does not remove grief, but it removes the endless replaying of choices, which is a separate and heavier burden.",
  "157": "'Should you be martyred or die in the cause of Allah, then His forgiveness and mercy are far better than whatever ˹wealth˺ those ˹who stay behind˺ accumulate.' (Quran 3:157). A comparison of returns.\n\nTwo ways of dying are named — 'martyred or die' — because a person who sets out in Allah's cause and dies of illness on the road is included, not only the one killed in battle. The Quran says this explicitly: 'those who emigrate in the cause of Allah and then are martyred or die, Allah will indeed grant them a good provision' (Quran 22:58).\n\nWhat is being compared is not death against life. It is what each side ends up holding. Those who went out receive 'forgiveness from Allah and mercy'. Those who stayed back accumulate — 'yajma'un', gather and pile up. The verb suggests hoarding.\n\n'Far better' — khayrun mimma yajma'un. This is the argument the whole passage has been making since 3:145: the same event yields different outcomes depending on what a person was reaching for.\n\nThere is a deliberate reversal of appearances here. The men who stayed home look like the sensible ones: alive, safe, still adding to their property. The men in the graves at Uhud look like the losers. The verse inverts the accounting, and the inversion is the point.\n\nThe application is not restricted to battle. Anyone who chooses the harder, more costly path for Allah's sake while others take the profitable one is inside this comparison. What you gave up is visible; what you received is not, and this verse names it.",
  "158": "'Whether you die or are martyred—all of you will be gathered before Allah.' (Quran 3:158). The shortest verse in this passage, and it closes the argument.\n\nThe two possibilities from 3:157 are repeated, and then flattened into one destination. It does not matter which way you go; the gathering is the same.\n\n'La ilallahi tuhsharun' — to Allah you will be gathered. The verb 'hashara' means to drive people together into one place from every direction, the word used for the great assembly of the Day of Judgment.\n\nRead this beside the anxious calculations of 3:154 and 3:156 — the men working out how deaths could have been avoided — and its purpose is clear. All that reasoning assumes death is the outcome to be managed. This verse says death is not an outcome at all. It is a doorway, and everyone goes through it into the same hall.\n\nThat reframing removes the whole basis of the hypocrites' argument. If staying home merely delays the appointment, then the question is no longer how to avoid the gathering but what to bring to it.\n\nFor a believer this is stabilising in a very practical way. Fear of death distorts decisions, usually toward safety and away from responsibility. A person who has genuinely accepted this verse is not reckless — the Prophet, peace be upon him, told his companions not to wish for the enemy — but he is not governed by that fear either.\n\nEveryone in the account of Uhud, the ones who ran and the ones who stayed, met in the same place eventually.",
  "159": "'It is out of Allah's mercy that you ˹O Prophet˺ have been lenient with them. Had you been cruel or hard-hearted, they would have certainly abandoned you. So pardon them, ask Allah's forgiveness for them, and consult with them in ˹conducting˺ matters. Once you make a decision, put your trust in Allah. Surely Allah loves those who trust in Him.' (Quran 3:159). One of the greatest verses on leadership in the Quran, and its timing is extraordinary.\n\nThis comes after Uhud. His orders were disobeyed, his army fled, he was wounded, and his uncle was killed and mutilated. This is the moment Allah chooses to speak about gentleness.\n\n'Had you been cruel or hard-hearted, they would have certainly abandoned you' — la infaddu min hawlik, they would have scattered from around you. Allah names the leader's gentleness as the reason the community held together, and calls it a mercy from Him.\n\nThen three instructions, each harder than the last. 'Pardon them' — release the offence. 'Ask Allah's forgiveness for them' — go further, and pray for the people who failed you. 'And consult with them in matters' — shawirhum fi al-amr. Consult the same men whose advice led to marching out, and whose discipline broke on the hill.\n\nThat third command is astonishing. The obvious lesson of Uhud was that consultation had failed. Allah's instruction is to keep consulting. It is a permanent principle for the ummah: 'who conduct their affairs by mutual consultation' (Quran 42:38).\n\n'Once you make a decision, put your trust in Allah' completes the method: consult widely, decide firmly, then rely on Allah.",
  "160": "'If Allah helps you, none can defeat you. But if He denies you help, then who else can help you? So in Allah let the believers put their trust.' (Quran 3:160). The principle underneath everything the surah has said about Badr and Uhud.\n\nTwo conditions, and between them there is nothing. If Allah helps, no combination of forces can overcome you. If He withholds help, no ally, weapon or plan can supply the difference. There is no third source of support.\n\n'Then who else can help you?' — fa man dha alladhi yansurukum min ba'dih. The question is left open because there is no answer.\n\nThis connects to the Uhud analysis. The believers had more men and better equipment at Uhud than at Badr, and lost. The variable was never the equipment.\n\nIbn 'Abbas reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, said to him, \"O boy! I will teach you a statement: Be mindful of Allah and He will protect you... Know that if the entire creation were to gather together to do something to benefit you- you would never get any benefit except that Allah had written for you. And if they were to gather to do something to harm you- you would never be harmed except that Allah had written for you\" (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 2516, graded hasan).\n\n'So in Allah let the believers put their trust' — tawakkul. This is not passivity. 'Umar reported that the Prophet said, \"If you were to rely upon Allah with the required reliance, then He would provide for you just as a bird is provided for, it goes out in the morning empty, and returns full\" (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 2344, graded hasan). The bird flies out. It simply does not carry the outcome.",
  "161": "'It is not appropriate for a prophet to illegally withhold spoils of war. And whoever does so, it will be held against them on the Day of Judgment. Then every soul will be paid in full for what it has done, and none will be wronged.' (Quran 3:161). A defence of the Prophet's integrity, and a warning to everyone else.\n\nThe background, recorded by Ibn Kathir commenting on this verse, is that something went missing after a battle and a suspicion was voiced. The verse settles it: a prophet does not do this.\n\n'Ghulul' is the specific word: taking secretly from the common spoils before they are distributed. It is theft from a shared fund, which is why it is treated so severely — the victim is everyone, so nobody notices, and each person's loss is small enough to overlook.\n\n'It will be held against them on the Day of Judgment' — the Arabic says he will come on that Day carrying what he took. The image is of a man arriving publicly with the stolen item on his shoulders.\n\nThen the principle: 'every soul will be paid in full for what it has done, and none will be wronged.' The same words used in 3:25.\n\nThe modern equivalents are exact. Public funds, charity money, organisational resources, workplace property — anything held in common invites the same reasoning: it belongs to no one in particular, and a small amount will not be missed. This verse says the accounting is individual and complete.\n\nAnd there is a lesson in why it is stated about a prophet first. Leaders are held to the standard before anyone else, and the ones handling other people's money are the most exposed.",
  "162": "'Are those who seek Allah's pleasure like those who deserve Allah's wrath? Hell is their home. What an evil destination!' (Quran 3:162). A question with an obvious answer, asked to make a point about appearances.\n\nAt Uhud, both types were physically present. Men who marched out and men who turned back; men who held the hill and men who came down; men who fought and men who sat and later explained why they were right to. From a distance they were one community.\n\nThe verse separates them by what they were pursuing. 'Those who seek Allah's pleasure' — man ittaba'a ridwan Allah, one who follows after Allah's approval. Ridwan was named in 3:15 as the highest thing promised in Paradise, ranked above the gardens themselves.\n\n'Those who deserve Allah's wrath' — man ba'a bi sakhatin mina Allah. The Arabic verb 'ba'a' means to return home carrying something. They went out and came back bearing anger from Allah. It is the language of a transaction: they went to the battle for gain and this is what they brought home.\n\nThe comparison is not between two levels of good. It is between two directions.\n\n'Hell is their home. What an evil destination' — the word 'ma'wa' means a place of shelter and return, which makes the sentence bitterly ironic. The place they end up resting is the Fire.\n\nThe practical question the verse leaves is simple and personal. In any given action, what is actually being sought? The same deed, done by two people standing side by side, can belong to either category.",
  "163": "'They ˹each˺ have varying degrees in the sight of Allah. And Allah is All-Seeing of what they do.' (Quran 3:163). A refinement of the sharp division drawn in 3:162.\n\n'Darajat' — degrees, ranks, levels. The word is used for steps on a ladder. Having separated two groups, the Quran now says that within them there are gradations. Not everyone who sought Allah's pleasure is at the same level, and not everyone in the other category is either.\n\nThat matters for how Muslims judge each other, which is to say: they should not. Two men prayed the same prayer and one of them was somewhere else entirely in his heart. Two men gave the same amount and one gave from what he loved (Quran 3:92). The external act is identical; the rank is not.\n\n'And Allah is All-Seeing of what they do' — basirun bi ma ya'malun. He sees the act and the weight behind it. Ranking requires that kind of sight, which is why it belongs to Him alone.\n\nRead alongside 3:162, the two verses give a balanced picture. There is a real, absolute division between those seeking Allah's pleasure and those earning His anger; nobody is on both sides. And within each side there is a fine gradation invisible to observers.\n\nThe encouraging half of this is that no effort is levelled down. A person who does a small thing with great sincerity is not filed alongside someone who did the same thing carelessly. The degrees are precise, and they are recorded by the only One in a position to measure them.",
  "164": "'Indeed, Allah has done the believers a ˹great˺ favour by raising a messenger from among them—reciting to them His revelations, purifying them, and teaching them the Book and wisdom. For indeed they had previously been clearly astray.' (Quran 3:164). After the hardest passage in the surah, a reminder of the greatest blessing.\n\n'A favour' — manna Allahu 'ala al-mu'minin. The verb suggests conferring something enormous on someone who could not have obtained it themselves.\n\n'From among them' — min anfusihim, from their own selves. Not an angel, not a foreigner. A man of their own community, whose family they knew, whose character they had watched for forty years before he claimed anything. That familiarity was itself part of the gift: 'There certainly has come to you a messenger from among yourselves. He is concerned by your suffering, anxious for your well-being' (Quran 9:128).\n\nThree functions are listed, and the order repays attention. 'Reciting to them His revelations' — delivering the text. 'Purifying them' — tazkiyah, cleaning the heart of arrogance, envy and greed. 'Teaching them the Book and wisdom' — explaining what the text means and how it applies.\n\nPurification is placed in the middle, between delivery and instruction. Knowledge poured into an unpurified heart produces the very diseases this surah has been describing: envy over Allah's bounty (3:73), argument for the sake of winning (3:19), and concealing truth for gain (3:71).\n\nThis was the answer to Ibrahim's prayer at the Ka'bah (Quran 2:129), and the same three functions are listed in 'purifying you, teaching you the Book and wisdom' (Quran 2:151).",
  "165": "'Why is it when you suffered casualties ˹at Uhud˺—although you had made your enemy suffer twice as much ˹at Badr˺—you protested, \"How could this be?\"? Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"It is because of your disobedience.\" Surely Allah is Most Capable of everything.' (Quran 3:165). The question the community was asking, answered directly.\n\n'How could this be?' — anna hadha. The full sense is: where did this come from? We are the believers, we were promised help, and we lost. It is an honest question, and it is the one every believing community asks after a setback.\n\nBefore answering, the Quran corrects the arithmetic: 'you had made your enemy suffer twice as much'. At Badr, Quraysh lost around seventy killed and seventy captured; at Uhud the Muslims lost around seventy. The ledger was still in their favour. Grief distorts accounting, and the verse restores it.\n\nThen the answer, and it is one sentence: 'It is because of your disobedience' — huwa min 'indi anfusikum, it is from your own selves. Not from Allah abandoning them. Not from the enemy's superiority. From themselves.\n\nThat is a hard answer and a hopeful one. Hard, because it removes every external explanation. Hopeful, because a cause located in yourself is a cause you can address.\n\n'Surely Allah is Most Capable of everything' closes the verse. He could have given victory despite the disobedience; His power was never the limiting factor. He chose to let the consequence stand, because a community that never experiences the results of its own decisions never learns anything.\n\nThis verse is the one Muslims should reach for first when analysing their condition, and it is usually the last one they consider.",
  "166": "'So what you suffered on the day the two armies met was by Allah's Will, so that He might distinguish the ˹true˺ believers' (Quran 3:166). Having said in 3:165 that the defeat came from their own conduct, the Quran now says it came by Allah's permission. Both are true, and holding them together is the point.\n\n'By Allah's Will' — fa bi idhni Allah. Nothing happened outside His permission. The archers made a free choice, and the consequence of that choice unfolded within a decree that allowed it.\n\nThis is the Quran's consistent position on responsibility and destiny. Human beings genuinely choose, and nothing occurs outside Allah's will. The verse places both statements one after the other without any sense of contradiction, and a believer is not required to resolve the tension philosophically — only to act as though his choices matter, which they do, and to trust that nothing has escaped Allah's control, which it has not.\n\n'So that He might distinguish the true believers' — wa li ya'lama al-mu'minin. The same phrase as in 3:140 and 3:142: that it be known, made actual, brought out where it can be seen.\n\nThe word for the day is 'the day the two armies met' — yawma iltaqa al-jam'an. The Quran keeps using this neutral description rather than 'the day of your defeat'. Even in the middle of the criticism, the framing is not humiliating.\n\nWhat follows in the next verse is the other half of the distinguishing: the believers were shown, and so were the hypocrites, whose words that day are about to be quoted back to them.",
  "167": "'and expose the hypocrites. When it was said to them, \"Come fight in the cause of Allah or ˹at least˺ defend yourselves,\" they replied, \"If we had known there was fighting, we would have definitely gone with you.\" They were closer to disbelief than to belief on that day—for saying with their mouths what was not in their hearts. Allah is All-Knowing of what they hide.' (Quran 3:167). The second purpose of Uhud, completing 3:166.\n\nThe hypocrites here are the roughly three hundred who withdrew with 'Abdullah ibn Ubayy on the road to the battle. They were offered two options, and the second removes every excuse: fight for Allah, or at least defend your own city. Even a man with no religious motive should defend his home.\n\nTheir answer is transparent nonsense: 'If we had known there was fighting, we would have definitely gone with you.' An army of Quraysh was camped outside Madinah. Everyone knew.\n\n'They were closer to disbelief than to belief on that day' — this is the Quran's careful phrasing. It does not declare them disbelievers outright; it describes their position on that specific day. The measure given is the gap between mouth and heart.\n\nThe Quran describes them elsewhere: 'Surely the hypocrites seek to deceive Allah, but He outwits them' (Quran 4:142), and their excuses at another campaign: 'Do not march forth in the heat.' Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"The Fire of Hell is far hotter!\"' (Quran 9:81).\n\nThe self-examination this invites is uncomfortable. Producing a reasonable-sounding excuse for avoiding a duty is a universal skill. The verse measures not the quality of the excuse but the distance between what we say and what we know.",
  "168": "'Those who sat at home, saying about their brothers, \"Had they listened to us, they would not have been killed.\" Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Try not to die if what you say is true!\"' (Quran 3:168). The hypocrites' commentary on the dead, and the reply is devastating.\n\n'Those who sat at home' — alladhina qa'adu. They stayed back, and afterwards they had opinions about the men who did not.\n\nTheir statement is the same one condemned in 3:156, but now with an added edge: 'Had they listened to us.' The advice they gave was ignored, seventy men died, and they are collecting on being right. There is no grief in the sentence, only vindication.\n\nThe reply is one of the sharpest in the Quran: 'Try not to die if what you say is true!' — fadra'u 'an anfusikumu al-mawta in kuntum sadiqin. If your strategy is what keeps people alive, then use it on yourselves. Stay home permanently and see whether death respects the arrangement.\n\nThe logic is exact. Their claim assumes that human decisions determine the moment of death. The reply simply asks them to test the theory. Nobody can, because 'No soul can ever die without Allah's Will at the destined time' (Quran 3:145).\n\nThere is a lesson here about a habit that outlives its original setting. Analysing other people's disasters from safety, and concluding that our own choices would have produced a better outcome, is a way of making ourselves feel secure. The Quran's answer is to point out that the security is imaginary. The same appointment is coming for the person doing the analysing.",
  "169": "'Never think of those martyred in the cause of Allah as dead. In fact, they are alive with their Lord, well provided for—' (Quran 3:169). After all the talk about who died and who avoided death, the Quran corrects the category itself.\n\n'Never think' — wa la tahsabanna. It is a prohibition on a thought, not merely on a statement. The command addresses how the survivors are to understand what happened to their fathers, brothers and sons.\n\n'They are alive with their Lord' — bal ahya'un 'inda rabbihim. Not remembered fondly, not living on in their legacy. Alive. The same is said in Surah al-Baqarah: 'Never say that those martyred in the cause of Allah are dead—in fact, they are alive! But you do not perceive it' (Quran 2:154). That last clause is the key: the life is real and our perception does not reach it.\n\n'Well provided for' — yurzaqun. They are receiving provision, in the present tense.\n\nMasruq said: We asked 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud about this verse. He said that they had asked the Prophet, peace be upon him, about it, and he said, \"The souls of the martyrs live in the bodies of green birds who have their nests in chandeliers hung from the throne of the Almighty. They eat the fruits of Paradise from wherever they like and then nestle in these chandeliers\" (Sahih Muslim 1887).\n\nFor the families of the seventy men buried at Uhud, this verse changed everything. They had been grieving people who, it turned out, were not there to be grieved.",
  "170": "'rejoicing in Allah's bounties and being delighted for those yet to join them. There will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.' (Quran 3:170). The state of the martyrs described further, and the second half is unexpected.\n\n'Rejoicing in Allah's bounties' — farihina bi ma atahumu Allah. They are not merely comfortable; they are glad.\n\nThen: 'being delighted for those yet to join them' — wa yastabshiruna bi alladhina lam yalhaqu bihim min khalfihim. They are receiving good news about the believers still alive on earth, and they are happy on their behalf.\n\nThink about what that means. The men killed at Uhud are aware, in some manner, of those they left behind, and their reaction is joy rather than pity. That is the reversal the whole passage has been building. The living were pitying the dead; the dead are rejoicing over the living.\n\nThe same hadith reported by Masruq from Ibn Mas'ud continues: when their Lord asked them three times whether they desired anything, they finally said, \"O Lord, we wish that Thou mayest return our souls to our bodies so that we may be slain in Thy way once again\" (Sahih Muslim 1887). Having seen what they received, the only thing they wanted was to do it again.\n\n'There will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve' — la khawfun 'alayhim wa la hum yahzanun. This refrain runs through the Quran (Quran 2:112, Quran 41:30). Fear concerns the future and grief concerns the past; both are cancelled, which is the definition of peace.",
  "171": "'They are joyful for receiving Allah's grace and bounty, and that Allah does not deny the reward of the believers.' (Quran 3:171). The third verse in a row describing the martyrs' joy, and it names what pleases them most.\n\n'Grace and bounty' — ni'matin mina Allahi wa fadl. Two words are used. A ni'mah is a blessing, and fadl is what is given beyond what is deserved. They received both: what was promised, and then extra.\n\n'And that Allah does not deny the reward of the believers' — wa anna Allaha la yudi'u ajra al-mu'minin. The verb 'ada'a' means to let something be lost or wasted. Their joy includes a discovery: nothing was lost.\n\nThis matters because of what these men had given up. They left families, property and futures, and from the outside it looked like everything was wasted for a battle that was lost. The verse says the account was settled in full, and it uses the same principle stated in 3:115 about the sincere among the People of the Book: 'They will never be denied the reward for any good they have done.'\n\nNotice too that they rejoice in a general truth, not only in their own reward: that Allah does not waste the reward of the believers, all of them. Their joy includes the good news for everyone still working.\n\nFor the reader, that is the transferable part. Whatever you have given for Allah's sake — time, money, opportunities, comfort — has not evaporated. The martyrs of Uhud are described as delighted specifically by the discovery that this is true.",
  "172": "'˹As for˺ those who responded to the call of Allah and His Messenger after their injury, those of them who did good and were mindful ˹of Allah˺ will have a great reward.' (Quran 3:172). A specific episode, and it happened the day after Uhud.\n\nQuraysh had withdrawn, then began to reconsider and turn back. The Prophet, peace be upon him, called for the army to march out again in pursuit, and he restricted it to those who had fought the previous day. Wounded, exhausted men who had buried their dead that morning got up and went. They reached a place called Hamra' al-Asad, and Quraysh, hearing of it, continued home.\n\n'After their injury' — min ba'di ma asabahumu al-qarh. The word 'qarh' means wounds. The Quran specifies their condition, because that is what makes the response remarkable.\n\nTwo qualities are named: 'those of them who did good and were mindful of Allah' — ahsanu minhum wattaqaw. Ihsan and taqwa, the same pairing rewarded throughout this surah.\n\nThe strategic effect was real. A defeated army that immediately marches out again does not look defeated, and Quraysh drew the obvious conclusion.\n\nThe spiritual lesson is larger. Uhud ended in disobedience and flight; the very next day the same men answered a call that cost them more than the first one. That is what repentance looks like in practice. Not words, but the next decision.\n\nAnyone who has failed badly at something can take the pattern directly: the response to a bad day is the next day.",
  "173": "'Those who were warned, \"Your enemies have mobilized their forces against you, so fear them,\" the warning only made them grow stronger in faith and they replied, \"Allah ˹alone˺ is sufficient ˹as an aid˺ for us and ˹He˺ is the best Protector.\"' (Quran 3:173). The same expedition, and now the psychological attack on it.\n\nPeople came to the Muslim camp with news designed to frighten: Quraysh has regrouped, they are coming in force, you cannot survive this. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records that these reports reached them at Hamra' al-Asad.\n\nThe response is the opposite of what was intended: 'the warning only made them grow stronger in faith' — fazadahum imanan. The attempt to frighten them increased them.\n\nThen the words they said, which have become one of the most repeated supplications in Islam: 'Hasbuna Allahu wa ni'ma al-wakil' — Allah is sufficient for us, and He is the best Protector. 'Hasb' means what suffices, what is enough. 'Wakil' means the one to whom an affair is entrusted, the trustee who handles it on your behalf.\n\nIbn 'Abbas reported that this was said by Ibrahim when he was thrown into the fire, and by Muhammad, peace be upon them both, when he was told what is quoted in this verse (Sahih al-Bukhari 4563).\n\nThe Quran gives the same assurance: 'whoever puts their trust in Allah, then He ˹alone˺ is sufficient for them' (Quran 65:3).\n\nWhat makes this verse so useful is the situation. These were not people at ease. They were wounded, outnumbered and being told the truth about the danger.",
  "174": "'So they returned with Allah's favours and grace, suffering no harm. For they sought to please Allah. And surely Allah is ˹the˺ Lord of infinite bounty.' (Quran 3:174). The outcome of the march to Hamra' al-Asad.\n\nThree things came back with them. 'Allah's favours' — ni'mah. 'And grace' — fadl, which the commentators, including al-Tabari on this verse, connect to trade profit made on that journey, since a caravan was present. And 'suffering no harm' — lam yamsashum su'. No battle occurred at all.\n\nRead that against what they set out expecting. They marched out wounded, anticipating a second engagement against a stronger force, and came home safe, rewarded and better off than when they left.\n\n'For they sought to please Allah' — wattaba'u ridwan Allah. This is the reason given, and it is the same phrase from 3:162 about those who follow after Allah's approval. It is contrasted deliberately with the archers at Uhud who came down for the spoils: those who went after the world got the world and lost the day; these went after Allah's pleasure and were given both.\n\n'Allah is the Lord of infinite bounty' — dhu fadlin 'azim. The surah has used this closing before (Quran 3:74).\n\nThe lesson is not that seeking Allah's pleasure guarantees a comfortable outcome; the seventy men buried the day before had sought the same thing. It is that the return is never short. Sometimes it is paid here, sometimes there, and 3:169-171 has just described what the payment looks like for those who receive it there.",
  "175": "'That ˹warning˺ was only ˹from˺ Satan, trying to prompt you to fear his followers. So do not fear them; fear Me if you are ˹true˺ believers.' (Quran 3:175). The frightening reports of 3:173 are traced to their source.\n\n'That was only Satan' — innama dhalikumu al-shaytan. The people who carried the news were human, but the strategy behind it is named. Fear is his main instrument, because it works without needing an argument.\n\n'Trying to prompt you to fear his followers' — yukhawwifu awliya'ah. The construction can be read as making you fear his allies, or making his allies seem fearsome. Either way, the mechanism is inflation: the enemy is made to look larger than it is.\n\nNotice how weak his position actually is. He cannot compel anyone; he can only suggest. The Quran calls his schemes weak: 'Indeed, Satan's schemes are ever weak' (Quran 4:76). And at Badr he abandoned his own side once the fighting began: 'I have absolutely nothing to do with you' (Quran 8:48).\n\n'So do not fear them; fear Me if you are true believers.' The Quran does not tell the believers to stop feeling fear altogether. It redirects it. Fear is a real and useful faculty; the question is where it is aimed. A person who fears Allah properly has very little capacity left for fearing anyone else, and that is what makes him free.\n\nThe same redirection is commanded elsewhere: 'So do not fear the people; fear Me!' (Quran 5:44).",
  "176": "'˹O Prophet!˺ Do not grieve for those who race to disbelieve—surely they will not harm Allah in the least. It is Allah's Will to disallow them a share in the Hereafter, and they will suffer a tremendous punishment.' (Quran 3:176). A word of comfort to the Prophet about a grief that was genuinely his.\n\n'Do not grieve' — la yahzunka. He grieved over people who rejected the message. This is stated repeatedly in the Quran, and it tells us something about him: the opposition did not make him bitter, it made him sad. He wanted them saved.\n\n'Those who race to disbelieve' — yusari'una fi al-kufr. The verb is the same one used in 3:133 for hastening toward forgiveness. The same energy, pointed the other way. These are not people drifting; they are running.\n\n'They will not harm Allah in the least' — lan yadurru Allaha shay'a. This is repeated in the next verse and again in 3:144. Allah is not diminished by rejection. Nothing is at stake for Him.\n\nThat is the actual comfort being offered. The Prophet's sadness carried an unspoken assumption — that something was being lost — and the verse locates the loss precisely: it is entirely theirs. 'It is Allah's Will to disallow them a share in the Hereafter' — alla yaj'ala lahum hazzan fi al-akhirah. A hazz is a portion, a share of an inheritance.\n\nThere is guidance here for anyone who carries grief over a family member or friend who has turned away. Care, invite, pray — and then remember that the outcome is not your burden to carry, and that the loss, if it comes, is not yours.",
  "177": "'Those who trade belief for disbelief will never harm Allah in the least, and they will suffer a painful punishment.' (Quran 3:177). The same point as 3:176, restated as a transaction.\n\n'Trade' — ishtaraw, they purchased. The verb is used for buying, and it appears throughout this surah for bad bargains (Quran 3:77, Quran 3:187). The Quran consistently frames choices as commerce: something given up, something acquired.\n\nWhat is given here is faith; what is bought is disbelief. Notice the direction. They already had belief and exchanged it for the alternative. This is not describing someone who never came to faith.\n\n'Will never harm Allah in the least' — the repetition from the previous verse. It is worth dwelling on, because a great deal of religious anger comes from the unspoken feeling that Allah has been injured and must be defended. He has not been, and He does not need to be.\n\nThat realisation changes how a believer responds to apostasy or public mockery of the faith. If nothing has been taken from Allah, then the reaction of outrage is misplaced. What remains is concern for the person and a duty to state the truth clearly, which is what the Prophet was instructed to do throughout this surah (Quran 3:20).\n\n'They will suffer a painful punishment' names where the damage lands. Every verse in this cluster does the same thing: it relocates the harm from Allah, who cannot be harmed, and from the believers, whose faith is intact, onto the person who made the trade.",
  "178": "'Those who disbelieve should not think that living longer is good for them. They are only given more time to increase in sin, and they will suffer a humiliating punishment.' (Quran 3:178). A hard verse about a comfortable assumption.\n\nThe assumption is that a long, undisturbed life is evidence of divine approval. The Makkan leaders were prospering while the Muslims were burying their dead; the surah has already addressed the same reasoning about wealth and children (Quran 3:10, Quran 3:116).\n\n'We only give them more time' — innama numli lahum. The verb 'amla' means to extend a rope, to give slack. The image is of an animal given more line to wander on, which feels like freedom and is not.\n\n'To increase in sin' — li yazdadu ithma. The extra years are not neutral. They are being used, and what is accumulating is not in their favour.\n\nThis is the counterpart of Allah's name 'Halim', the Forbearing, mentioned in 3:155. He does not punish immediately. For a person turning back, that delay is the mercy that makes repentance possible. For a person running the other way, the same delay simply extends the account.\n\nSo the delay itself is not the verdict; what is done with it is. That is the honest reading, and it prevents two errors: envying people who seem to get away with everything, and assuming our own untroubled circumstances are a certificate of good standing.\n\n'A humiliating punishment' — 'adhabun muhin. Humiliation is named specifically, because these were people who valued their standing above almost everything.",
  "179": "'Allah would not leave the believers in the condition you were in, until He distinguished the good from the evil ˹among you˺. Nor would Allah ˹directly˺ reveal to you the unseen, but He chooses whoever He wills as a messenger. So believe in Allah and His messengers. And if you are faithful and mindful ˹of Allah˺, you will receive a great reward.' (Quran 3:179). The theological summary of the entire Uhud passage.\n\n'The condition you were in' — a community in which sincere believers and hypocrites were mixed together indistinguishably. Allah would not leave it that way. 'Until He distinguished the good from the evil' — hatta yamiza al-khabitha mina al-tayyib, until He separates the foul from the pure. It is the language of sorting grain.\n\nThat is what Uhud accomplished. A comfortable community cannot be sorted, because everyone behaves the same when nothing is at stake. Pressure did in one day what years of peace could not.\n\nThen the second half answers an obvious question: why not simply tell us who the hypocrites are? 'Nor would Allah directly reveal to you the unseen' — the knowledge of hearts is not distributed to people. Allah discloses it to whom He wills among His messengers, and even then selectively.\n\nThis is a mercy. A community in which everyone's inner state was public knowledge would be unliveable, and it would remove the whole point of the test.\n\n'So believe in Allah and His messengers' — the practical conclusion. Since you cannot see hearts, your job is not detection. Your job is your own faith and taqwa, and the reward is attached to those.",
  "180": "'And do not let those who ˹greedily˺ withhold Allah's bounties think it is good for them—in fact, it is bad for them! They will be leashed ˹by their necks˺ on the Day of Judgment with whatever ˹wealth˺ they used to withhold. And Allah is the ˹sole˺ inheritor of the heavens and the earth. And Allah is All-Aware of what you do.' (Quran 3:180). A verse about hoarding, and the image in it is unforgettable.\n\n'Withhold Allah's bounties' — yabkhaluna bi ma atahumu Allahu min fadlih. Two things are established by the wording. What they hold was given to them, and it was given as fadl, bounty beyond entitlement. Withholding it is refusing to pass on what was passed to you.\n\n'They think it is good for them' — the illusion that keeps a miser miserly. Saving feels like security.\n\n'They will be leashed by their necks with whatever they used to withhold' — sayutawwaquna ma bakhilu bihi. The wealth becomes a collar.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said, \"Whoever is made wealthy by Allah and does not pay the Zakat of his wealth, then on the Day of Resurrection his wealth will be made like a baldheaded poisonous male snake with two black spots over the eyes. The snake will encircle his neck and bite his cheeks and say, 'I am your wealth, I am your treasure.'\" Then he recited this verse (Sahih al-Bukhari 1403).\n\n'Allah is the sole inheritor of the heavens and the earth' settles the logic. Everything returns to Him regardless. The only question is whether it leaves your hands as charity or is taken from them at death.",
  "181": "'Indeed, Allah has heard those ˹among the Jews˺ who said, \"Allah is poor; we are rich!\" We have certainly recorded their slurs and their killing of prophets unjustly. Then We will say, \"Taste the torment of burning!' (Quran 3:181). A statement made in mockery, and the response to it.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records the occasion: when the verse 'Who will lend to Allah a good loan?' (Quran 2:245) was recited, some responded by sneering that Allah must be poor if He is asking for loans while they are rich. The same insult is recorded in Surah al-Ma'idah: '˹Some among˺ the Jews said, \"Allah is tight-fisted\"' (Quran 5:64).\n\n'Allah has heard' — laqad sami'a Allah. Not learned of it later. Heard it as it was said.\n\n'We have certainly recorded' — sanaktubu ma qalu. The remark was not merely noted; it was written into the record. Casual speech is not casual: 'We certainly record whatever he claims' (Quran 19:79).\n\nThen their mockery is placed alongside 'their killing of prophets unjustly'. That pairing seems disproportionate until you see the connection: both come from the same source, a refusal to accept that Allah has any claim on them. One expresses it with a joke, the other with a sword.\n\nThe verse also answers the insult by exposing what it reveals. Allah's invitation to lend was an opportunity offered to them, and they read it as a request for help. A person who cannot recognise generosity when it is extended will experience every command as an imposition.",
  "182": "'This is ˹the reward˺ for what your hands have done. And Allah is never unjust to ˹His˺ creation.' (Quran 3:182). The sentence following the punishment announced in 3:181.\n\n'What your hands have done' — ma qaddamat aydikum, literally what your hands sent ahead. The Quran uses this image often: deeds are dispatched ahead of you, and you arrive to find them waiting.\n\nNotice that it says 'your hands', not 'your tongues', even though the offence in 3:181 was something they said. The Arabic idiom covers all deeds, and the point is that speech is an action. Words are done, not merely uttered.\n\n'And Allah is never unjust to His creation' — wa anna Allaha laysa bi zallamin li al-'abid. The Arabic uses an intensive form: not in the least unjust, never in any degree. The surah has now made this statement three times (Quran 3:108, Quran 3:117, and here), each time immediately after describing a punishment.\n\nThat repetition is deliberate. The Quran anticipates the objection every reader eventually raises, and it answers with a claim about Allah's nature rather than a defence of the individual case.\n\nNote also the word used for people here: 'al-'abid', the servants. Even while announcing punishment, the relationship is named. They are His servants, and He does not wrong His servants.\n\nThe practical form of this belief is trust. Whatever happens to you, and whatever verdict is eventually pronounced on anyone, the one certainty is that it will not be unfair.",
  "183": "'Those ˹are the same people˺ who say, \"Allah has commanded us not to believe in any messenger unless he brings us an offering to be consumed by fire ˹from the sky˺.\" Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Other prophets did in fact come to you before me with clear proofs and ˹even˺ what you demanded—why then did you kill them, if what you say is true?\"' (Quran 3:183). A demand for a specific miracle, and the reply is historical.\n\nThe demand had a basis in their tradition: a genuine offering would be consumed by fire descending from the sky. They presented this as a condition Allah Himself had set.\n\nThe reply does not argue about whether the condition is valid. It asks a simpler question: prophets already met it, and you killed them. So the condition was never the obstacle.\n\nThat is the whole method of this passage. When someone names a requirement for belief, the honest test is whether meeting a similar requirement previously changed anything. Usually it did not, which shows that the requirement was never the real reason.\n\n'Why then did you kill them, if what you say is true?' — the same construction used in 3:168 to the hypocrites: if your claim is genuine, act on it.\n\nThe Quran states the pattern elsewhere: 'Why is it that every time a messenger comes to you ˹Israelites˺ with something you do not like, you become arrogant, rejecting some and killing others?' (Quran 2:87).\n\nThe lesson is one every believer can apply to himself. When we say we would change if only we had more evidence, more time or more certainty, it is worth asking honestly what happened the last time we received exactly that.",
  "184": "'If you are rejected by them, so too were messengers before you who came with clear proofs, divine Books, and enlightening Scriptures.' (Quran 3:184). Consolation for the Prophet, and the method is to widen the frame.\n\n'If you are rejected' — fa in kadhdhabuk, if they call you a liar. That was the accusation, and it landed on a man whose own people had called him al-Amin, the trustworthy, for forty years.\n\nThe comfort offered is not that the rejection will stop. It is that he is in the right company. Every messenger before him faced the same, and they came with everything a reasonable person could ask for: 'clear proofs' — al-bayyinat, evidence; 'divine Books' — al-zubur; and 'enlightening Scriptures' — al-kitab al-munir, the illuminating Book.\n\nThat list matters. It rules out the idea that rejection happens because the case was poorly made. These messengers had proof, scripture and light, and were still rejected. The failure was never in the presentation.\n\nThere is a specific relief in this for anyone doing religious work. The natural assumption after being dismissed is that you explained it badly, chose the wrong moment, or lacked knowledge. Sometimes that is true and worth fixing. But this verse establishes that even a flawless delivery by a prophet of Allah does not guarantee acceptance, because acceptance is not produced by argument alone.\n\nThe surah has already given the Prophet the same freedom twice: 'your duty is only to deliver ˹the message˺' (Quran 3:20). Here it adds the emotional half of that release.",
  "185": "'Every soul will taste death. And you will only receive your full reward on the Day of Judgment. Whoever is spared from the Fire and is admitted into Paradise will ˹indeed˺ triumph, whereas the life of this world is no more than the delusion of enjoyment.' (Quran 3:185). One of the most quoted verses in the Quran, and every clause of it corrects an assumption.\n\n'Every soul will taste death' — kullu nafsin dha'iqatu al-mawt. The verb is 'taste', not suffer or undergo. Tasting is brief and it is a passage to something else. The same statement appears in 'Every soul will taste death. And We test you ˹O humanity˺ with good and evil as a trial' (Quran 21:35).\n\n'You will only receive your full reward on the Day of Judgment' — the accounts are not settled here. That single sentence explains most of what looks unjust in the world. Anyone expecting the ledger to balance before death is reading the wrong page.\n\nThen the definition of success, and it is deliberately minimal: 'Whoever is spared from the Fire and is admitted into Paradise will indeed triumph.' Not whoever accumulates, achieves or is remembered. Just this: kept out of one place, let into another. Everything else people call success is left out of the sentence entirely.\n\n'The delusion of enjoyment' — mata'u al-ghurur. Mata' is temporary goods, as in 3:14; ghurur is deception. The world is not called evil. It is called a short-term provision that misleads people about its own importance.",
  "186": "'You ˹believers˺ will surely be tested in your wealth and yourselves, and you will certainly hear many hurtful words from those who were given the Scripture before you and ˹from˺ the polytheists. But if you are patient and mindful ˹of Allah˺—surely this is a resolve to aspire to.' (Quran 3:186). A promise of difficulty, stated plainly.\n\n'You will surely be tested' — la tublawunna. The construction is emphatic: this will certainly happen. Islam does not offer an untroubled life, and any preaching that suggests otherwise is not drawn from this Book.\n\nThree categories of trial are named. 'In your wealth' — losses, expenses, obligations to give. 'And yourselves' — health, safety, bereavement, effort. And then a third that gets its own clause: 'you will certainly hear many hurtful words'.\n\nThat the Quran singles out insult as a distinct trial is worth noticing. Words wound in a way that financial loss does not, and being mocked for your faith is one of the most corrosive pressures a believer faces, because it works slowly and never quite justifies a response.\n\nThe prescription is the pairing given throughout this surah: 'if you are patient and mindful of Allah' — in tasbiru wa tattaqu, exactly as in 3:120 and 3:125. Sabr absorbs; taqwa keeps you from being dragged into wrong conduct by the provocation.\n\n'This is a resolve to aspire to' — min 'azmi al-umur, among the matters requiring firm determination. The same phrase is used of Luqman's advice to his son (Quran 31:17).\n\nNote also that 3:111 already told the believers this kind of harm is 'a little annoyance'. Real, and survivable.",
  "187": "'˹Remember, O Prophet,˺ when Allah took the covenant of those who were given the Scripture to make it known to people and not hide it, yet they cast it behind their backs and traded it for a fleeting gain. What a miserable profit!' (Quran 3:187). A covenant about knowledge, and how it was broken.\n\nThe obligation was twofold: 'to make it known to people and not hide it' — la tubayyinunnahu li al-nasi wa la taktumunah. Explain it, and do not conceal it. Whoever receives revelation is not its owner but its trustee, and the terms of the trust require passing it on.\n\n'They cast it behind their backs' — fa nabadhuhu wara'a zuhurihim. The image is of throwing something over your shoulder without looking, the way you discard what no longer concerns you.\n\n'Traded it for a fleeting gain' — thamanan qalila, a small price. The surah has used this expression before, in 3:77, about those who sell their covenant with Allah. What was bought was position, income, or the approval of a community that preferred not to hear certain things.\n\n'What a miserable profit' — fa bi'sa ma yashtarun.\n\nThis is not a foreign failing. Any Muslim who knows a ruling and stays quiet because saying it would be awkward, unpopular or costly is inside the terms of this verse. So is anyone who softens what he knows to be true in order to keep an audience.\n\nThe Quran states the consequence elsewhere: 'Those who hide the clear proofs and guidance that We have revealed— after We made it clear for humanity in the Book—will be condemned by Allah' (Quran 2:159).",
  "188": "'Do not let those who rejoice in their misdeeds and love to take credit for what they have not done think they will escape torment. They will suffer a painful punishment.' (Quran 3:188). A verse about a very specific character, and it is uncomfortably recognisable.\n\nTwo traits are named. First, 'those who rejoice in their misdeeds' — yafrahuna bi ma ataw, they are pleased with what they have done. Not merely doing wrong, but being satisfied about it, which removes the one thing that might lead to repentance.\n\nSecond, 'love to take credit for what they have not done' — yuhibbuna an yuhmadu bi ma lam yaf'alu. They want praise for things they never did.\n\nThe original occasion, according to the reports gathered by Ibn Kathir commenting on this verse, involved people who concealed what they knew of scripture, congratulated themselves on how they had handled the question, and expected to be honoured as scholars. It has also been reported as being about hypocrites who stayed back from battle, made excuses, and then wanted to be thanked as though they had gone.\n\nBoth descriptions share one structure: the appearance of virtue detached from the substance of it.\n\nThis is worth applying honestly. Reputation is a currency, and it can be earned falsely — by accepting praise for work someone else did, by letting a mistaken impression of one's piety stand, by taking credit for an outcome one did not produce. The verse says such people believe they are safe, and it is written specifically to remove that belief.",
  "189": "'To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth. And Allah is Most Capable of everything.' (Quran 3:189). A short verse placed as a closing statement to everything that came before.\n\nThe surah has now said something like this three times (Quran 3:109, Quran 3:129, and here), and each time it appears after a passage about human behaviour — plotting, hoarding, concealing, taking false credit. The placement is the argument. All that scheming happens inside a kingdom owned by someone else.\n\n'Al-mulk' means dominion, sovereignty, the right to command and dispose. It is not merely that Allah made these things; He rules them continuously.\n\n'And Allah is Most Capable of everything' — 'ala kulli shay'in qadir. Ownership plus power. An owner without power could be robbed; a power without ownership would be a usurper. Both together mean the arrangement is entirely secure.\n\nFor the people described in the preceding verses, this is a warning: the profits they gathered were taken from a kingdom whose owner has full records and full ability to act.\n\nFor the believers who had just lost a battle and were being insulted for it, this is reassurance: the territory you are standing on, and the territory your opponents occupy, belong to the same Lord.\n\nAnd the verse serves as a bridge. Having named the Owner, the surah turns immediately to the creation He owns, inviting people to look at the heavens and the earth and think.",
  "190": "'Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth and the alternation of the day and night there are signs for people of reason.' (Quran 3:190). One of the great verses of reflection, and it opens the surah's closing passage.\n\nTwo things are offered as evidence, and both are free and permanently available. The creation of the heavens and the earth — the structure of everything. And 'the alternation of the day and night' — ikhtilaf al-layli wa al-nahar, the regular exchange of one for the other, which the surah mentioned earlier as an act of Allah (Quran 3:27).\n\n'Signs' — ayat, the same word used for verses of the Quran. There are two books: the recited one and the observed one, and both are called by the same name.\n\n'For people of reason' — li ulu al-albab. The word 'lubb' means the kernel of something, the part inside the shell. So these are people with a core, who get past the surface of what they look at.\n\nIbn 'Abbas reported: I stayed overnight in the house of my aunt Maymunah. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, talked with his wife for a while and then went to bed. When it was the last third of the night, he got up and looked towards the sky and recited, \"Verily! In the creation of the Heavens and the Earth and in the alteration of night and day, there are indeed signs for men of understanding.\" Then he stood, performed ablution, used the siwak, and prayed eleven rak'at (Sahih al-Bukhari 4569).\n\nHe looked at the sky, recited this verse, and got up to pray. That is what reflection is for.",
  "191": "'˹They are˺ those who remember Allah while standing, sitting, and lying on their sides, and reflect on the creation of the heavens and the earth ˹and pray˺, \"Our Lord! You have not created ˹all of˺ this without purpose. Glory be to You! Protect us from the torment of the Fire.\"' (Quran 3:191). The people of reason are described, and the description covers every posture a body can take.\n\n'Standing, sitting, and lying on their sides' — qiyaman wa qu'udan wa 'ala junubihim. Between them these three cover the whole of waking life. Remembrance is not confined to a prayer mat or a fixed hour; it runs underneath everything.\n\nThen the second activity: 'reflect on the creation of the heavens and the earth' — yatafakkaruna. Remembrance and reflection are named as a pair. Dhikr without thought becomes mechanical; thought without dhikr becomes mere curiosity. Together they produce what follows.\n\nAnd what follows is a conclusion reached by looking: 'You have not created all of this without purpose' — ma khalaqta hadha batila. That is the finding. A universe this ordered is not an accident, and it is not a joke.\n\n'Glory be to You' — subhanak. The instinctive response to the realisation.\n\nThen, immediately, 'Protect us from the torment of the Fire.' That is the unexpected turn. Contemplating the stars leads them straight to asking for safety on the Day of Judgment. The reasoning is direct: if creation has a purpose, then there is accountability, and if there is accountability, the most urgent thing is to be safe from its consequences.\n\nReflection that does not end in prayer has stopped halfway.",
  "192": "'Our Lord! Indeed, those You commit to the Fire will be ˹completely˺ disgraced! And the wrongdoers will have no helpers.' (Quran 3:192). The prayer of the people of reason continues, and it explains why they asked for protection.\n\n'Will be completely disgraced' — faqad akhzaytah. The word 'khizy' means disgrace and public humiliation. What frightens them is not primarily the physical pain. It is the shame.\n\nThat is a mature fear. A person mainly afraid of the Fire's heat is afraid of a sensation. A person afraid of being disgraced before Allah is afraid of a relationship being broken, and of standing exposed before the One whose opinion he actually cares about.\n\n'And the wrongdoers will have no helpers' — wa ma li al-zalimina min ansar. This refrain has run through the entire surah (Quran 3:22, Quran 3:56, Quran 3:91, Quran 3:111). Here it appears one last time, on the lips of the believers themselves, as part of their supplication.\n\nThat is worth noticing. Throughout the surah, Allah told the readers that wrongdoers will find no helper. Now the people of reason repeat it back in prayer. They have absorbed what they were taught and made it part of how they speak to their Lord.\n\nThere is a model here for how to pray. These believers are not reciting a formula. They are reasoning aloud in front of Allah: this is what we saw, this is what we concluded, this is what we are therefore afraid of, and this is what we are asking for.",
  "193": "'Our Lord! We have heard the caller to ˹true˺ belief, ˹proclaiming,˺ \"Believe in your Lord ˹alone˺,\" so we believed. Our Lord! Forgive our sins, absolve us of our misdeeds, and allow us ˹each˺ to die as one of the virtuous.' (Quran 3:193). The prayer continues, and now it presents a claim.\n\n'We have heard the caller... so we believed' — sami'na munadiyan yunadi li al-imani an aminu bi rabbikum fa amanna. The caller is the Prophet, peace be upon him, or the Quran itself. And notice the structure: we heard, and we believed. No delay is mentioned between the two.\n\nThey are not boasting. They are presenting the one thing they have. It is the same posture as the believers in 3:16, whose prayer opened with 'We have believed, so forgive our sins'.\n\nThree requests follow, and they escalate. 'Forgive our sins' — ighfir lana dhunubana, cover the major ones. 'Absolve us of our misdeeds' — kaffir 'anna sayyi'atina, wipe away the lesser ones. The distinction is deliberate: they want the large debts covered and the small accumulated ones erased.\n\n'And allow us to die as one of the virtuous' — wa tawaffana ma'a al-abrar. Not merely to die forgiven, but to die in good company and good standing. The word 'abrar' describes those whose righteousness is habitual.\n\nThis third request is the one people forget to make. Faith today does not guarantee faith at the end, which is why this surah has already taught the prayer 'Do not let our hearts deviate after you have guided us' (Quran 3:8). Here it is again, in its final form: let the last moment be a good one.",
  "194": "'Our Lord! Grant us what You have promised us through Your messengers and do not put us to shame on Judgment Day—for certainly You never fail in Your promise.\"' (Quran 3:194). The final request of the people of reason, and it is built on a striking piece of reasoning.\n\n'Grant us what You have promised us through Your messengers' — they are asking Allah for something He has already promised. That seems unnecessary until you see the humility in it. They do not claim the promise as a debt owed to them. They ask for it as a favour, because a promise from Allah is conditional on the recipient qualifying, and they will not presume that they do.\n\n'Do not put us to shame on Judgment Day' — wa la tukhzina yawma al-qiyamah. Disgrace again, as in 3:192. It is the thing they mention most.\n\n'For certainly You never fail in Your promise' — innaka la tukhlifu al-mi'ad. They end by affirming Allah's reliability, which is the same statement made in the surah's earlier prayer: 'Surely Allah does not break His promise' (Quran 3:9). The surah's opening prayers and its closing prayers say the same thing, which is a deliberate structure.\n\nNotice what the entire supplication of 3:191-194 contains and what it does not. No request for wealth, health, victory, status or relief from enemies — even though this surah has described a defeat, insults, hypocrisy and hostility. Everything they ask for concerns forgiveness, a good death and safety on the Day.\n\nThat is what a person prays for once he has actually looked at the sky and thought about it.",
  "195": "'So their Lord responded to them: \"I will never deny any of you—male or female—the reward of your deeds. Both are equal in reward. Those who migrated or were expelled from their homes, and were persecuted for My sake and fought and ˹some˺ were martyred—I will certainly forgive their sins and admit them into Gardens under which rivers flow, as a reward from Allah. And with Allah is the finest reward!\"' (Quran 3:195). The prayer of 3:191-194 is answered on the spot.\n\n'So their Lord responded to them' — fa istajaba lahum rabbuhum. The verses said 'Our Lord' four times, and here the answer comes immediately, in the same passage. The Quran shows the response arriving rather than merely promising that it will.\n\n'Male or female—both are equal in reward'. 'Amr ibn Dinar reported from Umm Salamah that she said: \"O Messenger of Allah! I have not heard Allah mentioning anything about women and emigration.\" So Allah revealed: \"Never will I allow to be lost the work of any of you, be he male or female. You are members one of another\" (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 3023, graded hasan by Darussalam).\n\n'Ba'dukum min ba'd' — you are from one another. Men and women are of one origin and one enterprise; the reward system does not have two tiers. The same principle is stated in 'Whoever does good, whether male or female, and is a believer, We will surely bless them with a good life' (Quran 16:97).\n\nThen a list of what these people endured: migration, expulsion, persecution, fighting, death. Every stage of loss the early Muslims went through is named, and each one is entered into the account.\n\n'I will never deny any of you the reward of your deeds' — la udi'u. Nothing is wasted, which is the promise this surah keeps returning to (Quran 3:171).",
  "196": "'Do not be deceived by the prosperity of the disbelievers throughout the land.' (Quran 3:196). A short verse addressing a very ordinary temptation.\n\n'Do not be deceived' — la yaghurrannaka. The same root as 'ghurur' in 3:185, where the world was called 'the delusion of enjoyment'. Being fooled is the risk under discussion.\n\n'The prosperity... throughout the land' — taqallubu alladhina kafaru fi al-bilad. 'Taqallub' means moving about freely, travelling for trade, going from place to place with ease. It is a picture of people with resources, mobility and options.\n\nThe verse is addressed to believers who had just lost seventy men at Uhud and were watching their opponents ride out of Makkah on well-supplied caravans. The contrast was visible and painful, and the question it raises is the oldest one in religion: why do they have it so easy?\n\nThe Quran gives the same warning elsewhere in almost the same words: 'None disputes the signs of Allah except the disbelievers, so do not be deceived by their prosperity throughout the land' (Quran 40:4).\n\nNotice what the verse does not say. It does not deny that they are prospering. It does not claim their success is fake or that it will collapse next week. It grants the fact and challenges the conclusion drawn from it.\n\nThat is the practical point. Comfort is not evidence of approval, and hardship is not evidence of abandonment. Reading either as a verdict is the error being forbidden here.",
  "197": "'It is only a brief enjoyment. Then Hell will be their home—what an evil place to rest!' (Quran 3:197). The completion of the previous verse, and it supplies the missing information.\n\n'A brief enjoyment' — mata'un qalil. Two words. Mata' is provisions for a journey, useful but temporary. Qalil means little. The same phrase is used elsewhere: '˹It is only˺ a brief enjoyment in this world, then to Us is their return' (Quran 10:70).\n\nThe argument here is entirely about duration. It never says that what they have is worthless. It says it is short. And a short thing is only deceptive if you mistake it for a long thing.\n\n'Then Hell will be their home' — thumma ma'wahum jahannam. The word 'ma'wa' means the place you return to and settle in, the opposite of a temporary stop. So the sentence sets a brief stay against a permanent residence.\n\n'What an evil place to rest' — wa bi'sa al-mihad. 'Mihad' is a bed or a cradle, something prepared for you to lie in. The word is bitterly chosen, and it is the same one used in 3:12 of the same people.\n\nThe comparison being asked for is not between poverty now and wealth now. It is between a short comfort and a permanent one. Anyone who genuinely believes there is a Hereafter has already accepted the arithmetic; the difficulty is keeping it in view while watching someone else's life look easier than yours.",
  "198": "'But those who are mindful of their Lord will be in Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there forever—as an accommodation from Allah. And what is with Allah is best for the virtuous.' (Quran 3:198). The other side of the comparison.\n\n'Lakin' — 'but'. The verse is deliberately positioned against the two before it, so the reader compares the two outcomes directly.\n\n'To stay there forever' — khalidina fiha. Set against 'a brief enjoyment' in 3:197. That single contrast is the entire argument of this closing passage.\n\n'As an accommodation from Allah' — nuzulan min 'ind Allah. 'Nuzul' is what a host prepares for a guest on arrival: the welcome meal, the room made ready. Classical commentators including al-Qurtubi note the implication — if this is merely the reception, what follows is greater still.\n\n'And what is with Allah is best for the virtuous' — wa ma 'inda Allahi khayrun li al-abrar. 'Abrar' is the same word used in the prayer of 3:193, where they asked to die among the virtuous. What they asked for is now confirmed as available.\n\nThe Quran repeats this comparison often: 'Whatever pleasure you have been given is no more than a fleeting enjoyment and adornment of this worldly life. But what is with Allah is far better and more lasting' (Quran 28:60).\n\nNote that the condition attached is taqwa, mindfulness of Allah — not achievement, not victory, not visible success. The believers at Uhud had lost the battle. The requirement was never that they win.",
  "199": "'Indeed, there are some among the People of the Book who truly believe in Allah and what has been revealed to you ˹believers˺ and what was revealed to them. They humble themselves before Allah—never trading Allah's revelations for a fleeting gain. Their reward is with their Lord. Surely Allah is swift in reckoning.' (Quran 3:199). After a long and often severe discussion of the People of the Book, the surah closes that subject with fairness.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, reports that it was revealed concerning the Negus of Abyssinia, an-Najashi, who believed in the Prophet and died in his own land. Abu Hurayrah narrated that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, informed the people of the death of an-Najashi on the very day he died, went out to the prayer place, arranged them in rows and said four takbirs, praying the funeral prayer over him (Sahih al-Bukhari 1245).\n\nThat is the setting: a Christian king, never in Madinah, prayed over by the Prophet himself.\n\nThree things are said about such people. They believe in what came to Muhammad and in what came to them — no contradiction, because the source is one. They 'humble themselves before Allah' — khashi'ina lillah, and khushu' is inward submission rather than outward display. And they never trade Allah's revelations 'for a fleeting gain' — the exact charge levelled at others in 3:187, here explicitly withheld from them.\n\nThis is the same balance the surah struck at 3:113: 'They are not all alike'. A community can be criticised for a real failure without every one of its members being included in the charge, and the Quran models that discipline carefully.",
  "200": "'O believers! Patiently endure, persevere, stand on guard, and be mindful of Allah, so you may be successful.' (Quran 3:200). The last verse of the surah, and it gathers everything into four commands.\n\n'Isbiru' — patiently endure. Bear what comes: the loss at Uhud, the insults of 3:186, the death of relatives, the slowness of relief.\n\n'Wa sabiru' — persevere, or out-endure. The Arabic form implies patience against someone else's patience: your opponents are also holding out, and you must hold out longer. Endurance is being described as a contest.\n\n'Wa rabitu' — stand on guard. The word means to be stationed at a frontier, tied to your post, ready. Its root is used of tethering horses at a border. But the Prophet, peace be upon him, extended it: Abu Hurayrah reported that he said, \"Shall I not tell you of that by which Allah wipes away sins and raises ranks?\" They said, \"Yes, O Messenger of Allah.\" He said, \"Performing wudu thoroughly despite difficulty, taking many steps to the mosques, and waiting for the next prayer after a prayer — that is the ribat\" (Sahih Muslim 251a).\n\n'Wa ittaqu Allah' — and be mindful of Allah. The condition attached to every promise in this surah, and the last word of instruction in it.\n\nThen, 'so you may be successful' — la'allakum tuflihun. Not a guarantee of victory, wealth or ease. Falah is the success defined back in 3:185: kept out of the Fire, admitted into the Garden.\n\nThree verses on holding your ground, and one on keeping Allah in view. That is the surah's final answer to a year that began with a defeat."
};
